{"surah": 22, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ ٱتَّقُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْۚ إِنَّ زَلْزَلَةَ ٱلسَّاعَةِ شَىْءٌ عَظِيمٌ ", "translation": "O mankind, fear your Lord. Indeed, the convulsion of the [final] Hour is a terrible thing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱ", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "ittaqū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbakum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "زَلْزَلَةَ", "translit": "َẗَlَzْlَz", "root": "زلزل", "latinTranslit": "zalzalata"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع", "latinTranslit": "l-sāʿati"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "شَىْءٌ", "translit": "ٌءْàَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayon"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَظِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِẓَع", "root": "عظم", "latinTranslit": "ʿaẓīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "People, be mindful of your Lord, for the earthquake of the Last Hour will be a mighty thing", "clearyTranslation": "People, be mindful of your Lord, for the earthquake of the Last Hour will be a mighty thing"}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "يَوْمَ تَرَوْنَهَا تَذْهَلُ كُلُّ مُرْضِعَةٍ عَمَّآ أَرْضَعَتْ وَتَضَعُ كُلُّ ذَاتِ حَمْلٍ حَمْلَهَا وَتَرَى ٱلنَّاسَ سُكَٰرَىٰ وَمَا هُم بِسُكَٰرَىٰ وَلَٰكِنَّ عَذَابَ ٱللَّهِ شَدِيدٌ ", "translation": "On the Day you see it every nursing mother will be distracted from that [child] she was nursing, and every pregnant woman will abort her pregnancy, and you will see the people [appearing] intoxicated while they are not intoxicated; but the punishment of Allāh is severe.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawma"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَوْنَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnْwَrَt", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "tarawnahā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَذْهَلُ", "translit": "ُlَhْhdَt", "root": "ذهل", "latinTranslit": "tadhhalu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kullu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُرْضِعَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَعِḍْrُm", "root": "رضع", "latinTranslit": "mur'ḍiʿatin"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَمَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّmَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿammā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَرْضَعَتْ", "translit": "ْtَعَḍْrَaʾ", "root": "رضع", "latinTranslit": "arḍaʿat"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَتَضَعُ", "translit": "ُعَḍَtَw", "root": "وضع", "latinTranslit": "wataḍaʿu"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kullu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذَاتِ", "translit": "ِtāَhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhāti"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "حَمْلٍ", "translit": "ٍlْmَḥ", "root": "حمل", "latinTranslit": "ḥamlin"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "حَمْلَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlْmَḥ", "root": "حمل", "latinTranslit": "ḥamlahā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَتَرَى", "translit": "àَrَtَw", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "watarā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsa"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "سُكَٰرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَٰkُs", "root": "سكر", "latinTranslit": "sukārā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "هُم", "translit": "mُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hum"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِسُكَٰرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَٰkُsِb", "root": "سكر", "latinTranslit": "bisukārā"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِkَٰlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walākinna"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "عَذَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhāba"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "شَدِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد", "latinTranslit": "shadīdun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "on the Day you see it, every nursing mother will think no more of her baby, every pregnant female will miscarry, you will think people are drunk when they are not, so severe will be God’s torment", "clearyTranslation": "on the Day you see it, every nursing mother will think no more of her baby, every pregnant female will miscarry, you will think people are drunk when they are not, so severe will be God’s torment"}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلنَّاسِ مَن يُجَٰدِلُ فِى ٱللَّهِ بِغَيْرِ عِلْمٍ وَيَتَّبِعُ كُلَّ شَيْطَٰنٍ مَّرِيدٍ ", "translation": "And of the people is he who disputes about Allāh without knowledge and follows every rebellious devil.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamina"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُجَٰدِلُ", "translit": "ُlِdَٰjgُy", "root": "جدل", "latinTranslit": "yujādilu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغِb", "root": "غير", "latinTranslit": "bighayri"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عِلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlِع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿil'min"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَيَتَّبِعُ", "translit": "ُعِbَّtَyَw", "root": "تبع", "latinTranslit": "wayattabiʿu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulla"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "شَيْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْyَhs", "root": "شطن", "latinTranslit": "shayṭānin"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مَّرِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِrَّm", "root": "مرد", "latinTranslit": "marīdin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Yet still there are some who, with no knowledge, argue about God, who follow every devilish rebel", "clearyTranslation": "Yet still there are some who, with no knowledge, argue about God, who follow every devilish rebel"}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِ أَنَّهُۥ مَن تَوَلَّاهُ فَأَنَّهُۥ يُضِلُّهُۥ وَيَهْدِيهِ إِلَىٰ عَذَابِ ٱلسَّعِيرِ ", "translation": "It has been decreed for him [i.e., every devil] that whoever turns to him - he will misguide him and will lead him to the punishment of the Blaze.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُتِبَ", "translit": "َbِtُk", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "kutiba"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "annahu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَوَلَّاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَّlَwَt", "root": "ولي", "latinTranslit": "tawallāhu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-annahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُضِلُّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُّlِḍُy", "root": "ضلل", "latinTranslit": "yuḍilluhu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيَهْدِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِdْhَyَw", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "wayahdīhi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhābi"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلسَّعِيرِ", "translit": "ِryِعَّslٱ", "root": "سعر", "latinTranslit": "l-saʿīri"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "fated to lead astray those who take his side, and guide them to the suffering of the blazing flame", "clearyTranslation": "fated to lead astray those who take his side, and guide them to the suffering of the blazing flame"}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ إِن كُنتُمْ فِى رَيْبٍ مِّنَ ٱلْبَعْثِ فَإِنَّا خَلَقْنَٰكُم مِّن تُرَابٍ ثُمَّ مِن نُّطْفَةٍ ثُمَّ مِنْ عَلَقَةٍ ثُمَّ مِن مُّضْغَةٍ مُّخَلَّقَةٍ وَغَيْرِ مُخَلَّقَةٍ لِّنُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْۚ وَنُقِرُّ فِى ٱلْأَرْحَامِ مَا نَشَآءُ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّى ثُمَّ نُخْرِجُكُمْ طِفْلًا ثُمَّ لِتَبْلُغُوٓا۟ أَشُدَّكُمْۖ وَمِنكُم مَّن يُتَوَفَّىٰ وَمِنكُم مَّن يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰٓ أَرْذَلِ ٱلْعُمُرِ لِكَيْلَا يَعْلَمَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ عِلْمٍ شَيْـًٔاۚ وَتَرَى ٱلْأَرْضَ هَامِدَةً فَإِذَآ أَنزَلْنَا عَلَيْهَا ٱلْمَآءَ ٱهْتَزَّتْ وَرَبَتْ وَأَنۢبَتَتْ مِن كُلِّ زَوْجٍۭ بَهِيجٍ ", "translation": "O people, if you should be in doubt about the Resurrection, then [consider that] indeed, We created you from dust, then from a sperm-drop, then from a clinging clot, and then from a lump of flesh, formed and unformed[1] - that We may show you.[2] And We settle in the wombs whom We will for a specified term, then We bring you out as a child, and then [We develop you] that you may reach your [time of] maturity. And among you is he who is taken in [early] death, and among you is he who is returned to the most decrepit [old] age so that he knows, after [once having] knowledge, nothing. And you see the earth barren, but when We send down upon it rain, it quivers and swells and grows [something] of every beautiful kind.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "in"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "kuntum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَيْبٍ", "translit": "ٍbْyَr", "root": "ريب", "latinTranslit": "raybin"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْبَعْثِ", "translit": "ِhtْعَbْlٱ", "root": "بعث", "latinTranslit": "l-baʿthi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَإِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-innā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰكُم", "translit": "mُkَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "khalaqnākum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "تُرَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَrُt", "root": "ترب", "latinTranslit": "turābin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "نُّطْفَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَfْṭُّn", "root": "نطف", "latinTranslit": "nuṭ'fatin"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "عَلَقَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَqَlَع", "root": "علق", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaqatin"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مُّضْغَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَغْḍُّm", "root": "مضغ", "latinTranslit": "muḍ'ghatin"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "مُّخَلَّقَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَqَّlَhkُّm", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "mukhallaqatin"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغَw", "root": "غير", "latinTranslit": "waghayri"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مُخَلَّقَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَqَّlَhkُm", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "mukhallaqatin"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لِّنُبَيِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّyَbُnِّl", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "linubayyina"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَنُقِرُّ", "translit": "ُّrِqُnَw", "root": "قرر", "latinTranslit": "wanuqirru"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْحَامِ", "translit": "ِmāَḥْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "l-arḥāmi"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "نَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَn", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "nashāu"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "أَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل", "latinTranslit": "ajalin"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "مُّسَمًّى", "translit": "àًّmَsُّm", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "musamman"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "نُخْرِجُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُjgِrْhkُn", "root": "خرج", "latinTranslit": "nukh'rijukum"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "طِفْلًا", "translit": "āًlْfِṭ", "root": "طفل", "latinTranslit": "ṭif'lan"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "لِتَبْلُغُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُغُlْbَtِl", "root": "بلغ", "latinTranslit": "litablughū"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "أَشُدَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّdُhsَaʾ", "root": "شدد", "latinTranslit": "ashuddakum"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "وَمِنكُم", "translit": "mُknِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waminkum"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "يُتَوَفَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّfَwَtُy", "root": "وفي", "latinTranslit": "yutawaffā"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "وَمِنكُم", "translit": "mُknِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waminkum"}, {"id": 45, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 46, "arabic": "يُرَدُّ", "translit": "ُّdَrُy", "root": "ردد", "latinTranslit": "yuraddu"}, {"id": 47, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 48, "arabic": "أَرْذَلِ", "translit": "ِlَhdْrَaʾ", "root": "رذل", "latinTranslit": "ardhali"}, {"id": 49, "arabic": "ٱلْعُمُرِ", "translit": "ِrُmُعْlٱ", "root": "عمر", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿumuri"}, {"id": 50, "arabic": "لِكَيْلَا", "translit": "āَlْyَkِl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "likaylā"}, {"id": 51, "arabic": "يَعْلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlْعَy", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "yaʿlama"}, {"id": 52, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 53, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْعَb", "root": "بعد", "latinTranslit": "baʿdi"}, {"id": 54, "arabic": "عِلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlِع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿil'min"}, {"id": 55, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayan"}, {"id": 56, "arabic": "وَتَرَى", "translit": "àَrَtَw", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "watarā"}, {"id": 57, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍa"}, {"id": 58, "arabic": "هَامِدَةً", "translit": "ًẗَdِmāَh", "root": "همد", "latinTranslit": "hāmidatan"}, {"id": 59, "arabic": "فَإِذَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhdِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-idhā"}, {"id": 60, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل", "latinTranslit": "anzalnā"}, {"id": 61, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhā"}, {"id": 62, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه", "latinTranslit": "l-māa"}, {"id": 63, "arabic": "ٱهْتَزَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّzَtْhٱ", "root": "هزز", "latinTranslit": "ih'tazzat"}, {"id": 64, "arabic": "وَرَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَrَw", "root": "ربو", "latinTranslit": "warabat"}, {"id": 65, "arabic": "وَأَنۢبَتَتْ", "translit": "ْtَtَbۢnَaʾَw", "root": "نبت", "latinTranslit": "wa-anbatat"}, {"id": 66, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 67, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulli"}, {"id": 68, "arabic": "زَوْجٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭjgْwَz", "root": "زوج", "latinTranslit": "zawjin"}, {"id": 69, "arabic": "بَهِيجٍ", "translit": "ٍjgyِhَb", "root": "بهج", "latinTranslit": "bahījin"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "People, [remember,] if you doubt the Resurrection, that We created you from dust, then a drop of fluid, then a clinging form, then a lump of flesh, both shaped and unshaped: We mean to make Our power clear to you. Whatever We choose We cause to remain in the womb for an appointed time, then We bring you forth as infants and then you grow and reach maturity. Some die young and some are left to live on to such an age that they forget all they once knew. You sometimes see the earth lifeless, yet when We send down water it stirs and swells and produces every kind of joyous growth", "clearyTranslation": "People, [remember,] if you doubt the Resurrection, that We created you from dust, then a drop of fluid, then a clinging form, then a lump of flesh, both shaped and unshaped: We mean to make Our power clear to you. Whatever We choose We cause to remain in the womb for an appointed time, then We bring you forth as infants and then you grow and reach maturity. Some die young and some are left to live on to such an age that they forget all they once knew. You sometimes see the earth lifeless, yet when We send down water it stirs and swells and produces every kind of joyous growth"}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلْحَقُّ وَأَنَّهُۥ يُحْىِ ٱلْمَوْتَىٰ وَأَنَّهُۥ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ ", "translation": "That is because Allāh is the True Reality and because He gives life to the dead and because He is over all things competent", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bi-anna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥaqu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-annahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُحْىِ", "translit": "ِàْḥُy", "root": "حيي", "latinTranslit": "yuḥ'yī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت", "latinTranslit": "l-mawtā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-annahu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulli"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قَدِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِdَq", "root": "قدر", "latinTranslit": "qadīrun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "this is because God is the Truth; He brings the dead back to life; He has power over everything", "clearyTranslation": "this is because God is the Truth; He brings the dead back to life; He has power over everything"}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَأَنَّ ٱلسَّاعَةَ ءَاتِيَةٌ لَّا رَيْبَ فِيهَا وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَبْعَثُ مَن فِى ٱلْقُبُورِ ", "translation": "And [that they may know] that the Hour is coming - no doubt about it - and that Allāh will resurrect those in the graves.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةَ", "translit": "َẗَعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع", "latinTranslit": "l-sāʿata"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَاتِيَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَyِtāَء", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "ātiyatun"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَيْبَ", "translit": "َbْyَr", "root": "ريب", "latinTranslit": "rayba"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَبْعَثُ", "translit": "ُhtَعْbَy", "root": "بعث", "latinTranslit": "yabʿathu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْقُبُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُbُqْlٱ", "root": "قبر", "latinTranslit": "l-qubūri"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "There is no doubt that the Last Hour is bound to come, nor that God will raise the dead from their graves", "clearyTranslation": "There is no doubt that the Last Hour is bound to come, nor that God will raise the dead from their graves"}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلنَّاسِ مَن يُجَٰدِلُ فِى ٱللَّهِ بِغَيْرِ عِلْمٍ وَلَا هُدًى وَلَا كِتَٰبٍ مُّنِيرٍ ", "translation": "And of the people is he who disputes about Allāh without knowledge or guidance or an enlightening book [from Him],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamina"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُجَٰدِلُ", "translit": "ُlِdَٰjgُy", "root": "جدل", "latinTranslit": "yujādilu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغِb", "root": "غير", "latinTranslit": "bighayri"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عِلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlِع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿil'min"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "هُدًى", "translit": "àًdُh", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "hudan"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كِتَٰبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "kitābin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مُّنِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِnُّm", "root": "نور", "latinTranslit": "munīrin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "yet still there are some who, with no knowledge or guidance or any book of enlightenment, argue about God", "clearyTranslation": "yet still there are some who, with no knowledge or guidance or any book of enlightenment, argue about God"}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "ثَانِىَ عِطْفِهِۦ لِيُضِلَّ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِۖ لَهُۥ فِى ٱلدُّنْيَا خِزْىٌۖ وَنُذِيقُهُۥ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ عَذَابَ ٱلْحَرِيقِ ", "translation": "Twisting his neck [in arrogance] to mislead [people] from the way of Allāh. For him in the world is disgrace, and We will make him taste on the Day of Resurrection the punishment of the Burning Fire [while it is said],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثَانِىَ", "translit": "َàِnāَht", "root": "ثني", "latinTranslit": "thāniya"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عِطْفِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِfْṭِع", "root": "عطف", "latinTranslit": "ʿiṭ'fihi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِيُضِلَّ", "translit": "َّlِḍُyِl", "root": "ضلل", "latinTranslit": "liyuḍilla"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿan"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل", "latinTranslit": "sabīli"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو", "latinTranslit": "l-dun'yā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خِزْىٌ", "translit": "ٌàْzِhk", "root": "خزي", "latinTranslit": "khiz'yun"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَنُذِيقُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُqyِhdُnَw", "root": "ذوق", "latinTranslit": "wanudhīquhu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawma"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "l-qiyāmati"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَذَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhāba"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرِيقِ", "translit": "ِqyِrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرق", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥarīqi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "turning scornfully aside to lead others away from God’s path. Disgrace in this world awaits such a person and, on the Day of Resurrection, We shall make him taste the suffering of the Fire", "clearyTranslation": "turning scornfully aside to lead others away from God’s path. Disgrace in this world awaits such a person and, on the Day of Resurrection, We shall make him taste the suffering of the Fire"}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِمَا قَدَّمَتْ يَدَاكَ وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَيْسَ بِظَلَّٰمٍ لِّلْعَبِيدِ ", "translation": "\"That is for what your hands have put forth and because Allāh is not ever unjust to [His] servants.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bimā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَدَّمَتْ", "translit": "ْtَmَّdَq", "root": "قدم", "latinTranslit": "qaddamat"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَدَاكَ", "translit": "َkāَdَy", "root": "يدي", "latinTranslit": "yadāka"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس", "latinTranslit": "laysa"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِظَلَّٰمٍ", "translit": "ٍmَّٰlَẓِb", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "biẓallāmin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِّلْعَبِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِbَعْlِّl", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "lil'ʿabīdi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "[It will be said], ‘This is for what you have stored up with your own hands: God is never unjust to His creatures.’", "clearyTranslation": "[It will be said], ‘This is for what you have stored up with your own hands: God is never unjust to His creatures.’"}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلنَّاسِ مَن يَعْبُدُ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ حَرْفٍۖ فَإِنْ أَصَابَهُۥ خَيْرٌ ٱطْمَأَنَّ بِهِۦۖ وَإِنْ أَصَابَتْهُ فِتْنَةٌ ٱنقَلَبَ عَلَىٰ وَجْهِهِۦ خَسِرَ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَٱلْءَاخِرَةَۚ ذَٰلِكَ هُوَ ٱلْخُسْرَانُ ٱلْمُبِينُ ", "translation": "And of the people is he who worships Allāh on an edge.[1] If he is touched by good, he is reassured by it; but if he is struck by trial, he turns on his face [to unbelief]. He has lost [this] world and the Hereafter. That is what is the manifest loss.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamina"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَعْبُدُ", "translit": "ُdُbْعَy", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "yaʿbudu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَرْفٍ", "translit": "ٍfْrَḥ", "root": "حرف", "latinTranslit": "ḥarfin"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-in"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَصَابَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَbāَṣَaʾ", "root": "صوب", "latinTranslit": "aṣābahu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayrun"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱطْمَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَmْṭٱ", "root": "طمان", "latinTranslit": "iṭ'ma-anna"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَصَابَتْهُ", "translit": "ُhْtَbāَṣَaʾ", "root": "صوب", "latinTranslit": "aṣābathu"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِتْنَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن", "latinTranslit": "fit'natun"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱنقَلَبَ", "translit": "َbَlَqnٱ", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "inqalaba"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَجْهِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِhْjgَw", "root": "وجه", "latinTranslit": "wajhihi"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "خَسِرَ", "translit": "َrِsَhk", "root": "خسر", "latinTranslit": "khasira"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو", "latinTranslit": "l-dun'yā"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَٱلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlٱَw", "root": "اخر", "latinTranslit": "wal-ākhirata"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْخُسْرَانُ", "translit": "ُnāَrْsُhkْlٱ", "root": "خسر", "latinTranslit": "l-khus'rānu"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْمُبِينُ", "translit": "ُnyِbُmْlٱ", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "l-mubīnu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "There are also some who serve God with unsteady faith: if something good comes their way, they are satisfied, but if they are tested, they revert to their old ways, losing both this world and the next- that is the clearest loss", "clearyTranslation": "There are also some who serve God with unsteady faith: if something good comes their way, they are satisfied, but if they are tested, they revert to their old ways, losing both this world and the next- that is the clearest loss"}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَدْعُوا۟ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مَا لَا يَضُرُّهُۥ وَمَا لَا يَنفَعُهُۥۚ ذَٰلِكَ هُوَ ٱلضَّلَٰلُ ٱلْبَعِيدُ ", "translation": "He invokes instead of Allāh that which neither harms him nor benefits him. That is what is the extreme error.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعْdَy", "root": "دعو", "latinTranslit": "yadʿū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون", "latinTranslit": "dūni"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَضُرُّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُّrُḍَy", "root": "ضرر", "latinTranslit": "yaḍurruhu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَنفَعُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُعَfnَy", "root": "نفع", "latinTranslit": "yanfaʿuhu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلضَّلَٰلُ", "translit": "ُlَٰlَّḍlٱ", "root": "ضلل", "latinTranslit": "l-ḍalālu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْبَعِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِعَbْlٱ", "root": "بعد", "latinTranslit": "l-baʿīdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Instead of God, they call upon what can neither harm nor help them- that is straying far away", "clearyTranslation": "Instead of God, they call upon what can neither harm nor help them- that is straying far away"}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "يَدْعُوا۟ لَمَن ضَرُّهُۥٓ أَقْرَبُ مِن نَّفْعِهِۦۚ لَبِئْسَ ٱلْمَوْلَىٰ وَلَبِئْسَ ٱلْعَشِيرُ ", "translation": "He invokes one whose harm is closer than his benefit - how wretched the protector and how wretched the associate.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَدْعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعْdَy", "root": "دعو", "latinTranslit": "yadʿū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَمَن", "translit": "nَmَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "laman"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ضَرُّهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُّrَḍ", "root": "ضرر", "latinTranslit": "ḍarruhu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَقْرَبُ", "translit": "ُbَrْqَaʾ", "root": "قرب", "latinTranslit": "aqrabu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَّفْعِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِعْfَّn", "root": "نفع", "latinTranslit": "nafʿihi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَبِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِbَl", "root": "باس", "latinTranslit": "labi'sa"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَmْlٱ", "root": "ولي", "latinTranslit": "l-mawlā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَبِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِbَlَw", "root": "باس", "latinTranslit": "walabi'sa"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَشِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِhsَعْlٱ", "root": "عشر", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿashīru"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "or invoke one whose harm is closer than his help: an evil master and an evil companion", "clearyTranslation": "or invoke one whose harm is closer than his help: an evil master and an evil companion"}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُدْخِلُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ مَا يُرِيدُ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh will admit those who believe and do righteous deeds to gardens beneath which rivers flow. Indeed, Allāh does what He intends.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُدْخِلُ", "translit": "ُlِhkْdُy", "root": "دخل", "latinTranslit": "yud'khilu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَعَw", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "waʿamilū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣāliḥāti"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن", "latinTranslit": "jannātin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري", "latinTranslit": "tajrī"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت", "latinTranslit": "taḥtihā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "l-anhāru"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَفْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَعْfَy", "root": "فعل", "latinTranslit": "yafʿalu"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُy", "root": "رود", "latinTranslit": "yurīdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "But God will admit those who believe and do good deeds to Gardens graced with flowing streams. God does whatever He wishes", "clearyTranslation": "But God will admit those who believe and do good deeds to Gardens graced with flowing streams. God does whatever He wishes"}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "مَن كَانَ يَظُنُّ أَن لَّن يَنصُرَهُ ٱللَّهُ فِى ٱلدُّنْيَا وَٱلْءَاخِرَةِ فَلْيَمْدُدْ بِسَبَبٍ إِلَى ٱلسَّمَآءِ ثُمَّ لْيَقْطَعْ فَلْيَنظُرْ هَلْ يُذْهِبَنَّ كَيْدُهُۥ مَا يَغِيظُ ", "translation": "Whoever should think that Allāh will not support him [i.e., Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ)] in this world and the Hereafter - let him extend a rope to the ceiling, then cut off [his breath],[1] and let him see: will his effort remove that which enrages [him]?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "kāna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَظُنُّ", "translit": "ُّnُẓَy", "root": "ظنن", "latinTranslit": "yaẓunnu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّن", "translit": "nَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lan"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَنصُرَهُ", "translit": "ُhَrُṣnَy", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "yanṣurahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو", "latinTranslit": "l-dun'yā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱَw", "root": "اخر", "latinTranslit": "wal-ākhirati"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَلْيَمْدُدْ", "translit": "ْdُdْmَyْlَf", "root": "مدد", "latinTranslit": "falyamdud"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بِسَبَبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَbَsِb", "root": "سبب", "latinTranslit": "bisababin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāi"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لْيَقْطَعْ", "translit": "ْعَṭْqَyْl", "root": "قطع", "latinTranslit": "l'yaqṭaʿ"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "فَلْيَنظُرْ", "translit": "ْrُẓnَyْlَf", "root": "نظر", "latinTranslit": "falyanẓur"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hal"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يُذْهِبَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَbِhْhdُy", "root": "ذهب", "latinTranslit": "yudh'hibanna"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "كَيْدُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُdْyَk", "root": "كيد", "latinTranslit": "kayduhu"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "يَغِيظُ", "translit": "ُẓyِغَy", "root": "غيظ", "latinTranslit": "yaghīẓu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Anyone who thinks that God will not support him in this world and the next should stretch a rope up to the sky, climb all the way up it, and see whether this strategy removes the cause of his anger", "clearyTranslation": "Anyone who thinks that God will not support him in this world and the next should stretch a rope up to the sky, climb all the way up it, and see whether this strategy removes the cause of his anger"}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ أَنزَلْنَٰهُ ءَايَٰتٍۭ بَيِّنَٰتٍ وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَهْدِى مَن يُرِيدُ ", "translation": "And thus have We sent it [i.e., the Qur’ān] down as verses of clear evidence and because Allāh guides whom He intends.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَkَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wakadhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنزَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل", "latinTranslit": "anzalnāhu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "āyātin"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيِّنَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰnِّyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "bayyinātin"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَy", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "yahdī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُy", "root": "رود", "latinTranslit": "yurīdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "In this way, We send the Quran down as clear messages, and God guides whoever He will", "clearyTranslation": "In this way, We send the Quran down as clear messages, and God guides whoever He will"}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَٱلَّذِينَ هَادُوا۟ وَٱلصَّٰبِـِٔينَ وَٱلنَّصَٰرَىٰ وَٱلْمَجُوسَ وَٱلَّذِينَ أَشْرَكُوٓا۟ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَفْصِلُ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ شَهِيدٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who have believed and those who were Jews and the Sabeans and the Christians and the Magians and those who associated with Allāh - Allāh will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed Allāh is, over all things, Witness.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هَادُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdāَh", "root": "هود", "latinTranslit": "hādū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلصَّٰبِـِٔينَ", "translit": "َnyِٔـِbَّٰṣlٱَw", "root": "صبا", "latinTranslit": "wal-ṣābiīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّصَٰرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَٰṣَّnlٱَw", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "wal-naṣārā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَجُوسَ", "translit": "َswُjgَmْlٱَw", "root": "مجس", "latinTranslit": "wal-majūsa"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَشْرَكُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُkَrْhsَaʾ", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "ashrakū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَفْصِلُ", "translit": "ُlِṣْfَy", "root": "فصل", "latinTranslit": "yafṣilu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "baynahum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawma"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "l-qiyāmati"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulli"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayin"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "شَهِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد", "latinTranslit": "shahīdun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "As for the believers, those who follow the Jewish faith, the Sabians, the Christians, the Magians, and the idolaters, God will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection; God witnesses all things", "clearyTranslation": "As for the believers, those who follow the Jewish faith, the Sabians, the Christians, the Magians, and the idolaters, God will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection; God witnesses all things"}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَسْجُدُ لَهُۥ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَن فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱلشَّمْسُ وَٱلْقَمَرُ وَٱلنُّجُومُ وَٱلْجِبَالُ وَٱلشَّجَرُ وَٱلدَّوَآبُّ وَكَثِيرٌ مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِۖ وَكَثِيرٌ حَقَّ عَلَيْهِ ٱلْعَذَابُۗ وَمَن يُهِنِ ٱللَّهُ فَمَا لَهُۥ مِن مُّكْرِمٍۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَفْعَلُ مَا يَشَآءُ۩ ", "translation": "Do you not see [i.e., know] that to Allāh prostrates whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth and the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, the moving creatures and many of the people? But upon many the punishment has been justified.[1] And he whom Allāh humiliates - for him there is no bestower of honor. Indeed, Allāh does what He wills.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "tara"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَسْجُدُ", "translit": "ُdُjgْsَy", "root": "سجد", "latinTranslit": "yasjudu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāwāti"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّمْسُ", "translit": "ُsْmَّhslٱَw", "root": "شمس", "latinTranslit": "wal-shamsu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرُ", "translit": "ُrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر", "latinTranslit": "wal-qamaru"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَٱلنُّجُومُ", "translit": "ُmwُjgُّnlٱَw", "root": "نجم", "latinTranslit": "wal-nujūmu"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَٱلْجِبَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَbِjgْlٱَw", "root": "جبل", "latinTranslit": "wal-jibālu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَٱلشَّجَرُ", "translit": "ُrَjgَّhslٱَw", "root": "شجر", "latinTranslit": "wal-shajaru"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَٱلدَّوَآبُّ", "translit": "ُّbٓāَwَّdlٱَw", "root": "دبب", "latinTranslit": "wal-dawābu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَكَثِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِhtَkَw", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "wakathīrun"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَكَثِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِhtَkَw", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "wakathīrun"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "حَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "ḥaqqa"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhi"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَعْlٱ", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿadhābu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "يُهِنِ", "translit": "ِnِhُy", "root": "هون", "latinTranslit": "yuhini"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "فَمَا", "translit": "āَmَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "famā"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "مُّكْرِمٍ", "translit": "ٍmِrْkُّm", "root": "كرم", "latinTranslit": "muk'rimin"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "يَفْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَعْfَy", "root": "فعل", "latinTranslit": "yafʿalu"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "yashāu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Do you not realize [Prophet] that everything in the heavens and earth submits to God: the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, and the animals? So do many human beings, though for many others punishment is well deserved. Anyone disgraced by God will have no one to honour him: God does whatever He will", "clearyTranslation": "Do you not realize [Prophet] that everything in the heavens and earth submits to God: the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, and the animals? So do many human beings, though for many others punishment is well deserved. Anyone disgraced by God will have no one to honour him: God does whatever He will"}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "هَٰذَانِ خَصْمَانِ ٱخْتَصَمُوا۟ فِى رَبِّهِمْۖ فَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ قُطِّعَتْ لَهُمْ ثِيَابٌ مِّن نَّارٍ يُصَبُّ مِن فَوْقِ رُءُوسِهِمُ ٱلْحَمِيمُ ", "translation": "These[1] are two adversaries who have disputed over their Lord. But those who disbelieved will have cut out for them garments of fire. Poured upon their heads will be scalding water", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَٰذَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَhdَٰh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hādhāni"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَصْمَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَmْṣَhk", "root": "خصم", "latinTranslit": "khaṣmāni"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱخْتَصَمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmَṣَtْhkٱ", "root": "خصم", "latinTranslit": "ikh'taṣamū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbihim"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُطِّعَتْ", "translit": "ْtَعِّṭُq", "root": "قطع", "latinTranslit": "quṭṭiʿat"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ثِيَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَyِht", "root": "ثوب", "latinTranslit": "thiyābun"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "نَّارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَّn", "root": "نور", "latinTranslit": "nārin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُصَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَṣُy", "root": "صبب", "latinTranslit": "yuṣabbu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَوْقِ", "translit": "ِqْwَf", "root": "فوق", "latinTranslit": "fawqi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "رُءُوسِهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِswُءُr", "root": "راس", "latinTranslit": "ruūsihimu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمم", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥamīmu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "These two kinds of people disagree about their Lord. Garments of fire will be tailored for those who disbelieve; scalding water will be poured over their heads", "clearyTranslation": "These two kinds of people disagree about their Lord. Garments of fire will be tailored for those who disbelieve; scalding water will be poured over their heads"}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "يُصْهَرُ بِهِۦ مَا فِى بُطُونِهِمْ وَٱلْجُلُودُ ", "translation": "By which is melted that within their bellies and [their] skins.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُصْهَرُ", "translit": "ُrَhْṣُy", "root": "صهر", "latinTranslit": "yuṣ'haru"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بُطُونِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِnwُṭُb", "root": "بطن", "latinTranslit": "buṭūnihim"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱلْجُلُودُ", "translit": "ُdwُlُjgْlٱَw", "root": "جلد", "latinTranslit": "wal-julūdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "melting their insides as well as their skins", "clearyTranslation": "melting their insides as well as their skins"}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَلَهُم مَّقَٰمِعُ مِنْ حَدِيدٍ ", "translation": "And for [striking] them are maces of iron.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walahum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَّقَٰمِعُ", "translit": "ُعِmَٰqَّm", "root": "قمع", "latinTranslit": "maqāmiʿu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَدِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِdَḥ", "root": "حدد", "latinTranslit": "ḥadīdin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "there will be iron crooks to restrain them", "clearyTranslation": "there will be iron crooks to restrain them"}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "كُلَّمَآ أَرَادُوٓا۟ أَن يَخْرُجُوا۟ مِنْهَا مِنْ غَمٍّ أُعِيدُوا۟ فِيهَا وَذُوقُوا۟ عَذَابَ ٱلْحَرِيقِ ", "translation": "Every time they want to get out of it [i.e., Hellfire] from anguish, they will be returned to it, and [it will be said], \"Taste the punishment of the Burning Fire!\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كُلَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kullamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرَادُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdāَrَaʾ", "root": "رود", "latinTranslit": "arādū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَخْرُجُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُjgُrْhkَy", "root": "خرج", "latinTranslit": "yakhrujū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "غَمٍّ", "translit": "ٍّmَغ", "root": "غمم", "latinTranslit": "ghammin"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أُعِيدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdyِعُaʾ", "root": "عود", "latinTranslit": "uʿīdū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَذُوقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqwُhdَw", "root": "ذوق", "latinTranslit": "wadhūqū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَذَابَ", "translit": "َbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhāba"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرِيقِ", "translit": "ِqyِrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرق", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥarīqi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "whenever, in their anguish, they try to escape, they will be pushed back in and told, ‘Taste the suffering of the Fire.’", "clearyTranslation": "whenever, in their anguish, they try to escape, they will be pushed back in and told, ‘Taste the suffering of the Fire.’"}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُدْخِلُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ يُحَلَّوْنَ فِيهَا مِنْ أَسَاوِرَ مِن ذَهَبٍ وَلُؤْلُؤًاۖ وَلِبَاسُهُمْ فِيهَا حَرِيرٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh will admit those who believe and do righteous deeds to gardens beneath which rivers flow. They will be adorned therein with bracelets of gold and pearl, and their garments therein will be silk.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُدْخِلُ", "translit": "ُlِhkْdُy", "root": "دخل", "latinTranslit": "yud'khilu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَعَw", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "waʿamilū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣāliḥāti"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن", "latinTranslit": "jannātin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري", "latinTranslit": "tajrī"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت", "latinTranslit": "taḥtihā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "l-anhāru"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُحَلَّوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَّlَḥُy", "root": "حلي", "latinTranslit": "yuḥallawna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَسَاوِرَ", "translit": "َrِwāَsَaʾ", "root": "سور", "latinTranslit": "asāwira"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ذَهَبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَhَhd", "root": "ذهب", "latinTranslit": "dhahabin"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَلُؤْلُؤًا", "translit": "āًwʾُlْwʾُlَw", "root": "لؤلؤ", "latinTranslit": "walu'lu-an"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَلِبَاسُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُsāَbِlَw", "root": "لبس", "latinTranslit": "walibāsuhum"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "حَرِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِrَḥ", "root": "حرر", "latinTranslit": "ḥarīrun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "But God will admit those who believe and do good deeds to Gardens graced with flowing streams; there they will be adorned with golden bracelets and pearls; there they will have silken garments", "clearyTranslation": "But God will admit those who believe and do good deeds to Gardens graced with flowing streams; there they will be adorned with golden bracelets and pearls; there they will have silken garments"}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "وَهُدُوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱلطَّيِّبِ مِنَ ٱلْقَوْلِ وَهُدُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطِ ٱلْحَمِيدِ ", "translation": "And they had been guided [in worldly life] to good speech, and they were guided to the path of the Praiseworthy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُدُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdُhَw", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "wahudū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلطَّيِّبِ", "translit": "ِbِّyَّṭlٱ", "root": "طيب", "latinTranslit": "l-ṭayibi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلِ", "translit": "ِlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "l-qawli"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَهُدُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُdُhَw", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "wahudū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صِرَٰطِ", "translit": "ِṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط", "latinTranslit": "ṣirāṭi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥamīdi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "They were guided to good speech and to the path of the One Worthy of all Praise", "clearyTranslation": "They were guided to good speech and to the path of the One Worthy of all Praise"}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَيَصُدُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَٱلْمَسْجِدِ ٱلْحَرَامِ ٱلَّذِى جَعَلْنَٰهُ لِلنَّاسِ سَوَآءً ٱلْعَٰكِفُ فِيهِ وَٱلْبَادِۚ وَمَن يُرِدْ فِيهِ بِإِلْحَادٍۭ بِظُلْمٍ نُّذِقْهُ مِنْ عَذَابٍ أَلِيمٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who have disbelieved and avert [people] from the way of Allāh and [from] al-Masjid al-Ḥarām, which We made for the people - equal are the resident therein and one from outside - and [also] whoever intends [a deed] therein[1] of deviation [in religion] by wrongdoing - We will make him taste of a painful punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَصُدُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّdُṣَyَw", "root": "صدد", "latinTranslit": "wayaṣuddūna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿan"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل", "latinTranslit": "sabīli"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَسْجِدِ", "translit": "ِdِjgْsَmْlٱَw", "root": "سجد", "latinTranslit": "wal-masjidi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْحَرَامِ", "translit": "ِmāَrَḥْlٱ", "root": "حرم", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥarāmi"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhī"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْlَعَjg", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "jaʿalnāhu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlِl", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "lilnnāsi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سَوَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَwَs", "root": "سوي", "latinTranslit": "sawāan"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْعَٰكِفُ", "translit": "ُfِkَٰعْlٱ", "root": "عكف", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿākifu"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَٱلْبَادِ", "translit": "ِdāَbْlٱَw", "root": "بدو", "latinTranslit": "wal-bādi"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يُرِدْ", "translit": "ْdِrُy", "root": "رود", "latinTranslit": "yurid"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhi"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بِإِلْحَادٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭdāَḥْlِiʾِb", "root": "لحد", "latinTranslit": "bi-il'ḥādin"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "بِظُلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlُẓِb", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "biẓul'min"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "نُّذِقْهُ", "translit": "ُhْqِhdُّn", "root": "ذوق", "latinTranslit": "nudhiq'hu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "عَذَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhābin"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "أَلِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم", "latinTranslit": "alīmin"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "As for the disbelievers, who bar others from God’s path and from the Sacred Mosque- which We made for all people, residents and visitors alike- and who try to violate it with wrongdoing, We shall make them taste a painful punishment", "clearyTranslation": "As for the disbelievers, who bar others from God’s path and from the Sacred Mosque- which We made for all people, residents and visitors alike- and who try to violate it with wrongdoing, We shall make them taste a painful punishment"}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "وَإِذْ بَوَّأْنَا لِإِبْرَٰهِيمَ مَكَانَ ٱلْبَيْتِ أَن لَّا تُشْرِكْ بِى شَيْـًٔا وَطَهِّرْ بَيْتِىَ لِلطَّآئِفِينَ وَٱلْقَآئِمِينَ وَٱلرُّكَّعِ ٱلسُّجُودِ ", "translation": "And [mention, O Muḥammad], when We designated for Abraham the site of the House, [saying], \"Do not associate anything with Me and purify My House for those who perform ṭawāf[1] and those who stand [in prayer] and those who bow and prostrate.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-idh"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَوَّأْنَا", "translit": "āَnْaʾَّwَb", "root": "بوا", "latinTranslit": "bawwanā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِإِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾِl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "li-ib'rāhīma"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَm", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "makāna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيْتِ", "translit": "ِtْyَbْlٱ", "root": "بيت", "latinTranslit": "l-bayti"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُشْرِكْ", "translit": "ْkِrْhsُt", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "tush'rik"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِى", "translit": "àِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bī"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayan"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَطَهِّرْ", "translit": "ْrِّhَṭَw", "root": "طهر", "latinTranslit": "waṭahhir"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَيْتِىَ", "translit": "َàِtْyَb", "root": "بيت", "latinTranslit": "baytiya"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِلطَّآئِفِينَ", "translit": "َnyِfِyʾٓāَّṭlِl", "root": "طوف", "latinTranslit": "lilṭṭāifīna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَآئِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِyʾٓāَqْlٱَw", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "wal-qāimīna"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَٱلرُّكَّعِ", "translit": "ِعَّkُّrlٱَw", "root": "ركع", "latinTranslit": "wal-rukaʿi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلسُّجُودِ", "translit": "ِdwُjgُّslٱ", "root": "سجد", "latinTranslit": "l-sujūdi"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "We showed Abraham the site of the House, saying, ‘Do not assign partners to Me. Purify My House for those who circle around it, those who stand to pray, and those who bow and prostrate themselves", "clearyTranslation": "We showed Abraham the site of the House, saying, ‘Do not assign partners to Me. Purify My House for those who circle around it, those who stand to pray, and those who bow and prostrate themselves"}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "وَأَذِّن فِى ٱلنَّاسِ بِٱلْحَجِّ يَأْتُوكَ رِجَالًا وَعَلَىٰ كُلِّ ضَامِرٍ يَأْتِينَ مِن كُلِّ فَجٍّ عَمِيقٍ ", "translation": "And proclaim to the people the ḥajj [pilgrimage]; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel; they will come from every distant pass -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَذِّن", "translit": "nِّhdَaʾَw", "root": "اذن", "latinTranslit": "wa-adhin"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَجِّ", "translit": "ِّjgَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حجج", "latinTranslit": "bil-ḥaji"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَأْتُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "yatūka"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رِجَالًا", "translit": "āًlāَjgِr", "root": "رجل", "latinTranslit": "rijālan"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَعَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَعَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waʿalā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulli"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ضَامِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِmāَḍ", "root": "ضمر", "latinTranslit": "ḍāmirin"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يَأْتِينَ", "translit": "َnyِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "yatīna"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulli"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَجٍّ", "translit": "ٍّjgَf", "root": "فجج", "latinTranslit": "fajjin"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَمِيقٍ", "translit": "ٍqyِmَع", "root": "عمق", "latinTranslit": "ʿamīqin"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Proclaim the Pilgrimage to all people. They will come to you on foot and on every kind of swift mount, emerging from every deep mountain pass", "clearyTranslation": "Proclaim the Pilgrimage to all people. They will come to you on foot and on every kind of swift mount, emerging from every deep mountain pass"}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "لِّيَشْهَدُوا۟ مَنَٰفِعَ لَهُمْ وَيَذْكُرُوا۟ ٱسْمَ ٱللَّهِ فِىٓ أَيَّامٍ مَّعْلُومَٰتٍ عَلَىٰ مَا رَزَقَهُم مِّنۢ بَهِيمَةِ ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِۖ فَكُلُوا۟ مِنْهَا وَأَطْعِمُوا۟ ٱلْبَآئِسَ ٱلْفَقِيرَ ", "translation": "That they may witness [i.e., attend] benefits for themselves and mention the name of Allāh on known [i.e., specific] days over what He has provided for them of [sacrificial] animals.[1] So eat of them and feed the miserable and poor.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِّيَشْهَدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdَhْhsَyِّl", "root": "شهد", "latinTranslit": "liyashhadū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنَٰفِعَ", "translit": "َعِfَٰnَm", "root": "نفع", "latinTranslit": "manāfiʿa"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيَذْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhdَyَw", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "wayadhkurū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱسْمَ", "translit": "َmْsٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "is'ma"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَيَّامٍ", "translit": "ٍmāَّyَaʾ", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "ayyāmin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّعْلُومَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰmwُlْعَّm", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "maʿlūmātin"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَزَقَهُم", "translit": "mُhَqَzَr", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "razaqahum"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَهِيمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmyِhَb", "root": "بهم", "latinTranslit": "bahīmati"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِ", "translit": "ِmَٰعْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "l-anʿāmi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَكُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُkَf", "root": "اكل", "latinTranslit": "fakulū"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hā"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَأَطْعِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِعْṭَaʾَw", "root": "طعم", "latinTranslit": "wa-aṭʿimū"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلْبَآئِسَ", "translit": "َsِyʾٓāَbْlٱ", "root": "باس", "latinTranslit": "l-bāisa"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْفَقِيرَ", "translit": "َryِqَfْlٱ", "root": "فقر", "latinTranslit": "l-faqīra"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "to attain benefits and celebrate God’s name, on specified days, over the livestock He has provided for them- feed yourselves and the poor and unfortunate", "clearyTranslation": "to attain benefits and celebrate God’s name, on specified days, over the livestock He has provided for them- feed yourselves and the poor and unfortunate"}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "ثُمَّ لْيَقْضُوا۟ تَفَثَهُمْ وَلْيُوفُوا۟ نُذُورَهُمْ وَلْيَطَّوَّفُوا۟ بِٱلْبَيْتِ ٱلْعَتِيقِ ", "translation": "Then let them end their untidiness and fulfill their vows and perform ṭawāf around the ancient House.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لْيَقْضُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḍْqَyْl", "root": "قضي", "latinTranslit": "l'yaqḍū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَفَثَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَhtَfَt", "root": "تفث", "latinTranslit": "tafathahum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَلْيُوفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfwُyْlَw", "root": "وفي", "latinTranslit": "walyūfū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُذُورَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَrwُhdُn", "root": "نذر", "latinTranslit": "nudhūrahum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلْيَطَّوَّفُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُfَّwَّṭَyْlَw", "root": "طوف", "latinTranslit": "walyaṭṭawwafū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَيْتِ", "translit": "ِtْyَbْlٱِb", "root": "بيت", "latinTranslit": "bil-bayti"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْعَتِيقِ", "translit": "ِqyِtَعْlٱ", "root": "عتق", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿatīqi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "so let the pilgrims perform their acts of cleansing, fulfil their vows, and circle around the Ancient House.’", "clearyTranslation": "so let the pilgrims perform their acts of cleansing, fulfil their vows, and circle around the Ancient House.’"}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ وَمَن يُعَظِّمْ حُرُمَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ فَهُوَ خَيْرٌ لَّهُۥ عِندَ رَبِّهِۦۗ وَأُحِلَّتْ لَكُمُ ٱلْأَنْعَٰمُ إِلَّا مَا يُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْكُمْۖ فَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ ٱلرِّجْسَ مِنَ ٱلْأَوْثَٰنِ وَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ قَوْلَ ٱلزُّورِ ", "translation": "That [has been commanded], and whoever honors the sacred ordinances of Allāh - it is best for him in the sight of his Lord. And permitted to you are the grazing livestock, except what is recited to you.[1] So avoid the uncleanliness of idols and avoid false statement,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُعَظِّمْ", "translit": "ْmِّẓَعُy", "root": "عظم", "latinTranslit": "yuʿaẓẓim"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حُرُمَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰmُrُḥ", "root": "حرم", "latinTranslit": "ḥurumāti"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fahuwa"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayrun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿinda"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbihi"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَأُحِلَّتْ", "translit": "ْtَّlِḥُaʾَw", "root": "حلل", "latinTranslit": "wa-uḥillat"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakumu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْعَٰمُ", "translit": "ُmَٰعْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "l-anʿāmu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "يُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُy", "root": "تلو", "latinTranslit": "yut'lā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaykum"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِnَtْjgٱَf", "root": "جنب", "latinTranslit": "fa-ij'tanibū"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "ٱلرِّجْسَ", "translit": "َsْjgِّrlٱ", "root": "رجس", "latinTranslit": "l-rij'sa"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوْثَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰhtْwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "وثن", "latinTranslit": "l-awthāni"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbِnَtْjgٱَw", "root": "جنب", "latinTranslit": "wa-ij'tanibū"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "قَوْلَ", "translit": "َlْwَq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qawla"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلزُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّzlٱ", "root": "زور", "latinTranslit": "l-zūri"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "All this [is ordained by God]: anyone who honours the sacred ordinances of God will have good rewards from his Lord. Livestock have been made lawful to you, except for what has been explicitly forbidden. Shun the filth of idolatrous beliefs and practices and shun false utterances", "clearyTranslation": "All this [is ordained by God]: anyone who honours the sacred ordinances of God will have good rewards from his Lord. Livestock have been made lawful to you, except for what has been explicitly forbidden. Shun the filth of idolatrous beliefs and practices and shun false utterances"}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "حُنَفَآءَ لِلَّهِ غَيْرَ مُشْرِكِينَ بِهِۦۚ وَمَن يُشْرِكْ بِٱللَّهِ فَكَأَنَّمَا خَرَّ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ فَتَخْطَفُهُ ٱلطَّيْرُ أَوْ تَهْوِى بِهِ ٱلرِّيحُ فِى مَكَانٍ سَحِيقٍ ", "translation": "Inclining [only] to Allāh, not associating [anything] with Him. And he who associates with Allāh - it is as though he had fallen from the sky and was snatched by the birds or the wind carried him down into a remote place.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "حُنَفَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَfَnُḥ", "root": "حنف", "latinTranslit": "ḥunafāa"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "lillahi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "غَيْرَ", "translit": "َrْyَغ", "root": "غير", "latinTranslit": "ghayra"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُشْرِكِينَ", "translit": "َnyِkِrْhsُm", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "mush'rikīna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُشْرِكْ", "translit": "ْkِrْhsُy", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "yush'rik"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "bil-lahi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَكَأَنَّمَا", "translit": "āَmَّnَaʾَkَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "faka-annamā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَرَّ", "translit": "َّrَhk", "root": "خرر", "latinTranslit": "kharra"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "فَتَخْطَفُهُ", "translit": "ُhُfَṭْhkَtَf", "root": "خطف", "latinTranslit": "fatakhṭafuhu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلطَّيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَّṭlٱ", "root": "طير", "latinTranslit": "l-ṭayru"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "aw"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تَهْوِى", "translit": "àِwْhَt", "root": "هوي", "latinTranslit": "tahwī"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِهِ", "translit": "ِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلرِّيحُ", "translit": "ُḥyِّrlٱ", "root": "روح", "latinTranslit": "l-rīḥu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "مَكَانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَkَm", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "makānin"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "سَحِيقٍ", "translit": "ٍqyِḥَs", "root": "سحق", "latinTranslit": "saḥīqin"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Devote yourselves to God and assign Him no partners, for the person who does so is like someone who has been hurled down from the skies and snatched up by the birds or flung to a distant place by the wind", "clearyTranslation": "Devote yourselves to God and assign Him no partners, for the person who does so is like someone who has been hurled down from the skies and snatched up by the birds or flung to a distant place by the wind"}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ وَمَن يُعَظِّمْ شَعَٰٓئِرَ ٱللَّهِ فَإِنَّهَا مِن تَقْوَى ٱلْقُلُوبِ ", "translation": "That [is so]. And whoever honors the symbols [i.e., rites] of Allāh - indeed, it is from the piety of hearts.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُعَظِّمْ", "translit": "ْmِّẓَعُy", "root": "عظم", "latinTranslit": "yuʿaẓẓim"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "شَعَٰٓئِرَ", "translit": "َrِyʾَٰٓعَhs", "root": "شعر", "latinTranslit": "shaʿāira"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّnِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-innahā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَقْوَى", "translit": "àَwْqَt", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "taqwā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْقُلُوبِ", "translit": "ِbwُlُqْlٱ", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "l-qulūbi"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "All this [is ordained by God]: those who honour God’s rites show the piety of their hearts", "clearyTranslation": "All this [is ordained by God]: those who honour God’s rites show the piety of their hearts"}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "لَكُمْ فِيهَا مَنَٰفِعُ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّى ثُمَّ مَحِلُّهَآ إِلَى ٱلْبَيْتِ ٱلْعَتِيقِ ", "translation": "For you therein [i.e., the animals marked for sacrifice] are benefits for a specified term;[1] then their place of sacrifice is at the ancient House.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَنَٰفِعُ", "translit": "ُعِfَٰnَm", "root": "نفع", "latinTranslit": "manāfiʿu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَجَلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل", "latinTranslit": "ajalin"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُّسَمًّى", "translit": "àًّmَsُّm", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "musamman"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَحِلُّهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhُّlِḥَm", "root": "حلل", "latinTranslit": "maḥilluhā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيْتِ", "translit": "ِtْyَbْlٱ", "root": "بيت", "latinTranslit": "l-bayti"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْعَتِيقِ", "translit": "ِqyِtَعْlٱ", "root": "عتق", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿatīqi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Live-stock are useful to you until the set time. Then their place of sacrifice is near the Ancient House", "clearyTranslation": "Live-stock are useful to you until the set time. Then their place of sacrifice is near the Ancient House"}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَلِكُلِّ أُمَّةٍ جَعَلْنَا مَنسَكًا لِّيَذْكُرُوا۟ ٱسْمَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَىٰ مَا رَزَقَهُم مِّنۢ بَهِيمَةِ ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِۗ فَإِلَٰهُكُمْ إِلَٰهٌ وَٰحِدٌ فَلَهُۥٓ أَسْلِمُوا۟ۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلْمُخْبِتِينَ ", "translation": "And for every [religious] community We have appointed a rite [of sacrifice][1] that they may mention the name of Allāh over what He has provided for them of [sacrificial] animals. For your god is one God, so to Him submit. And, [O Muḥammad], give good tidings to the humble [before their Lord]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِlَw", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "walikulli"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُمَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم", "latinTranslit": "ummatin"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَعَjg", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "jaʿalnā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَنسَكًا", "translit": "āًkَsnَm", "root": "نسك", "latinTranslit": "mansakan"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِّيَذْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhdَyِّl", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "liyadhkurū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱسْمَ", "translit": "َmْsٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "is'ma"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَزَقَهُم", "translit": "mُhَqَzَr", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "razaqahum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَهِيمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmyِhَb", "root": "بهم", "latinTranslit": "bahīmati"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْعَٰمِ", "translit": "ِmَٰعْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "l-anʿāmi"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَإِلَٰهُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُhَٰlِiʾَf", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "fa-ilāhukum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَٰهٌ", "translit": "ٌhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "ilāhun"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَٰحِدٌ", "translit": "ٌdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد", "latinTranslit": "wāḥidun"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "فَلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "falahu"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَسْلِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِlْsَaʾ", "root": "سلم", "latinTranslit": "aslimū"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَبَشِّرِ", "translit": "ِrِّhsَbَw", "root": "بشر", "latinTranslit": "wabashiri"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْمُخْبِتِينَ", "translit": "َnyِtِbْhkُmْlٱ", "root": "خبت", "latinTranslit": "l-mukh'bitīna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "We appointed acts of devotion for every community, for them to celebrate God’s name over the livestock He provided for them: your God is One, so devote yourselves to Him. [Prophet], give good news to the humble", "clearyTranslation": "We appointed acts of devotion for every community, for them to celebrate God’s name over the livestock He provided for them: your God is One, so devote yourselves to Him. [Prophet], give good news to the humble"}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ إِذَا ذُكِرَ ٱللَّهُ وَجِلَتْ قُلُوبُهُمْ وَٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ عَلَىٰ مَآ أَصَابَهُمْ وَٱلْمُقِيمِى ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ ", "translation": "Who, when Allāh is mentioned, their hearts are fearful, and [to] the patient over what has afflicted them, and the establishers of prayer and those who spend from what We have provided them.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "idhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذُكِرَ", "translit": "َrِkُhd", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "dhukira"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجِلَتْ", "translit": "ْtَlِjgَw", "root": "وجل", "latinTranslit": "wajilat"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbuhum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِbَّٰṣlٱَw", "root": "صبر", "latinTranslit": "wal-ṣābirīna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَصَابَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbāَṣَaʾ", "root": "صوب", "latinTranslit": "aṣābahum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُقِيمِى", "translit": "àِmyِqُmْlٱَw", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "wal-muqīmī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣalati"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَمِمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamimmā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْqَzَr", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "razaqnāhum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يُنفِقُونَ", "translit": "َnwُqِfnُy", "root": "نفق", "latinTranslit": "yunfiqūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "whose hearts fill with awe whenever God is mentioned, who endure whatever happens to them with patience, who keep up the prayer, who give to others out of Our provision to them", "clearyTranslation": "whose hearts fill with awe whenever God is mentioned, who endure whatever happens to them with patience, who keep up the prayer, who give to others out of Our provision to them"}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَٱلْبُدْنَ جَعَلْنَٰهَا لَكُم مِّن شَعَٰٓئِرِ ٱللَّهِ لَكُمْ فِيهَا خَيْرٌۖ فَٱذْكُرُوا۟ ٱسْمَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْهَا صَوَآفَّۖ فَإِذَا وَجَبَتْ جُنُوبُهَا فَكُلُوا۟ مِنْهَا وَأَطْعِمُوا۟ ٱلْقَانِعَ وَٱلْمُعْتَرَّۚ كَذَٰلِكَ سَخَّرْنَٰهَا لَكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ ", "translation": "And the camels and cattle We have appointed for you as among the symbols [i.e., rites] of Allāh; for you therein is good. So mention the name of Allāh upon them when lined up [for sacrifice]; and when they are [lifeless] on their sides, then eat from them and feed the needy [who does not seek aid] and the beggar. Thus have We subjected them to you that you may be grateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلْبُدْنَ", "translit": "َnْdُbْlٱَw", "root": "بدن", "latinTranslit": "wal-bud'na"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْlَعَjg", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "jaʿalnāhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَعَٰٓئِرِ", "translit": "ِrِyʾَٰٓعَhs", "root": "شعر", "latinTranslit": "shaʿāiri"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayrun"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَٱذْكُرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrُkْhdٱَf", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "fa-udh'kurū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱسْمَ", "translit": "َmْsٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "is'ma"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَلَيْهَا", "translit": "āَhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "صَوَآفَّ", "translit": "َّfٓāَwَṣ", "root": "صفف", "latinTranslit": "ṣawāffa"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-idhā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَجَبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَjgَw", "root": "وجب", "latinTranslit": "wajabat"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "جُنُوبُهَا", "translit": "āَhُbwُnُjg", "root": "جنب", "latinTranslit": "junūbuhā"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "فَكُلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlُkَf", "root": "اكل", "latinTranslit": "fakulū"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مِنْهَا", "translit": "āَhْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَأَطْعِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِعْṭَaʾَw", "root": "طعم", "latinTranslit": "wa-aṭʿimū"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلْقَانِعَ", "translit": "َعِnāَqْlٱ", "root": "قنع", "latinTranslit": "l-qāniʿa"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُعْتَرَّ", "translit": "َّrَtْعُmْlٱَw", "root": "عرر", "latinTranslit": "wal-muʿ'tara"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "kadhālika"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "سَخَّرْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْrَّhkَs", "root": "سخر", "latinTranslit": "sakharnāhā"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَعَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "laʿallakum"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "تَشْكُرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrُkْhsَt", "root": "شكر", "latinTranslit": "tashkurūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "We have made camels part of God’s sacred rites for you. There is much good in them for you, so invoke God’s name over them as they are lined up for sacrifice, then, when they have fallen down dead, feed yourselves and those who do not ask, as well as those who do. We have subjected them to you in this way so that you may be thankful", "clearyTranslation": "We have made camels part of God’s sacred rites for you. There is much good in them for you, so invoke God’s name over them as they are lined up for sacrifice, then, when they have fallen down dead, feed yourselves and those who do not ask, as well as those who do. We have subjected them to you in this way so that you may be thankful"}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "لَن يَنَالَ ٱللَّهَ لُحُومُهَا وَلَا دِمَآؤُهَا وَلَٰكِن يَنَالُهُ ٱلتَّقْوَىٰ مِنكُمْۚ كَذَٰلِكَ سَخَّرَهَا لَكُمْ لِتُكَبِّرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ مَا هَدَىٰكُمْۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ ", "translation": "Their meat will not reach Allāh, nor will their blood, but what reaches Him is piety from you. Thus have We subjected them to you that you may glorify Allāh for that [to] which He has guided you; and give good tidings to the doers of good.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَن", "translit": "nَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lan"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَنَالَ", "translit": "َlāَnَy", "root": "نيل", "latinTranslit": "yanāla"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لُحُومُهَا", "translit": "āَhُmwُḥُl", "root": "لحم", "latinTranslit": "luḥūmuhā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "دِمَآؤُهَا", "translit": "āَhُwʾٓāَmِd", "root": "دمو", "latinTranslit": "dimāuhā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walākin"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَنَالُهُ", "translit": "ُhُlāَnَy", "root": "نيل", "latinTranslit": "yanāluhu"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلتَّقْوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwْqَّtlٱ", "root": "وقي", "latinTranslit": "l-taqwā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِنكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُknِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "minkum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "kadhālika"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَخَّرَهَا", "translit": "āَhَrَّhkَs", "root": "سخر", "latinTranslit": "sakharahā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لِتُكَبِّرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِّbَkُtِl", "root": "كبر", "latinTranslit": "litukabbirū"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "هَدَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَdَh", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "hadākum"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَبَشِّرِ", "translit": "ِrِّhsَbَw", "root": "بشر", "latinTranslit": "wabashiri"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِsْḥُmْlٱ", "root": "حسن", "latinTranslit": "l-muḥ'sinīna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "It is neither their meat nor their blood that reaches God but your piety. He has subjected them to you in this way so that you may glorify God for having guided you. Give good news to those who do good", "clearyTranslation": "It is neither their meat nor their blood that reaches God but your piety. He has subjected them to you in this way so that you may glorify God for having guided you. Give good news to those who do good"}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُدَٰفِعُ عَنِ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُحِبُّ كُلَّ خَوَّانٍ كَفُورٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh defends those who have believed. Indeed, Allāh does not like everyone treacherous and ungrateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُدَٰفِعُ", "translit": "ُعِfَٰdُy", "root": "دفع", "latinTranslit": "yudāfiʿu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿani"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "يُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُy", "root": "حبب", "latinTranslit": "yuḥibbu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "kulla"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "خَوَّانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَّwَhk", "root": "خون", "latinTranslit": "khawwānin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَفُورٍ", "translit": "ٍrwُfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafūrin"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "God will defend the believers; God does not love the unfaithful or the ungrateful", "clearyTranslation": "God will defend the believers; God does not love the unfaithful or the ungrateful"}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "أُذِنَ لِلَّذِينَ يُقَٰتَلُونَ بِأَنَّهُمْ ظُلِمُوا۟ۚ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ نَصْرِهِمْ لَقَدِيرٌ ", "translation": "Permission [to fight] has been given to those who are being fought,[1] because they were wronged. And indeed, Allāh is competent to give them victory.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أُذِنَ", "translit": "َnِhdُaʾ", "root": "اذن", "latinTranslit": "udhina"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lilladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُقَٰتَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَtَٰqُy", "root": "قتل", "latinTranslit": "yuqātalūna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِأَنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnَaʾِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bi-annahum"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ظُلِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِlُẓ", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "ẓulimū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَصْرِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِrْṣَn", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "naṣrihim"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَقَدِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِdَqَl", "root": "قدر", "latinTranslit": "laqadīrun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Those who have been attacked are permitted to take up arms because they have been wronged- God has the power to help them", "clearyTranslation": "Those who have been attacked are permitted to take up arms because they have been wronged- God has the power to help them"}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ أُخْرِجُوا۟ مِن دِيَٰرِهِم بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ إِلَّآ أَن يَقُولُوا۟ رَبُّنَا ٱللَّهُۗ وَلَوْلَا دَفْعُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلنَّاسَ بَعْضَهُم بِبَعْضٍ لَّهُدِّمَتْ صَوَٰمِعُ وَبِيَعٌ وَصَلَوَٰتٌ وَمَسَٰجِدُ يُذْكَرُ فِيهَا ٱسْمُ ٱللَّهِ كَثِيرًاۗ وَلَيَنصُرَنَّ ٱللَّهُ مَن يَنصُرُهُۥٓۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَقَوِىٌّ عَزِيزٌ ", "translation": "[They are] those who have been evicted from their homes without right - only because they say, \"Our Lord is Allāh.\" And were it not that Allāh checks the people, some by means of others, there would have been demolished monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques in which the name of Allāh is much mentioned [i.e., praised]. And Allāh will surely support those who support Him [i.e., His cause]. Indeed, Allāh is Powerful and Exalted in Might.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُخْرِجُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُjgِrْhkُaʾ", "root": "خرج", "latinTranslit": "ukh'rijū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دِيَٰرِهِم", "translit": "mِhِrَٰyِd", "root": "دور", "latinTranslit": "diyārihim"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِغَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَغِb", "root": "غير", "latinTranslit": "bighayri"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "حَقٍّ", "translit": "ٍّqَḥ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "ḥaqqin"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَقُولُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "yaqūlū"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبُّنَا", "translit": "āَnُّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbunā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَوْلَا", "translit": "āَlْwَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walawlā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "دَفْعُ", "translit": "ُعْfَd", "root": "دفع", "latinTranslit": "dafʿu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسَ", "translit": "َsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsa"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَعْضَهُم", "translit": "mُhَḍْعَb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "baʿḍahum"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "بِبَعْضٍ", "translit": "ٍḍْعَbِb", "root": "بعض", "latinTranslit": "bibaʿḍin"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَّهُدِّمَتْ", "translit": "ْtَmِّdُhَّl", "root": "هدم", "latinTranslit": "lahuddimat"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "صَوَٰمِعُ", "translit": "ُعِmَٰwَṣ", "root": "صمع", "latinTranslit": "ṣawāmiʿu"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "وَبِيَعٌ", "translit": "ٌعَyِbَw", "root": "بيع", "latinTranslit": "wabiyaʿun"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَصَلَوَٰتٌ", "translit": "ٌtَٰwَlَṣَw", "root": "صلو", "latinTranslit": "waṣalawātun"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "وَمَسَٰجِدُ", "translit": "ُdِjgَٰsَmَw", "root": "سجد", "latinTranslit": "wamasājidu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "يُذْكَرُ", "translit": "ُrَkْhdُy", "root": "ذكر", "latinTranslit": "yudh'karu"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱسْمُ", "translit": "ُmْsٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "us'mu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "كَثِيرًا", "translit": "āًryِhtَk", "root": "كثر", "latinTranslit": "kathīran"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَلَيَنصُرَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَrُṣnَyَlَw", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "walayanṣuranna"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "man"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "يَنصُرُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُrُṣnَy", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "yanṣuruhu"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "لَقَوِىٌّ", "translit": "ٌّàِwَqَl", "root": "قوي", "latinTranslit": "laqawiyyun"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "عَزِيزٌ", "translit": "ٌzyِzَع", "root": "عزز", "latinTranslit": "ʿazīzun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "those who have been driven unjustly from their homes only for saying, ‘Our Lord is God.’ If God did not repel some people by means of others, many monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques, where God’s name is much invoked, would have been destroyed. God is sure to help those who help His cause- God is strong and mighty", "clearyTranslation": "those who have been driven unjustly from their homes only for saying, ‘Our Lord is God.’ If God did not repel some people by means of others, many monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques, where God’s name is much invoked, would have been destroyed. God is sure to help those who help His cause- God is strong and mighty"}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ إِن مَّكَّنَّٰهُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ أَقَامُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَءَاتَوُا۟ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَأَمَرُوا۟ بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ وَنَهَوْا۟ عَنِ ٱلْمُنكَرِۗ وَلِلَّهِ عَٰقِبَةُ ٱلْأُمُورِ ", "translation": "[And they are] those who, if We give them authority in the land, establish prayer and give zakāh and enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong. And to Allāh belongs the outcome of [all] matters.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "in"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّكَّنَّٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّٰnَّkَّm", "root": "مكن", "latinTranslit": "makkannāhum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَقَامُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmāَqَaʾ", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "aqāmū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣalata"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَءَاتَوُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwَtāَءَw", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "waātawū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَkَّzlٱ", "root": "زكو", "latinTranslit": "l-zakata"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَأَمَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَmَaʾَw", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "wa-amarū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ", "translit": "ِfwُrْعَmْlٱِb", "root": "عرف", "latinTranslit": "bil-maʿrūfi"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَنَهَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَhَnَw", "root": "نهي", "latinTranslit": "wanahaw"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿani"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنكَرِ", "translit": "ِrَknُmْlٱ", "root": "نكر", "latinTranslit": "l-munkari"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَلِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِlَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "walillahi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَٰقِبَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَbِqَٰع", "root": "عقب", "latinTranslit": "ʿāqibatu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأُمُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُmُaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "l-umūri"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "those who, when We establish them in the land, keep up the prayer, pay the prescribed alms, command what is right, and forbid what is wrong: God controls the outcome of all events", "clearyTranslation": "those who, when We establish them in the land, keep up the prayer, pay the prescribed alms, command what is right, and forbid what is wrong: God controls the outcome of all events"}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "وَإِن يُكَذِّبُوكَ فَقَدْ كَذَّبَتْ قَبْلَهُمْ قَوْمُ نُوحٍ وَعَادٌ وَثَمُودُ ", "translation": "And if they deny you, [O Muḥammad] - so, before them, did the people of Noah and ʿAad and Thamūd deny [their prophets],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُكَذِّبُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُbِّhdَkُy", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "yukadhibūka"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "faqad"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَذَّبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "kadhabat"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَبْلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlْbَq", "root": "قبل", "latinTranslit": "qablahum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَq", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "qawmu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "nūḥin"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَعَادٌ", "translit": "ٌdāَعَw", "root": "عود", "latinTranslit": "waʿādun"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَثَمُودُ", "translit": "ُdwُmَhtَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wathamūdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "If they reject you [Prophet], so did the people of Noah before them, and those of Ad, Thamud", "clearyTranslation": "If they reject you [Prophet], so did the people of Noah before them, and those of Ad, Thamud"}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "وَقَوْمُ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَقَوْمُ لُوطٍ ", "translation": "And the people of Abraham and the people of Lot", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَqَw", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "waqawmu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ib'rāhīma"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَقَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَqَw", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "waqawmu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لُوطٍ", "translit": "ٍṭwُl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lūṭin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Abraham, Lot", "clearyTranslation": "Abraham, Lot"}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "وَأَصْحَٰبُ مَدْيَنَۖ وَكُذِّبَ مُوسَىٰ فَأَمْلَيْتُ لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ ثُمَّ أَخَذْتُهُمْۖ فَكَيْفَ كَانَ نَكِيرِ ", "translation": "And the inhabitants of Madyan. And Moses was denied, so I prolonged enjoyment for the disbelievers; then I seized them, and how [terrible] was My reproach.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَصْحَٰبُ", "translit": "ُbَٰḥْṣَaʾَw", "root": "صحب", "latinTranslit": "wa-aṣḥābu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَدْيَنَ", "translit": "َnَyْdَm", "root": "مدن", "latinTranslit": "madyana"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَكُذِّبَ", "translit": "َbِّhdُkَw", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "wakudhiba"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mūsā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَأَمْلَيْتُ", "translit": "ُtْyَlْmَaʾَf", "root": "ملو", "latinTranslit": "fa-amlaytu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِلْكَٰفِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِfَٰkْlِl", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "lil'kāfirīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَخَذْتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ", "latinTranslit": "akhadhtuhum"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَكَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَkَf", "root": "كيف", "latinTranslit": "fakayfa"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "kāna"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "نَكِيرِ", "translit": "ِryِkَn", "root": "نكر", "latinTranslit": "nakīri"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Midian. Moses too was called a liar. I gave the disbelievers time, but in the end I punished them. How I condemned them", "clearyTranslation": "Midian. Moses too was called a liar. I gave the disbelievers time, but in the end I punished them. How I condemned them"}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "فَكَأَيِّن مِّن قَرْيَةٍ أَهْلَكْنَٰهَا وَهِىَ ظَالِمَةٌ فَهِىَ خَاوِيَةٌ عَلَىٰ عُرُوشِهَا وَبِئْرٍ مُّعَطَّلَةٍ وَقَصْرٍ مَّشِيدٍ ", "translation": "And how many a city did We destroy while it was committing wrong - so it is [now] fallen into ruin[1] - and [how many] an abandoned well and [how many] a lofty palace.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَأَيِّن", "translit": "nِّyَaʾَkَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "faka-ayyin"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري", "latinTranslit": "qaryatin"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك", "latinTranslit": "ahlaknāhā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَهِىَ", "translit": "َàِhَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wahiya"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ظَالِمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmِlāَẓ", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "ẓālimatun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَهِىَ", "translit": "َàِhَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fahiya"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَاوِيَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَyِwāَhk", "root": "خوي", "latinTranslit": "khāwiyatun"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عُرُوشِهَا", "translit": "āَhِhswُrُع", "root": "عرش", "latinTranslit": "ʿurūshihā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَبِئْرٍ", "translit": "ٍrْyʾِbَw", "root": "بار", "latinTranslit": "wabi'rin"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُّعَطَّلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlَّṭَعُّm", "root": "عطل", "latinTranslit": "muʿaṭṭalatin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَقَصْرٍ", "translit": "ٍrْṣَqَw", "root": "قصر", "latinTranslit": "waqaṣrin"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مَّشِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِhsَّm", "root": "شيد", "latinTranslit": "mashīdin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "How many towns steeped in wrongdoing We have destroyed and left in total ruin; how many deserted wells; how many lofty palaces", "clearyTranslation": "How many towns steeped in wrongdoing We have destroyed and left in total ruin; how many deserted wells; how many lofty palaces"}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "أَفَلَمْ يَسِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَتَكُونَ لَهُمْ قُلُوبٌ يَعْقِلُونَ بِهَآ أَوْ ءَاذَانٌ يَسْمَعُونَ بِهَاۖ فَإِنَّهَا لَا تَعْمَى ٱلْأَبْصَٰرُ وَلَٰكِن تَعْمَى ٱلْقُلُوبُ ٱلَّتِى فِى ٱلصُّدُورِ ", "translation": "So have they not traveled through the earth and have hearts by which to reason and ears by which to hear? For indeed, it is not eyes that are blinded, but blinded are the hearts which are within the breasts.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَfَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "afalam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسِيرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُryِsَy", "root": "سير", "latinTranslit": "yasīrū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَتَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَtَf", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "fatakūna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قُلُوبٌ", "translit": "ٌbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَعْقِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِqْعَy", "root": "عقل", "latinTranslit": "yaʿqilūna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "aw"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ءَاذَانٌ", "translit": "ٌnāَhdāَء", "root": "اذن", "latinTranslit": "ādhānun"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع", "latinTranslit": "yasmaʿūna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَإِنَّهَا", "translit": "āَhَّnِiʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-innahā"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "تَعْمَى", "translit": "àَmْعَt", "root": "عمي", "latinTranslit": "taʿmā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْأَبْصَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰṣْbَaʾْlٱ", "root": "بصر", "latinTranslit": "l-abṣāru"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walākin"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "تَعْمَى", "translit": "àَmْعَt", "root": "عمي", "latinTranslit": "taʿmā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْقُلُوبُ", "translit": "ُbwُlُqْlٱ", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "l-qulūbu"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلَّتِى", "translit": "àِtَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "allatī"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلصُّدُورِ", "translit": "ِrwُdُّṣlٱ", "root": "صدر", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣudūri"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Have these people [of Mecca] not travelled through the land with hearts to understand and ears to hear? It is not people’s eyes that are blind, but their hearts within their breasts", "clearyTranslation": "Have these people [of Mecca] not travelled through the land with hearts to understand and ears to hear? It is not people’s eyes that are blind, but their hearts within their breasts"}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ بِٱلْعَذَابِ وَلَن يُخْلِفَ ٱللَّهُ وَعْدَهُۥۚ وَإِنَّ يَوْمًا عِندَ رَبِّكَ كَأَلْفِ سَنَةٍ مِّمَّا تَعُدُّونَ ", "translation": "And they urge you to hasten the punishment. But Allāh will never fail in His promise. And indeed, a day with your Lord is like a thousand years of those which you count.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَسْتَعْجِلُونَكَ", "translit": "َkَnwُlِjgْعَtْsَyَw", "root": "عجل", "latinTranslit": "wayastaʿjilūnaka"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَعْlٱِb", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "bil-ʿadhābi"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَن", "translit": "nَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walan"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُخْلِفَ", "translit": "َfِlْhkُy", "root": "خلف", "latinTranslit": "yukh'lifa"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَعْدَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdْعَw", "root": "وعد", "latinTranslit": "waʿdahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَوْمًا", "translit": "āًmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawman"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿinda"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbika"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كَأَلْفِ", "translit": "ِfْlَaʾَk", "root": "الف", "latinTranslit": "ka-alfi"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَنَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَnَs", "root": "سنو", "latinTranslit": "sanatin"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِّمَّا", "translit": "āَّmِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mimmā"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تَعُدُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّdُعَt", "root": "عدد", "latinTranslit": "taʿuddūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "They will challenge you [Prophet] to hasten the punishment. God will not fail in His promise- a Day with your Lord is like a thousand years by your reckoning", "clearyTranslation": "They will challenge you [Prophet] to hasten the punishment. God will not fail in His promise- a Day with your Lord is like a thousand years by your reckoning"}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "وَكَأَيِّن مِّن قَرْيَةٍ أَمْلَيْتُ لَهَا وَهِىَ ظَالِمَةٌ ثُمَّ أَخَذْتُهَا وَإِلَىَّ ٱلْمَصِيرُ ", "translation": "And for how many a city did I prolong enjoyment while it was committing wrong. Then I seized it, and to Me is the [final] destination.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَأَيِّن", "translit": "nِّyَaʾَkَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waka-ayyin"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري", "latinTranslit": "qaryatin"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَمْلَيْتُ", "translit": "ُtْyَlْmَaʾ", "root": "ملو", "latinTranslit": "amlaytu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَهِىَ", "translit": "َàِhَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wahiya"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ظَالِمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmِlāَẓ", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "ẓālimatun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَخَذْتُهَا", "translit": "āَhُtْhdَhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ", "latinTranslit": "akhadhtuhā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِلَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَlِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-ilayya"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْمَصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَmْlٱ", "root": "صير", "latinTranslit": "l-maṣīru"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "To many a town steeped in wrongdoing I gave more time and then struck them down: they all return to Me in the end", "clearyTranslation": "To many a town steeped in wrongdoing I gave more time and then struck them down: they all return to Me in the end"}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "قُلْ يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ إِنَّمَآ أَنَا۠ لَكُمْ نَذِيرٌ مُّبِينٌ ", "translation": "Say, \"O people, I am only to you a clear warner.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِنَّمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "innamā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "نَذِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِhdَn", "root": "نذر", "latinTranslit": "nadhīrun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّبِينٌ", "translit": "ٌnyِbُّm", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "mubīnun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Say [Prophet], ‘People, I am sent only to give you clear warning.’", "clearyTranslation": "Say [Prophet], ‘People, I am sent only to give you clear warning.’"}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "فَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ لَهُم مَّغْفِرَةٌ وَرِزْقٌ كَرِيمٌ ", "translation": "And those who have believed and done righteous deeds - for them is forgiveness and noble provision.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَعَw", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "waʿamilū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣāliḥāti"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّغْفِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِfْغَّm", "root": "غفر", "latinTranslit": "maghfiratun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَرِزْقٌ", "translit": "ٌqْzِrَw", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "wariz'qun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَرِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِrَk", "root": "كرم", "latinTranslit": "karīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Those who believe and do good deeds will be forgiven and have a generous reward", "clearyTranslation": "Those who believe and do good deeds will be forgiven and have a generous reward"}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ سَعَوْا۟ فِىٓ ءَايَٰتِنَا مُعَٰجِزِينَ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ أَصْحَٰبُ ٱلْجَحِيمِ ", "translation": "But the ones who strove against Our verses, [seeking] to cause failure[1] - those are the companions of Hellfire.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَعَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَعَs", "root": "سعي", "latinTranslit": "saʿaw"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "āyātinā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُعَٰجِزِينَ", "translit": "َnyِzِjgَٰعُm", "root": "عجز", "latinTranslit": "muʿājizīna"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَصْحَٰبُ", "translit": "ُbَٰḥْṣَaʾ", "root": "صحب", "latinTranslit": "aṣḥābu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْجَحِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِḥَjgْlٱ", "root": "جحم", "latinTranslit": "l-jaḥīmi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "but those who strive to oppose Our messages and try in vain to defeat Us are destined for the Blaze", "clearyTranslation": "but those who strive to oppose Our messages and try in vain to defeat Us are destined for the Blaze"}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَا مِن قَبْلِكَ مِن رَّسُولٍ وَلَا نَبِىٍّ إِلَّآ إِذَا تَمَنَّىٰٓ أَلْقَى ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ فِىٓ أُمْنِيَّتِهِۦ فَيَنسَخُ ٱللَّهُ مَا يُلْقِى ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ ثُمَّ يُحْكِمُ ٱللَّهُ ءَايَٰتِهِۦۗ وَٱللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ ", "translation": "And We did not send before you any messenger or prophet except that when he spoke [or recited], Satan threw into it [some misunderstanding]. But Allāh abolishes that which Satan throws in; then Allāh makes precise His verses.[1] And Allāh is Knowing and Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "arsalnā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَبْلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlْbَq", "root": "قبل", "latinTranslit": "qablika"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَّسُولٍ", "translit": "ٍlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "rasūlin"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَبِىٍّ", "translit": "ٍّàِbَn", "root": "نبا", "latinTranslit": "nabiyyin"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "idhā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَمَنَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّnَmَt", "root": "مني", "latinTranslit": "tamannā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَلْقَى", "translit": "àَqْlَaʾ", "root": "لقي", "latinTranslit": "alqā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن", "latinTranslit": "l-shayṭānu"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أُمْنِيَّتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَّyِnْmُaʾ", "root": "مني", "latinTranslit": "um'niyyatihi"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "فَيَنسَخُ", "translit": "ُhkَsnَyَf", "root": "نسخ", "latinTranslit": "fayansakhu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يُلْقِى", "translit": "àِqْlُy", "root": "لقي", "latinTranslit": "yul'qī"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن", "latinTranslit": "l-shayṭānu"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "يُحْكِمُ", "translit": "ُmِkْḥُy", "root": "حكم", "latinTranslit": "yuḥ'kimu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "āyātihi"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "wal-lahu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "عَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿalīmun"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "حَكِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِkَḥ", "root": "حكم", "latinTranslit": "ḥakīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "We have never sent any messenger or prophet before you [Muhammad] into whose wishes Satan did not insinuate something, but God removes what Satan insinuates and then God affirms His message. God is all knowing and wise", "clearyTranslation": "We have never sent any messenger or prophet before you [Muhammad] into whose wishes Satan did not insinuate something, but God removes what Satan insinuates and then God affirms His message. God is all knowing and wise"}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "لِّيَجْعَلَ مَا يُلْقِى ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ فِتْنَةً لِّلَّذِينَ فِى قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌ وَٱلْقَاسِيَةِ قُلُوبُهُمْۗ وَإِنَّ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ لَفِى شِقَاقٍۭ بَعِيدٍ ", "translation": "[That is] so He may make what Satan throws in [i.e., asserts] a trial for those within whose hearts is disease[1] and those hard of heart. And indeed, the wrongdoers are in extreme dissension.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِّيَجْعَلَ", "translit": "َlَعْjgَyِّl", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "liyajʿala"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُلْقِى", "translit": "àِqْlُy", "root": "لقي", "latinTranslit": "yul'qī"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰṭْyَّhslٱ", "root": "شطن", "latinTranslit": "l-shayṭānu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِتْنَةً", "translit": "ًẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن", "latinTranslit": "fit'natan"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِّلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlِّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lilladhīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قُلُوبِهِم", "translit": "mِhِbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbihim"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّرَضٌ", "translit": "ٌḍَrَّm", "root": "مرض", "latinTranslit": "maraḍun"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَاسِيَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَyِsāَqْlٱَw", "root": "قسو", "latinTranslit": "wal-qāsiyati"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbuhum"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "l-ẓālimīna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَفِى", "translit": "àِfَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lafī"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شِقَاقٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭqāَqِhs", "root": "شقق", "latinTranslit": "shiqāqin"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بَعِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِعَb", "root": "بعد", "latinTranslit": "baʿīdin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "He makes Satan’s insinuations a temptation only for the sick at heart and those whose hearts are hardened- the evildoers are profoundly opposed [to the Truth]", "clearyTranslation": "He makes Satan’s insinuations a temptation only for the sick at heart and those whose hearts are hardened- the evildoers are profoundly opposed [to the Truth]"}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "وَلِيَعْلَمَ ٱلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْعِلْمَ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ مِن رَّبِّكَ فَيُؤْمِنُوا۟ بِهِۦ فَتُخْبِتَ لَهُۥ قُلُوبُهُمْۗ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَهَادِ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍ مُّسْتَقِيمٍ ", "translation": "And so those who were given knowledge may know that it is the truth from your Lord and [therefore] believe in it, and their hearts humbly submit to it. And indeed is Allāh the Guide of those who have believed to a straight path.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلِيَعْلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlْعَyِlَw", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "waliyaʿlama"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أُوتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "ūtū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمَ", "translit": "َmْlِعْlٱ", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿil'ma"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "annahu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥaqu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "رَّبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَّr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbika"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَيُؤْمِنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnِmْwʾُyَf", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "fayu'minū"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فَتُخْبِتَ", "translit": "َtِbْhkُtَf", "root": "خبت", "latinTranslit": "fatukh'bita"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "قُلُوبُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُbwُlُq", "root": "قلب", "latinTranslit": "qulūbuhum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَهَادِ", "translit": "ِdāَhَl", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "lahādi"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "صِرَٰطٍ", "translit": "ٍṭَٰrِṣ", "root": "صرط", "latinTranslit": "ṣirāṭin"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "mus'taqīmin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "and He causes those given knowledge to realize that this Revelation is your Lord’s Truth, so that they may believe in it and humble their hearts to Him: God guides the faithful to the straight path", "clearyTranslation": "and He causes those given knowledge to realize that this Revelation is your Lord’s Truth, so that they may believe in it and humble their hearts to Him: God guides the faithful to the straight path"}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "وَلَا يَزَالُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ فِى مِرْيَةٍ مِّنْهُ حَتَّىٰ تَأْتِيَهُمُ ٱلسَّاعَةُ بَغْتَةً أَوْ يَأْتِيَهُمْ عَذَابُ يَوْمٍ عَقِيمٍ ", "translation": "But those who disbelieve will not cease to be in doubt of it until the Hour comes upon them unexpectedly or there comes to them the punishment of a barren Day.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَزَالُ", "translit": "ُlāَzَy", "root": "زيل", "latinTranslit": "yazālu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrِm", "root": "مري", "latinTranslit": "mir'yatin"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِّنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ḥattā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَأْتِيَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَyِtْaʾَt", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "tatiyahumu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع", "latinTranslit": "l-sāʿatu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَغْتَةً", "translit": "ًẗَtْغَb", "root": "بغت", "latinTranslit": "baghtatan"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "aw"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَأْتِيَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَyِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "yatiyahum"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَذَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhābu"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَوْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawmin"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَقِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِqَع", "root": "عقم", "latinTranslit": "ʿaqīmin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "The disbelievers will remain in doubt about it until the Hour suddenly overpowers them or until torment descends on them on a Day devoid of all hope", "clearyTranslation": "The disbelievers will remain in doubt about it until the Hour suddenly overpowers them or until torment descends on them on a Day devoid of all hope"}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "ٱلْمُلْكُ يَوْمَئِذٍ لِّلَّهِ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْۚ فَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ فِى جَنَّٰتِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ ", "translation": "[All] sovereignty that Day is for Allāh;[1] He will judge between them. So they who believed and did righteous deeds will be in the Gardens of Pleasure.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلْمُلْكُ", "translit": "ُkْlُmْlٱ", "root": "ملك", "latinTranslit": "al-mul'ku"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawma-idhin"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِّلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِّl", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "lillahi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَحْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْḥَy", "root": "حكم", "latinTranslit": "yaḥkumu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "baynahum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَعَw", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "waʿamilū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣāliḥāti"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن", "latinTranslit": "jannāti"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلنَّعِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِعَّnlٱ", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "l-naʿīmi"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "On that Day control will belong to God: He will judge between them. Those who believe and do good deeds will be admitted to Gardens of Delight", "clearyTranslation": "On that Day control will belong to God: He will judge between them. Those who believe and do good deeds will be admitted to Gardens of Delight"}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَكَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ لَهُمْ عَذَابٌ مُّهِينٌ ", "translation": "And they who disbelieved and denied Our signs - for those there will be a humiliating punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَكَذَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّhdَkَw", "root": "كذب", "latinTranslit": "wakadhabū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "biāyātinā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fa-ulāika"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَع", "root": "عذب", "latinTranslit": "ʿadhābun"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّهِينٌ", "translit": "ٌnyِhُّm", "root": "هون", "latinTranslit": "muhīnun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "while those who disbelieve and reject Our revelations will receive a humiliating torment", "clearyTranslation": "while those who disbelieve and reject Our revelations will receive a humiliating torment"}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "وَٱلَّذِينَ هَاجَرُوا۟ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ثُمَّ قُتِلُوٓا۟ أَوْ مَاتُوا۟ لَيَرْزُقَنَّهُمُ ٱللَّهُ رِزْقًا حَسَنًاۚ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَهُوَ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ ", "translation": "And those who emigrated for the cause of Allāh and then were killed or died - Allāh will surely provide for them a good provision. And indeed, it is Allāh who is the best of providers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-alladhīna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَاجَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَjgāَh", "root": "هجر", "latinTranslit": "hājarū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل", "latinTranslit": "sabīli"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قُتِلُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُlِtُq", "root": "قتل", "latinTranslit": "qutilū"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "aw"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَاتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtāَm", "root": "موت", "latinTranslit": "mātū"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَيَرْزُقَنَّهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَّnَqُzْrَyَl", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "layarzuqannahumu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رِزْقًا", "translit": "āًqْzِr", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "riz'qan"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "حَسَنًا", "translit": "āًnَsَḥ", "root": "حسن", "latinTranslit": "ḥasanan"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahuwa"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayru"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِzَّٰrlٱ", "root": "رزق", "latinTranslit": "l-rāziqīna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "He will give a generous provision to those who migrated in God’s way and were killed or died. He is the Best Provider", "clearyTranslation": "He will give a generous provision to those who migrated in God’s way and were killed or died. He is the Best Provider"}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "لَيُدْخِلَنَّهُم مُّدْخَلًا يَرْضَوْنَهُۥۗ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَعَلِيمٌ حَلِيمٌ ", "translation": "He will surely cause them to enter an entrance with which they will be pleased, and indeed, Allāh is Knowing and Forbearing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَيُدْخِلَنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnَlِhkْdُyَl", "root": "دخل", "latinTranslit": "layud'khilannahum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُّدْخَلًا", "translit": "āًlَhkْdُّm", "root": "دخل", "latinTranslit": "mud'khalan"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَرْضَوْنَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnْwَḍْrَy", "root": "رضو", "latinTranslit": "yarḍawnahu"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَعَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَعَl", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "laʿalīmun"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَلِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِlَḥ", "root": "حلم", "latinTranslit": "ḥalīmun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "He will admit them to a place that will please them: God is all knowing and most forbearing", "clearyTranslation": "He will admit them to a place that will please them: God is all knowing and most forbearing"}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ وَمَنْ عَاقَبَ بِمِثْلِ مَا عُوقِبَ بِهِۦ ثُمَّ بُغِىَ عَلَيْهِ لَيَنصُرَنَّهُ ٱللَّهُۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَعَفُوٌّ غَفُورٌ ", "translation": "That [is so]. And whoever responds [to injustice] with the equivalent of that with which he was harmed and then is tyrannized - Allāh will surely aid him. Indeed, Allāh is Pardoning and Forgiving.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "waman"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَاقَبَ", "translit": "َbَqāَع", "root": "عقب", "latinTranslit": "ʿāqaba"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمِثْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhtِmِb", "root": "مثل", "latinTranslit": "bimith'li"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عُوقِبَ", "translit": "َbِqwُع", "root": "عقب", "latinTranslit": "ʿūqiba"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بُغِىَ", "translit": "َàِغُb", "root": "بغي", "latinTranslit": "bughiya"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhi"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَيَنصُرَنَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّnَrُṣnَyَl", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "layanṣurannahu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَعَفُوٌّ", "translit": "ٌّwُfَعَl", "root": "عفو", "latinTranslit": "laʿafuwwun"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "غَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَغ", "root": "غفر", "latinTranslit": "ghafūrun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "So it will be. God will help those who retaliate against an aggressive act merely with its like and are then wronged again: God is pardoning and most forgiving", "clearyTranslation": "So it will be. God will help those who retaliate against an aggressive act merely with its like and are then wronged again: God is pardoning and most forgiving"}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُولِجُ ٱلَّيْلَ فِى ٱلنَّهَارِ وَيُولِجُ ٱلنَّهَارَ فِى ٱلَّيْلِ وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَمِيعٌۢ بَصِيرٌ ", "translation": "That[1] is because Allāh causes the night to enter the day and causes the day to enter the night and because Allāh is Hearing and Seeing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bi-anna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُولِجُ", "translit": "ُjgِlwُy", "root": "ولج", "latinTranslit": "yūliju"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلَ", "translit": "َlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل", "latinTranslit": "al-layla"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱ", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "l-nahāri"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَيُولِجُ", "translit": "ُjgِlwُyَw", "root": "ولج", "latinTranslit": "wayūliju"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلنَّهَارَ", "translit": "َrāَhَّnlٱ", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "l-nahāra"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل", "latinTranslit": "al-layli"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "سَمِيعٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢعyِmَs", "root": "سمع", "latinTranslit": "samīʿun"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بَصِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِṣَb", "root": "بصر", "latinTranslit": "baṣīrun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "So it will be, because God makes night pass into day, and day into night, and He is all hearing and all seeing", "clearyTranslation": "So it will be, because God makes night pass into day, and day into night, and He is all hearing and all seeing"}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلْحَقُّ وَأَنَّ مَا يَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِهِۦ هُوَ ٱلْبَٰطِلُ وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ هُوَ ٱلْعَلِىُّ ٱلْكَبِيرُ ", "translation": "That is because Allāh is the True Reality, and that which they call upon other than Him is falsehood, and because Allāh is the Most High, the Grand.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bi-anna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقُّ", "translit": "ُّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥaqu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُعْdَy", "root": "دعو", "latinTranslit": "yadʿūna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "دُونِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِnwُd", "root": "دون", "latinTranslit": "dūnihi"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْبَٰطِلُ", "translit": "ُlِṭَٰbْlٱ", "root": "بطل", "latinTranslit": "l-bāṭilu"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-anna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْعَلِىُّ", "translit": "ُّàِlَعْlٱ", "root": "علو", "latinTranslit": "l-ʿaliyu"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْكَبِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِbَkْlٱ", "root": "كبر", "latinTranslit": "l-kabīru"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "So it will be, because it is God alone who is the Truth, and whatever else they invoke is sheer falsehood: it is God who is the Most High, the Most Great", "clearyTranslation": "So it will be, because it is God alone who is the Truth, and whatever else they invoke is sheer falsehood: it is God who is the Most High, the Most Great"}, {"ayah": 63, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ أَنزَلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءً فَتُصْبِحُ ٱلْأَرْضُ مُخْضَرَّةًۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَطِيفٌ خَبِيرٌ ", "translation": "Do you not see that Allāh has sent down rain from the sky and the earth becomes green? Indeed, Allāh is Subtle[1] and Aware.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "tara"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل", "latinTranslit": "anzala"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَآءً", "translit": "ًءٓāَm", "root": "موه", "latinTranslit": "māan"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَتُصْبِحُ", "translit": "ُḥِbْṣُtَf", "root": "صبح", "latinTranslit": "fatuṣ'biḥu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مُخْضَرَّةً", "translit": "ًẗَّrَḍْhkُm", "root": "خضر", "latinTranslit": "mukh'ḍarratan"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَطِيفٌ", "translit": "ٌfyِṭَl", "root": "لطف", "latinTranslit": "laṭīfun"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "خَبِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِbَhk", "root": "خبر", "latinTranslit": "khabīrun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Have you [Prophet] not considered how God sends water down from the sky and the next morning the earth becomes green? God is truly most subtle, all aware", "clearyTranslation": "Have you [Prophet] not considered how God sends water down from the sky and the next morning the earth becomes green? God is truly most subtle, all aware"}, {"ayah": 64, "text": "لَّهُۥ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۗ وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَهُوَ ٱلْغَنِىُّ ٱلْحَمِيدُ ", "translation": "To Him belongs what is in the heavens and what is on the earth. And indeed, Allāh is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāwāti"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-inna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahuwa"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْغَنِىُّ", "translit": "ُّàِnَغْlٱ", "root": "غني", "latinTranslit": "l-ghaniyu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْحَمِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِmَḥْlٱ", "root": "حمد", "latinTranslit": "l-ḥamīdu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "everything in the heavens and earth belongs to Him; God alone is self-sufficient, worthy of all praise", "clearyTranslation": "everything in the heavens and earth belongs to Him; God alone is self-sufficient, worthy of all praise"}, {"ayah": 65, "text": "أَلَمْ تَرَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَخَّرَ لَكُم مَّا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَٱلْفُلْكَ تَجْرِى فِى ٱلْبَحْرِ بِأَمْرِهِۦ وَيُمْسِكُ ٱلسَّمَآءَ أَن تَقَعَ عَلَى ٱلْأَرْضِ إِلَّا بِإِذْنِهِۦٓۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ بِٱلنَّاسِ لَرَءُوفٌ رَّحِيمٌ ", "translation": "Do you not see that Allāh has subjected to you whatever is on the earth and the ships which run through the sea by His command? And He restrains the sky from falling upon the earth, unless by His permission. Indeed Allāh, to the people, is Kind and Merciful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَرَ", "translit": "َrَt", "root": "راي", "latinTranslit": "tara"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَs", "root": "سخر", "latinTranslit": "sakhara"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lakum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱلْفُلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlُfْlٱَw", "root": "فلك", "latinTranslit": "wal-ful'ka"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري", "latinTranslit": "tajrī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلْبَحْرِ", "translit": "ِrْḥَbْlٱ", "root": "بحر", "latinTranslit": "l-baḥri"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِأَمْرِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrْmَaʾِb", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "bi-amrihi"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَيُمْسِكُ", "translit": "ُkِsْmُyَw", "root": "مسك", "latinTranslit": "wayum'siku"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāa"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "an"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "تَقَعَ", "translit": "َعَqَt", "root": "وقع", "latinTranslit": "taqaʿa"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "l-arḍi"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِإِذْنِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِnْhdِiʾِb", "root": "اذن", "latinTranslit": "bi-idh'nihi"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بِٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱِb", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "bil-nāsi"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "لَرَءُوفٌ", "translit": "ٌfwُءَrَl", "root": "راف", "latinTranslit": "laraūfun"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "رَّحِيمٌ", "translit": "ٌmyِḥَّr", "root": "رحم", "latinTranslit": "raḥīmun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Have you not considered how God has made everything on the earth of service to you? That ships sail the sea at His command? That He keeps the heavens from falling down on the earth without His permission? God is most compassionate and most merciful to mankind", "clearyTranslation": "Have you not considered how God has made everything on the earth of service to you? That ships sail the sea at His command? That He keeps the heavens from falling down on the earth without His permission? God is most compassionate and most merciful to mankind"}, {"ayah": 66, "text": "وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَحْيَاكُمْ ثُمَّ يُمِيتُكُمْ ثُمَّ يُحْيِيكُمْۗ إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لَكَفُورٌ ", "translation": "And He is the one who gave you life; then He causes you to die and then will [again] give you life. Indeed, mankind is ungrateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wahuwa"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَحْيَاكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkāَyْḥَaʾ", "root": "حيي", "latinTranslit": "aḥyākum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُمِيتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtyِmُy", "root": "موت", "latinTranslit": "yumītukum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "thumma"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُحْيِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِyْḥُy", "root": "حيي", "latinTranslit": "yuḥ'yīkum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-insāna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَكَفُورٌ", "translit": "ٌrwُfَkَl", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "lakafūrun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "it is He who gave you [people] life, will cause you to die, then will give you life again- but man is ungrateful", "clearyTranslation": "it is He who gave you [people] life, will cause you to die, then will give you life again- but man is ungrateful"}, {"ayah": 67, "text": "لِّكُلِّ أُمَّةٍ جَعَلْنَا مَنسَكًا هُمْ نَاسِكُوهُۖ فَلَا يُنَٰزِعُنَّكَ فِى ٱلْأَمْرِۚ وَٱدْعُ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَۖ إِنَّكَ لَعَلَىٰ هُدًى مُّسْتَقِيمٍ ", "translation": "For every [religious] community We have appointed rites which they perform. So, [O Muḥammad], let them [i.e., the disbelievers] not contend with you over the matter but invite [them] to your Lord. Indeed, you are upon straight guidance.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِّكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِّl", "root": "كلل", "latinTranslit": "likulli"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُمَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم", "latinTranslit": "ummatin"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَعَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَعَjg", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "jaʿalnā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَنسَكًا", "translit": "āًkَsnَm", "root": "نسك", "latinTranslit": "mansakan"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hum"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نَاسِكُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُkِsāَn", "root": "نسك", "latinTranslit": "nāsikūhu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "falā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُنَٰزِعُنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnُعِzَٰnُy", "root": "نزع", "latinTranslit": "yunāziʿunnaka"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "l-amri"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَٱدْعُ", "translit": "ُعْdٱَw", "root": "دعو", "latinTranslit": "wa-ud'ʿu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ilā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbika"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "innaka"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لَعَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَعَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "laʿalā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "هُدًى", "translit": "àًdُh", "root": "هدي", "latinTranslit": "hudan"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "mus'taqīmin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "We have appointed acts of devotion for every community to observe, so do not let them argue with you [Prophet] about this matter. Call them to your Lord- you are on the right path", "clearyTranslation": "We have appointed acts of devotion for every community to observe, so do not let them argue with you [Prophet] about this matter. Call them to your Lord- you are on the right path"}, {"ayah": 68, "text": "وَإِن جَٰدَلُوكَ فَقُلِ ٱللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ ", "translation": "And if they dispute with you, then say, \"Allāh is most knowing of what you do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَٰدَلُوكَ", "translit": "َkwُlَdَٰjg", "root": "جدل", "latinTranslit": "jādalūka"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَقُلِ", "translit": "ِlُqَf", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "faquli"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْعَaʾ", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "aʿlamu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bimā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تَعْمَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَmْعَt", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "taʿmalūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "and if they argue with you, say, ‘God is well aware of what you are doing.’", "clearyTranslation": "and if they argue with you, say, ‘God is well aware of what you are doing.’"}, {"ayah": 69, "text": "ٱللَّهُ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَكُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ فِيمَا كُنتُمْ فِيهِ تَخْتَلِفُونَ ", "translation": "Allāh will judge between you on the Day of Resurrection concerning that over which you used to differ.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "al-lahu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَحْكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkْḥَy", "root": "حكم", "latinTranslit": "yaḥkumu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَيْنَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "baynakum"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم", "latinTranslit": "yawma"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "l-qiyāmati"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِيمَا", "translit": "āَmyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīmā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "kuntum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِيهِ", "translit": "ِhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَخْتَلِفُونَ", "translit": "َnwُfِlَtْhkَt", "root": "خلف", "latinTranslit": "takhtalifūna"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "On the Day of Resurrection, God will judge between you regarding your differences", "clearyTranslation": "On the Day of Resurrection, God will judge between you regarding your differences"}, {"ayah": 70, "text": "أَلَمْ تَعْلَمْ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ وَٱلْأَرْضِۗ إِنَّ ذَٰلِكَ فِى كِتَٰبٍۚ إِنَّ ذَٰلِكَ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ يَسِيرٌ ", "translation": "Do you not know that Allāh knows what is in the heaven and earth? Indeed, that is in a Record.[1] Indeed that, for Allāh, is easy.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَعْلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlْعَt", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "taʿlam"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "anna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْعَy", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "yaʿlamu"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "l-samāi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض", "latinTranslit": "wal-arḍi"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كِتَٰبٍ", "translit": "ٍbَٰtِk", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "kitābin"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَسِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِsَy", "root": "يسر", "latinTranslit": "yasīrun"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Are you [Prophet] not aware that God knows all that is in the heavens and earth? All this is written in a Record; this is easy for God", "clearyTranslation": "Are you [Prophet] not aware that God knows all that is in the heavens and earth? All this is written in a Record; this is easy for God"}, {"ayah": 71, "text": "وَيَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مَا لَمْ يُنَزِّلْ بِهِۦ سُلْطَٰنًا وَمَا لَيْسَ لَهُم بِهِۦ عِلْمٌۗ وَمَا لِلظَّٰلِمِينَ مِن نَّصِيرٍ ", "translation": "And they worship besides Allāh that for which He has not sent down authority and that of which they have no knowledge. And there will not be for the wrongdoers any helper.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَعْبُدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdُbْعَyَw", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "wayaʿbudūna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون", "latinTranslit": "dūni"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lam"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يُنَزِّلْ", "translit": "ْlِّzَnُy", "root": "نزل", "latinTranslit": "yunazzil"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنًا", "translit": "āًnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط", "latinTranslit": "sul'ṭānan"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس", "latinTranslit": "laysa"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahum"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bihi"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عِلْمٌ", "translit": "ٌmْlِع", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "ʿil'mun"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlِl", "root": "ظلم", "latinTranslit": "lilẓẓālimīna"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "نَّصِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِṣَّn", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "naṣīrin"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Yet beside God they serve that for which He has sent no authority and of which they have no knowledge: the evildoers will have no one to help them", "clearyTranslation": "Yet beside God they serve that for which He has sent no authority and of which they have no knowledge: the evildoers will have no one to help them"}, {"ayah": 72, "text": "وَإِذَا تُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْ ءَايَٰتُنَا بَيِّنَٰتٍ تَعْرِفُ فِى وُجُوهِ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ ٱلْمُنكَرَۖ يَكَادُونَ يَسْطُونَ بِٱلَّذِينَ يَتْلُونَ عَلَيْهِمْ ءَايَٰتِنَاۗ قُلْ أَفَأُنَبِّئُكُم بِشَرٍّ مِّن ذَٰلِكُمُۗ ٱلنَّارُ وَعَدَهَا ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ۖ وَبِئْسَ ٱلْمَصِيرُ ", "translation": "And when Our verses are recited to them as clear evidences, you recognize in the faces of those who disbelieve disapproval. They are almost on the verge of assaulting those who recite to them Our verses. Say, \"Then shall I inform you of [what is] worse than that?[1] [It is] the Fire which Allāh has promised those who disbelieve, and wretched is the destination.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-idhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُتْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْtُt", "root": "تلو", "latinTranslit": "tut'lā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhim"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتُنَا", "translit": "āَnُtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "āyātunā"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيِّنَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰnِّyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "bayyinātin"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَعْرِفُ", "translit": "ُfِrْعَt", "root": "عرف", "latinTranslit": "taʿrifu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وُجُوهِ", "translit": "ِhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه", "latinTranslit": "wujūhi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنكَرَ", "translit": "َrَknُmْlٱ", "root": "نكر", "latinTranslit": "l-munkara"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَكَادُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdāَkَy", "root": "كود", "latinTranslit": "yakādūna"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يَسْطُونَ", "translit": "َnwُṭْsَy", "root": "سطو", "latinTranslit": "yasṭūna"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱِb", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "bi-alladhīna"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَتْلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlْtَy", "root": "تلو", "latinTranslit": "yatlūna"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalayhim"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "āyātinā"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قُلْ", "translit": "ْlُq", "root": "قول", "latinTranslit": "qul"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "أَفَأُنَبِّئُكُم", "translit": "mُkُyʾِّbَnُaʾَfَaʾ", "root": "نبا", "latinTranslit": "afa-unabbi-ukum"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "بِشَرٍّ", "translit": "ٍّrَhsِb", "root": "شرر", "latinTranslit": "bisharrin"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālikumu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارُ", "translit": "ُrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور", "latinTranslit": "l-nāru"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَعَدَهَا", "translit": "āَhَdَعَw", "root": "وعد", "latinTranslit": "waʿadahā"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَبِئْسَ", "translit": "َsْyʾِbَw", "root": "باس", "latinTranslit": "wabi'sa"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "ٱلْمَصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَmْlٱ", "root": "صير", "latinTranslit": "l-maṣīru"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "[Prophet], you can see the hostility on the faces of the disbelievers when Our messages are recited clearly to them: it is almost as if they are going to attack those who recite Our messages to them. Say, ‘Shall I tell you what is far worse than what you feel now? The Fire that God has promised the disbelievers! What a dismal end!’", "clearyTranslation": "[Prophet], you can see the hostility on the faces of the disbelievers when Our messages are recited clearly to them: it is almost as if they are going to attack those who recite Our messages to them. Say, ‘Shall I tell you what is far worse than what you feel now? The Fire that God has promised the disbelievers! What a dismal end!’"}, {"ayah": 73, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّاسُ ضُرِبَ مَثَلٌ فَٱسْتَمِعُوا۟ لَهُۥٓۚ إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ تَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ لَن يَخْلُقُوا۟ ذُبَابًا وَلَوِ ٱجْتَمَعُوا۟ لَهُۥۖ وَإِن يَسْلُبْهُمُ ٱلذُّبَابُ شَيْـًٔا لَّا يَسْتَنقِذُوهُ مِنْهُۚ ضَعُفَ ٱلطَّالِبُ وَٱلْمَطْلُوبُ ", "translation": "O people, an example is presented, so listen to it. Indeed, those you invoke besides Allāh will never create [as much as] a fly, even if they gathered together for it [i.e., that purpose]. And if the fly should steal from them a [tiny] thing, they could not recover it from him. Weak are the pursuer and pursued.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsu"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ضُرِبَ", "translit": "َbِrُḍ", "root": "ضرب", "latinTranslit": "ḍuriba"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَثَلٌ", "translit": "ٌlَhtَm", "root": "مثل", "latinTranslit": "mathalun"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَمِعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعِmَtْsٱَf", "root": "سمع", "latinTranslit": "fa-is'tamiʿū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَدْعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُعْdَt", "root": "دعو", "latinTranslit": "tadʿūna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون", "latinTranslit": "dūni"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَن", "translit": "nَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lan"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَخْلُقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqُlْhkَy", "root": "خلق", "latinTranslit": "yakhluqū"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذُبَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَbُhd", "root": "ذبب", "latinTranslit": "dhubāban"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَلَوِ", "translit": "ِwَlَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "walawi"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱجْتَمَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعَmَtْjgٱ", "root": "جمع", "latinTranslit": "ij'tamaʿū"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-in"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَسْلُبْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْbُlْsَy", "root": "سلب", "latinTranslit": "yaslub'humu"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱلذُّبَابُ", "translit": "ُbāَbُّhdlٱ", "root": "ذبب", "latinTranslit": "l-dhubābu"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا", "latinTranslit": "shayan"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "لَّا", "translit": "āَّl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lā"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَسْتَنقِذُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُhdِqnَtْsَy", "root": "نقذ", "latinTranslit": "yastanqidhūhu"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min'hu"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ضَعُفَ", "translit": "َfُعَḍ", "root": "ضعف", "latinTranslit": "ḍaʿufa"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱلطَّالِبُ", "translit": "ُbِlāَّṭlٱ", "root": "طلب", "latinTranslit": "l-ṭālibu"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَٱلْمَطْلُوبُ", "translit": "ُbwُlْṭَmْlٱَw", "root": "طلب", "latinTranslit": "wal-maṭlūbu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "People, here is an illustration, so listen carefully: those you call on beside God could not, even if they combined all their forces, create a fly, and if a fly took something away from them, they would not be able to retrieve it. How feeble are the petitioners and how feeble are those they petition", "clearyTranslation": "People, here is an illustration, so listen carefully: those you call on beside God could not, even if they combined all their forces, create a fly, and if a fly took something away from them, they would not be able to retrieve it. How feeble are the petitioners and how feeble are those they petition"}, {"ayah": 74, "text": "مَا قَدَرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ حَقَّ قَدْرِهِۦٓۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَقَوِىٌّ عَزِيزٌ ", "translation": "They have not appraised Allāh with true appraisal.[1] Indeed, Allāh is Powerful and Exalted in Might.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَدَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَdَq", "root": "قدر", "latinTranslit": "qadarū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "ḥaqqa"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَدْرِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِrْdَq", "root": "قدر", "latinTranslit": "qadrihi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَقَوِىٌّ", "translit": "ٌّàِwَqَl", "root": "قوي", "latinTranslit": "laqawiyyun"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَزِيزٌ", "translit": "ٌzyِzَع", "root": "عزز", "latinTranslit": "ʿazīzun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "They have no grasp of God’s true measure: God is truly most strong and mighty", "clearyTranslation": "They have no grasp of God’s true measure: God is truly most strong and mighty"}, {"ayah": 75, "text": "ٱللَّهُ يَصْطَفِى مِنَ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ رُسُلًا وَمِنَ ٱلنَّاسِۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَمِيعٌۢ بَصِيرٌ ", "translation": "Allāh chooses from the angels messengers and from the people. Indeed, Allāh is Hearing and Seeing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "al-lahu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْطَفِى", "translit": "àِfَṭْṣَy", "root": "صفو", "latinTranslit": "yaṣṭafī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك", "latinTranslit": "l-malāikati"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رُسُلًا", "translit": "āًlُsُr", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "rusulan"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamina"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "سَمِيعٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢعyِmَs", "root": "سمع", "latinTranslit": "samīʿun"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَصِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِṣَb", "root": "بصر", "latinTranslit": "baṣīrun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "God chooses messengers from among the angels and from among men. God is all hearing, all seeing", "clearyTranslation": "God chooses messengers from among the angels and from among men. God is all hearing, all seeing"}, {"ayah": 76, "text": "يَعْلَمُ مَا بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَمَا خَلْفَهُمْۗ وَإِلَى ٱللَّهِ تُرْجَعُ ٱلْأُمُورُ ", "translation": "He knows what is [presently] before them and what will be after them.[1] And to Allāh will be returned [all] matters.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْعَy", "root": "علم", "latinTranslit": "yaʿlamu"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "bayna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَيْدِيهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhyِdْyَaʾ", "root": "يدي", "latinTranslit": "aydīhim"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "خَلْفَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَfْlَhk", "root": "خلف", "latinTranslit": "khalfahum"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَإِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wa-ilā"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تُرْجَعُ", "translit": "ُعَjgْrُt", "root": "رجع", "latinTranslit": "tur'jaʿu"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأُمُورُ", "translit": "ُrwُmُaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "l-umūru"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "He knows what lies before and behind them. All matters return to Him", "clearyTranslation": "He knows what lies before and behind them. All matters return to Him"}, {"ayah": 77, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱرْكَعُوا۟ وَٱسْجُدُوا۟ وَٱعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْ وَٱفْعَلُوا۟ ٱلْخَيْرَ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ۩ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, bow and prostrate and worship your Lord and do good - that you may succeed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي", "latinTranslit": "yāayyuhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱرْكَعُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُعَkْrٱ", "root": "ركع", "latinTranslit": "ir'kaʿū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱسْجُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُjgْsٱَw", "root": "سجد", "latinTranslit": "wa-us'judū"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْعٱَw", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "wa-uʿ'budū"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbakum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱفْعَلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlَعْfٱَw", "root": "فعل", "latinTranslit": "wa-if'ʿalū"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْخَيْرَ", "translit": "َrْyَhkْlٱ", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "l-khayra"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "لَعَلَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّlَعَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "laʿallakum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تُفْلِحُونَ", "translit": "َnwُḥِlْfُt", "root": "فلح", "latinTranslit": "tuf'liḥūna"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Believers, bow down, prostrate yourselves, worship your Lord, and do good so that you may succeed", "clearyTranslation": "Believers, bow down, prostrate yourselves, worship your Lord, and do good so that you may succeed"}, {"ayah": 78, "text": "وَجَٰهِدُوا۟ فِى ٱللَّهِ حَقَّ جِهَادِهِۦۚ هُوَ ٱجْتَبَىٰكُمْ وَمَا جَعَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِى ٱلدِّينِ مِنْ حَرَجٍۚ مِّلَّةَ أَبِيكُمْ إِبْرَٰهِيمَۚ هُوَ سَمَّىٰكُمُ ٱلْمُسْلِمِينَ مِن قَبْلُ وَفِى هَٰذَا لِيَكُونَ ٱلرَّسُولُ شَهِيدًا عَلَيْكُمْ وَتَكُونُوا۟ شُهَدَآءَ عَلَى ٱلنَّاسِۚ فَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَءَاتُوا۟ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ وَٱعْتَصِمُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ هُوَ مَوْلَىٰكُمْۖ فَنِعْمَ ٱلْمَوْلَىٰ وَنِعْمَ ٱلنَّصِيرُ ", "translation": "And strive for Allāh with the striving due to Him. He has chosen you and has not placed upon you in the religion any difficulty. [It is] the religion of your father, Abraham. He [i.e., Allāh] named you \"Muslims\" before [in former scriptures] and in this [revelation] that the Messenger may be a witness over you and you may be witnesses over the people. So establish prayer and give zakāh and hold fast to Allāh. He is your protector; and excellent is the protector, and excellent is the helper.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَٰهِدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdِhَٰjgَw", "root": "جهد", "latinTranslit": "wajāhidū"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَقَّ", "translit": "َّqَḥ", "root": "حقق", "latinTranslit": "ḥaqqa"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جِهَادِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdāَhِjg", "root": "جهد", "latinTranslit": "jihādihi"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱجْتَبَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَbَtْjgٱ", "root": "جبي", "latinTranslit": "ij'tabākum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَعَjg", "root": "جعل", "latinTranslit": "jaʿala"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaykum"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "l-dīni"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "حَرَجٍ", "translit": "ٍjgَrَḥ", "root": "حرج", "latinTranslit": "ḥarajin"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِّلَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّlِّm", "root": "ملل", "latinTranslit": "millata"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَبِيكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkyِbَaʾ", "root": "ابو", "latinTranslit": "abīkum"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "إِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ib'rāhīma"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "سَمَّىٰكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkٰàَّmَs", "root": "سمو", "latinTranslit": "sammākumu"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْمُسْلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlْsُmْlٱ", "root": "سلم", "latinTranslit": "l-mus'limīna"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل", "latinTranslit": "qablu"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَفِى", "translit": "àِfَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wafī"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hādhā"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لِيَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَyِl", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "liyakūna"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلرَّسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَّrlٱ", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "l-rasūlu"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "شَهِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد", "latinTranslit": "shahīdan"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalaykum"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "وَتَكُونُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnwُkَtَw", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "watakūnū"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "شُهَدَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَdَhُhs", "root": "شهد", "latinTranslit": "shuhadāa"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿalā"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسِ", "translit": "ِsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس", "latinTranslit": "l-nāsi"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "فَأَقِيمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmyِqَaʾَf", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "fa-aqīmū"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣalata"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "وَءَاتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtāَءَw", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "waātū"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَkَّzlٱ", "root": "زكو", "latinTranslit": "l-zakata"}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "وَٱعْتَصِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِṣَtْعٱَw", "root": "عصم", "latinTranslit": "wa-iʿ'taṣimū"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "bil-lahi"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "huwa"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "مَوْلَىٰكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkٰàَlْwَm", "root": "ولي", "latinTranslit": "mawlākum"}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "فَنِعْمَ", "translit": "َmْعِnَf", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "faniʿ'ma"}, {"id": 42, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَmْlٱ", "root": "ولي", "latinTranslit": "l-mawlā"}, {"id": 43, "arabic": "وَنِعْمَ", "translit": "َmْعِnَw", "root": "نعم", "latinTranslit": "waniʿ'ma"}, {"id": 44, "arabic": "ٱلنَّصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَّnlٱ", "root": "نصر", "latinTranslit": "l-naṣīru"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Strive hard for God as is His due: He has chosen you and placed no hardship in your religion, the faith of your forefather Abraham. God has called you Muslims––both in the past and in this [message]––so that the Messenger can bear witness about you and so that you can bear witness about other people. So keep up the prayer, give the prescribed alms, and seek refuge in God: He is your protector––an excellent protector and an excellent helper", "clearyTranslation": "Strive hard for God as is His due: He has chosen you and placed no hardship in your religion, the faith of your forefather Abraham. God has called you Muslims––both in the past and in this [message]––so that the Messenger can bear witness about you and so that you can bear witness about other people. So keep up the prayer, give the prescribed alms, and seek refuge in God: He is your protector––an excellent protector and an excellent helper"}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "عليه وسلم قوله تعالى إن زلزلة الساعة شيء عظيم {قال} أتدرون أي يوم هذا هذا يوم يقال لآدم عليه الصلاة والسلام قم فابعث بعث النار من ذريتك فيقول كم فيقال من كل ألف تسعمائة وتسعة وتسعون إلى النار وواحد إلى الجنة قال فأبلس القوم وجعلوا يبكون وتعطلوا يومهم عن الاشتغال والعمل فخرج عليهم رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وقال ما لكم لا تعملون فقالوا ومن يشتغل بعمل بعد ما حدثتنا بهذا فقال كم أنتم في الأمم أين تأويل وثاريث ومنسك ويأجوج ومأجوج أمم لا", "english": "\"Whoever meets God not associating anything with Him, the Fire is forbidden to him\" (9) — \"and none shall enter it who has in his heart the weight of a grain of faith.\" (10)", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "2": [{"arabic": "يوم تذهل فيه كل مرضعة عما أرضعت وتضع كل ذات حمل حملها وترى الناس سكارى وما هم بسكارى ولكن عذاب الله شديد يوم تبدل الأرض غير الأرض والسموات وبرزوا لله الواحد القهار يوم تنسف فيه الجبال نسفا فتترك قاعا صفصفا لا ترى فيها عوجا ولا أمتا يوم ترى الجبال تحسبها جامدة وهي تمر مر السحاب يوم تنشق فيه السماء فتكون وردة كالدهان فيومئذ لا يسئل عن ذنبه إنس ولا جان يوم يمنع فيه العاصى من الكلام ولا يسئل فيه عن الأجرام بل يؤخذ بالنواصى والأقدام يوم تجد كل نفس ما عملت من خير محضرا وما عملت من سوء تود لو أن بينها وبينه أمدا", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "4": [{"arabic": "قلب} وقال تعالى {كتب عليه أنه من تولاه فإنه يضله ويهديه إلى عذاب السعير} ومن ساعد الشيطان بعمله فهو مواليه وإن ذكر الله بلسانه وإن كنت تقول", "english": "And this is because those hearts were purified of Satan's pasture and his strength — namely, the desires (shahawāt). So if you hope that Satan will be repelled from you by mere dhikr, just as he was repelled from ʿUmar—may God be pleased with him—that would be impossible. You would be like one who hopes to drink a medicine before abstaining [from what harms him], while his stomach is occupied with heavy foods, and hopes it will benefit him as it benefited one who drank it after abstaining and emptying the stomach. For dhikr is the medicine, and taqwā is the abstention — that is, emptying the heart of desires. So when dhikr descends upon a heart that is empty of anything other than dhikr, Satan is repelled, just as illness is repelled by the descent of medicine into a stomach empty of food. God—exalted is He—said: {إِنَّ فِي ذَٰلِكَ لَذِكْرَىٰ لِمَنْ كَانَ لَهُ قَلْبٌ} \"Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart\" (Q 50:37). And He—exalted is He—said: {كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِ أَنَّهُ مَنْ تَوَلَّاهُ فَأَنَّهُ يُضِلُّهُ وَيَهْدِيهِ إِلَىٰ عَذَابِ السَّعِيرِ} \"It has been decreed upon him that whoever takes him as an ally — he will mislead him and guide him to the punishment of the Blaze\" (Q 22:4). And whoever aids Satan by his deeds is his ally, even if he mentions God with his tongue. And if you say...", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 603", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 603: On the Remedy for Evil Presumption and the Repelling of Satan", "section_index": 603, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (Shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, or being consumed by fire; also used for one who is destroyed or whose blood is shed in vain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الشيطان (Satan) — the one who is far removed from divine mercy, derived from the root indicating his being burned or destroyed.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"شاط اللحم\" — meat that is burnt or spoiled by fire. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"شاط دمه\" — his blood was shed in vain (i.e., destroyed); \"أشاط السلطان دمه\" — the ruler declared his blood lawful to shed. Thus, Satan is so named for his being \"burned\" or \"destroyed\" by divine curse, and for his causing others to be destroyed.\n- Root: قلب (Qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; also the act of turning, overturning, or revolving a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning and directing of a thing from one aspect to another. The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — to turn a thing from its face. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems. The heart's \"suwaydāʾ\" (core/blackness) is its innermost center where Satan seeks to establish his seat.\n- Root: ذكر (Dhikr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, mention, recollection; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر is the presence of a thing in the mind, whether by utterance or by heart; true dhikr is that which takes root in the heart, not mere speech of the tongue.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uttering with the tongue. Metaphorical: the heart's presence with God, which alone has authority over the soul and repels Satan's incursions.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The verse {إِنَّ الَّذِينَ اتَّقَوْا إِذَا مَسَّهُمْ طَائِفٌ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ تَذَكَّرُوا} restricts the efficacy of dhikr in repelling Satan to the God-fearing (muttaqūn), whose hearts are emptied of passions, thereby specifying the condition for its effectiveness.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "5": [{"arabic": "ذكر الأرض ليتفكر في عجائبها فظهرها مقر للأحياء وبطنها مرقد للأموات قال تعالى ألم نجعل الأرض كفاتا أحياء وأمواتا فانظر إلى الأرض وهى ميتة فإذا أنزل عليها الماء اهتزت وربت واخضرت وأنبتت عجائب النبات وخرجت منها أصناف الحيوانات ثم انظر كيف أحكم جوانب الأرض بالجبال الراسيات الشوامخ الصم الصلاب وكيف أودع المياه تحتها ففجر العيون وأسال الأنهار تجري على وجهها وأخرج من الحجارة اليابسة ومن التراب الكدر ماء رقيقا عذبا صافيا زلالا وجعل به كل شيء حي فأخرج به فنون الأشجار والنبات من حب وعنب وقضب وزيتون ونخل ورمان وفواكه كثيرة لا تحصى مختلفة الأشكال والألوان والطعوم والصفات والأر", "english": "And when you have come to know the path of reflection (ṭarīq al-fikr) within yourself, then reflect upon the earth which is your dwelling-place, then upon its rivers, its mountains, and its returns [to God], then ascend from it to the dominion of the heavens (malakūt al-samāwāt). As for the earth — among His signs is that He created the earth as a couch and a resting-place (firāshan wa mihādan), and threaded within it wide pathways, and made it subservient (dhalūlan) that you might walk in its open tracts, and made it firm, not swaying, and anchored therein the mountains as pegs (awtādan) to prevent it from tilting with you. Then He widened its regions until human beings were rendered incapable of reaching all its extremities, even if their lifespans were prolonged and their wanderings multiplied. So He, exalted, said: {وَالسَّمَاءَ بَنَيْنَاهَا بِأَيْدٍ وَإِنَّا لَمُوسِعُونَ} \"And the heaven We built with might, and indeed We are expanding it,\" and {وَالْأَرْضَ فَرَشْنَاهَا فَنِعْمَ الْمَاهِدُونَ} \"And the earth We spread out — how excellent a Spreader are We.\" And He, exalted, said: {هُوَ الَّذِي جَعَلَ لَكُمُ الْأَرْضَ ذَلُولًا فَامْشُوا فِي مَنَاكِبِهَا} \"It is He who made the earth subservient to you, so walk in its open tracts.\" And He, exalted, said: {الَّذِي جَعَلَ لَكُمُ الْأَرْضَ فِرَاشًا} \"He who made for you the earth as a couch.\" And He has multiplied in His Mighty Book the mention of the earth, that one might reflect upon its wonders — for its surface is the resting-place of the living, and its belly is the resting-place of the dead. He, exalted, said: {أَلَمْ نَجْعَلِ الْأَرْضَ كِفَاتًا أَحْيَاءً وَأَمْوَاتًا} \"Did We not make the earth a container — of the living and the dead?\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1273", "section_title_en": "On Contemplating the Earth, Its Rivers, Mountains, and Minerals — Section 1273", "section_index": 1273, "anchors": "- Root: فكر (f-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: التفكر: تأمل القلب وتدبر الشيء، والفكر: إعمال النظر في الشيء\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الفكر: قوة العلم التي بها يدرك الإنسان، وقيل: هو ترتيب أمور معلومة للتأدي إلى مجهول\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الفكر: تصرف القلب في النظر — literal: إعمال الروية؛ metaphorical: التفكر في ملكوت الله\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: التي عليها الناس، وجمعها أرضون\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء، ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: الأرض المهاد والفراش؛ metaphorical: تذلل الأرض وسكونها\n- Root: دحا (d-ḥ-w) / مهد (m-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: مهد: وطأ المكان وهيأه، والمهاد: الفراش الموطأ\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): المهد: الموضع المهيأ للصبي، والمهاد: الأرض الموطأة\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: فرش الأرض وتمهيدها؛ metaphorical: جعلها قارة ساكنة لا تميد\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — the interposed clause \"فانظر\" after \"إلى الأرض\" maintains grammatical continuity while allowing rhetorical pause.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Dhikr"}, {"arabic": "انظر إلى أرض البوادي وفتش ظاهرها وباطنها فتراها ترابا متشابها فإذا أنزل عليها الماء اهتزت وربت وأنبتت من كل زوج بهيج ألوانا مختلفة ونباتا متشابها وغير متشابه لكل واحد طعم وريح ولون وشكل يخالف الآخر فانظر إلى كثرتها واختلاف أصنافها وكثرة أشكالها ثم اختلاف طبائع النبات وكثرة منافعه وكيف أودع الله تعالى العقاقير المنافع الغريبة فهذا النبات يغذي وهذا يقوي وهذا يحيي وهذا يقتل وهذا يبرد وهذا يسخن وهذا إذا حصل في المعدة قمع الصفراء من أعماق العروق وهذا يستحيل إلى الصفراء وهذا يقمع البلغم والسوداء وهذا يستحيل إليهما و", "english": "And when you have come to know the path of reflection (ṭarīq al-fikr) within yourself, then reflect upon the earth which is your dwelling-place, then upon its rivers, its mountains, and its returns [to God], then ascend from it to the dominion of the heavens (malakūt al-samāwāt). As for the earth — among His signs is that He created the earth as a couch and a resting-place (firāshan wa mihādan), and threaded within it wide pathways, and made it subservient (dhalūlan) that you might walk in its open tracts, and made it firm, not swaying, and anchored therein the mountains as pegs (awtādan) to prevent it from tilting with you. Then He widened its regions until human beings were rendered incapable of reaching all its extremities, even if their lifespans were prolonged and their wanderings multiplied. So He, exalted, said: {وَالسَّمَاءَ بَنَيْنَاهَا بِأَيْدٍ وَإِنَّا لَمُوسِعُونَ} \"And the heaven We built with might, and indeed We are expanding it,\" and {وَالْأَرْضَ فَرَشْنَاهَا فَنِعْمَ الْمَاهِدُونَ} \"And the earth We spread out — how excellent a Spreader are We.\" And He, exalted, said: {هُوَ الَّذِي جَعَلَ لَكُمُ الْأَرْضَ ذَلُولًا فَامْشُوا فِي مَنَاكِبِهَا} \"It is He who made the earth subservient to you, so walk in its open tracts.\" And He, exalted, said: {الَّذِي جَعَلَ لَكُمُ الْأَرْضَ فِرَاشًا} \"He who made for you the earth as a couch.\" And He has multiplied in His Mighty Book the mention of the earth, that one might reflect upon its wonders — for its surface is the resting-place of the living, and its belly is the resting-place of the dead. He, exalted, said: {أَلَمْ نَجْعَلِ الْأَرْضَ كِفَاتًا أَحْيَاءً وَأَمْوَاتًا} \"Did We not make the earth a container — of the living and the dead?\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1273", "section_title_en": "On Contemplating the Earth, Its Rivers, Mountains, and Minerals — Section 1273", "section_index": 1273, "anchors": "- Root: فكر (f-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: التفكر: تأمل القلب وتدبر الشيء، والفكر: إعمال النظر في الشيء\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الفكر: قوة العلم التي بها يدرك الإنسان، وقيل: هو ترتيب أمور معلومة للتأدي إلى مجهول\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الفكر: تصرف القلب في النظر — literal: إعمال الروية؛ metaphorical: التفكر في ملكوت الله\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: التي عليها الناس، وجمعها أرضون\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء، ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: الأرض المهاد والفراش؛ metaphorical: تذلل الأرض وسكونها\n- Root: دحا (d-ḥ-w) / مهد (m-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: مهد: وطأ المكان وهيأه، والمهاد: الفراش الموطأ\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): المهد: الموضع المهيأ للصبي، والمهاد: الأرض الموطأة\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): literal: فرش الأرض وتمهيدها؛ metaphorical: جعلها قارة ساكنة لا تميد\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — the interposed clause \"فانظر\" after \"إلى الأرض\" maintains grammatical continuity while allowing rhetorical pause.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}, {"arabic": "الله من أرذل الأشياء ثم من أقذرها إذ قد خلقه من تراب ثم من نطفة ثم من علقة ثم من مضغة ثم جعله عظما ثم كسا العظم لحما فقد كان هذا بداية وجوده حيث كان شيئا مذكورا فما صار شيئا مذكورا إلا وهو على أخس الأوصاف والنعوت إذ لم يخلق في ابتدائه كاملا بل خلقه جمادا ميتا لا يسمع ولا يبصر ولا يحس ولا يتحرك ولا ينطق ولا يبطش ولا يدرك ولا يعلم فبدأ بموته قبل حياته وبضعفه قبل قوته وبجهله قبل علمه وبعماه قبل بصره وبصممه قبل سمعه وببكمه قبل نطقه وبضلالته قبل هداه وبفقره قبل غناه وبعجزه قبل قدرته", "english": "The first: the extirpation of its root from its very source, and the uprooting of its tree from its planting-ground in the heart.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 863", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Character Traits of the Humble and the Sum Total of Wherein the Effect of Humility and Arrogance Appears — and the Exposition of the Method of Treating Arrogance and Acquiring Humility", "section_index": 863, "anchors": "- Root: تواضع (tawāḍuʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root و-ض-ع: to place down, lower, set in a low position; al-tawāḍuʿ is self-lowering, the voluntary assumption of lowness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waḍʿ is placing a thing in a station; tawāḍuʿ is the soul's self-abasement before truth and before the Creator, opposed to takabbur (self-elevation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical lowering of position. Majāz: the soul's humility and abandonment of claims to eminence.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root ك-ب-ر: bigness, magnitude; al-kibriyāʾ is exclusive divine majesty; al-kibr (arrogance) is the creature's false claim to that majesty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-kibr is the soul's self-magnification, a claim to a station of greatness that belongs to God alone; it is the root of all disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: physical greatness. Majāz: the heart's disdain of others and refusal of truth — the blameworthy kibr.\n- Root: نطف (nuṭfah)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nuṭfah: pure water, used for the seminal fluid. {«ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ»} [Al-Muʾminūn: 13].\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: clear water, little or much. Majāz: \"laylah naṭūf\" — a night raining until morning.\n- Root: خلق (khalaqa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khalq: measured estimation (taqdīr mustaqīm); used for bringing forth from nothing without model, and for producing a thing from another thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah: the tailor's cutting cloth to measure. Majāz: God's origination of creatures upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ʿaṭf ʿalā al-ḍamīr al-marfūʿ al-muttaṣil (Coordination upon the connected nominative pronoun) — permissible when preceded by an explicit subject, as in \"خلقه من نطفة فقدره\" where the sequence of verbs coordinates without repetition of the pronoun, each verb governing its own object in ordered succession.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "6": [{"arabic": "دفعه نسأل الله حسن التوفيق بلطفه وكرمه أنه على كل شيء قدير والحمد لله وحده", "english": "We ask Allah for good success (ḥusn al-tawfīq) through His gentleness (luṭf) and His generosity (karam). Verily, He is over all things Omnipotent. And praise be to Allah alone.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 705", "section_title_en": "The Third Category: What Is Necessary for Some People but Not Others — The Discipline of Anger", "section_index": 705, "anchors": "- Root: غضب (ghaḍab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: anger, wrath, fury — the boiling of the blood of the heart desiring vengeance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): When attributed to God, it means retribution (intiqām) alone, not an emotional state. For humans, it is the surge of blood in the heart seeking revenge.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): anger toward the living (ghaḍibtu lahu). Metaphorical (majāz): anger toward the dead (ghaḍibtu bihi), and the kindling of anger like fire catching flame.\n- Root: حب (ḥubb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Love, affection, attachment — the inclination of the heart toward what pleases it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Love is the heart's yearning for what it deems agreeable; it is the root from which anger branches, for one only becomes angry over what he loves.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: enduring affection. Metaphorical: love as the driving force behind action and reaction.\n- Root: رياضة (riyāḍah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Training, discipline, exercise — the breaking and subduing of the self through repeated practice.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Spiritual discipline: habituating the soul to virtuous acts until they become ingrained character (khuluq rāsikh).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: training an animal. Metaphorical: disciplining the soul's faculties.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التقسيم والتفصيل (Division and Detailing) — The periodic structure here divides anger into three categories (qism), then treats each sequentially with conditional and causal clauses (fa-inna, fa-idhā, wa-ammā), requiring precise tracking of antecedents and consequents.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "7": [{"arabic": "حق ومنكرا ونكيرا حق ووعدك حق ووعيدك حق ولقاءك حق والساعة آتية لا ريب فيها وأن الله يبعث من في القبور على ذلك أحيا وعليه أموت وعليه أبعث إن شاء الله اللهم أنت ربي لا إله إلا أنت خلقتني وأنا عبدك وأنا على عهدك ووعدك ما استطعت أعوذ بك اللهم من شر ما صنعت ومن شر كل ذي شر اللهم إني ظلمت نفسي فاغفر لي ذنوبي فإنه لا يغفر الذنوب إلا أنت واهدني لأحسن الأخلاق فإنه لا يهدي لأحسنها إلا أنت واصرف عني سيئها فإنه لا يصرف سيئها إلا أنت لبيك وسعديك والخير كله بيديك أنا لك وإليك أستغفرك وأتوب إليك آمنت ا", "english": "Ibrāhīm ibn Bashshār, his servant, narrated that he used to say this supplication every Friday, when he entered the morning and when he entered the evening: \"Welcome to the Day of Increase (yawm al-mazīd), and the new morning, and the scribe and the witness. This day of ours is a day of festival. Write down for us in it what we say: In the name of Allah, the Praiseworthy, the Glorious, the Exalted, the Loving, the Doer in His creation of whatsoever He wills. I have entered the morning believing in Allah, and affirming the truth of His meeting, and acknowledging His proof, and seeking forgiveness for my sin, and humbling myself before the Lordship of Allah, and denying, regarding what is other than Allah, any divinity, and I am needy towards Allah, and relying upon Allah, and turning in repentance to Allah. I call Allah to witness, and I call His angels, His prophets, His messengers, the bearers of His Throne, and those of His creation whom He has created, to witness that He is Allah — there is no god but He, alone, with no partner — and that Muḥammad is His servant and His messenger, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, and that the Garden is true, and that the Fire is true, and that the Basin (al-ḥawḍ) is true, and that intercession (al-shafāʿah) is true, and that Munkar and Nakīr are true, and that Your promise is true, and that Your threat is true, and that the meeting with You is true, and that the Hour is coming, in which there is no doubt, and that Allah will raise up those who are in the graves. Upon that I live, and upon that I die, and upon that I shall be raised, if Allah wills.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 250", "section_title_en": "The Supplications of Ibn al-Muʿtamir (Sulaymān al-Taymī) and His Glorifications, and the Supplication of Ibrāhīm ibn Adham — May Allah Be Well Pleased with Them", "section_index": 250, "anchors": "- Root: سبح (s-b-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swift motion (in water or air); extended to the act of prayer and glorification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التسبيح: تنزيه الله تعالى. وأصله: المر السريع في عبادة الله تعالى\" — Glorification is the declaration of Allah's transcendence; its root is the swift passage in worship of Allah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"سبحت الله وسبحت له\" — to declare Him transcendent. Metaphorical: \"والنجوم تسبح في الفلك\" — the stars glide in their orbits, a metaphor for their obedient motion.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of praise and commendation for a beneficial act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Praise (ḥamd) is the acknowledgment of beneficence with reverence; it is due to Allah alone in His absolute perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" — to praise Allah with all His praiseworthy attributes. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت الأرض: رضيت سكناها\" — to find a thing agreeable, extending praise to non-verbal contexts.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of swift swallowing or devouring.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإلهام: إلقاء الشيء في الروع\" — Inspiration is the casting of a thing into the heart/mind (al-rawʿ), specifically from Allah or the higher assembly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ألهمه الله الخير: ألقاه في روعه\" — Allah inspired him with good. Metaphorical: \"جواد يلتهم الأرض\" — a swift horse devours the ground, extending to rapid comprehension.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the clause remains grammatically sound, as seen in the extended supplicatory sentences.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "وهذا أمر ينكشف يوم تبلى السرائر ويوم يبعثر ما في القبور ويحصل ما في الصدور فعند ذلك تتميز السبيكة الخالصة من البهرجة فنعوذ بالله من الخزي يوم العرض الأكبر السادس وسخ البراجم وهي معاطف ظهور الأنامل كانت العرب لا تكثر غسل ذلك لتركها غسل اليد عقيب الطعام فيجتمع في تلك الغضون وسخ فأمرهم رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بغسل البراجم (1) السابع تنظيف الرواجب (2) أمر رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم العرب بتنظيفها وهي رءوس الأنامل وما تحت الأظفار من الوسخ لأ", "english": "In a report even rarer than this, ʿĀʾishah, may God be pleased with her, said: \"A group gathered at the door of the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, and he went out to them. I saw him looking into a vessel, arranging his hair and his beard.\" I said: \"O Messenger of God, do you do this?\" He said: \"Yes. Indeed, God loves that a servant adorn himself for his brethren when he goes out to them.\" The ignorant man might suppose that this is from love of self-display before people, drawing an analogy from the character of others and likening the angels to blacksmiths. How far from the truth! For the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, was commanded to call to God, and it was among his duties to strive to magnify the matter of his own station in their hearts, lest their souls despise him, and to render his appearance pleasing in their eyes, lest their eyes belittle him, and thus they be repelled — and the hypocrites seize upon that to repel them.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 115", "section_title_en": "** The Book of the Etiquette of Bathing and the Removal of Filth — Section 115", "section_index": 115, "anchors": "**\n- Root: شعر (shaʿr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hair of the head; the growth upon the body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used metaphorically for that which requires tending and care.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bodily hair; metaphorical for outward state.\n- Root: وسخ (wasakh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dirt, filth, grime.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Physical impurity; extended to moral defilement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical dirt; metaphorical for sin.\n- Root: طهر (ṭahara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Cleanliness, purity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Physical and ritual purity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for washing; metaphorical for spiritual purification.\n- Root: عور (ʿawira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The private parts; that which is exposed and shameful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The pudenda; from ʿār (disgrace).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the body's shameful parts; extended to vulnerability.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, breath.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul; the essence of a thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the self; metaphorical for the inner disposition.\n- Root: حمم (ḥamīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hot water; the bathhouse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The place of washing with hot water.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the bathhouse; extended to the reminder of Hellfire.\n- Root: نكر (nakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To disapprove, to reject.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The disapproved; the obligation to forbid evil.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for rejection; extended to the angels Munkar and Nakīr.\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To look, to see.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Physical sight; extended to reflection and insight.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for vision; metaphorical for contemplation.\n- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remember, to mention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Presence of something in the heart; remembrance of God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for recollection; extended to admonition.\n- Root: عبر (ʿabara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cross over; to interpret.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): To derive a lesson from one thing for another.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for crossing; metaphorical for taking heed.\n- Root: سمع (samiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hear.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Auditory perception; extended to acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for hearing; metaphorical for obedience.\n- Root: خلو (khalā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be empty, to be alone.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Solitude; a place free of others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for emptiness; extended to privacy.\n- Root: ستر (satara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to conceal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Covering; protection of honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for covering; metaphorical for modesty.\n- Root: حيي (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be shy, to be modest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shyness; a branch of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bashfulness; extended to moral restraint.\n- Root: غض (ghaḍḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To lower, to cast down.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): To lower the gaze; to restrain.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for lowering; metaphorical for self-control.\n- Root: نفع (nafaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To benefit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Utility; advantage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for benefit; extended to spiritual gain.\n- Root: ضرر (ḍarra)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To harm.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Injury; detriment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for harm; extended to moral damage.\n- Root: قبح (qabuḥa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be ugly, to be foul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which reason and revelation deem repugnant.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for ugliness; metaphorical for moral evil.\n- Root: حسن (ḥasuna)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be beautiful, to be good.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): That which pleases the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for beauty; extended to virtue.\n- Root: خوف (khāfa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fear.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Apprehension of harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for fear; extended to reverence.\n- Root: رجس (rijs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Filth, abomination.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Impurity; extended to sin and punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical filth; metaphorical for spiritual defilement.\n- Root: شيطان (shayṭān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Satan; from shaṭana (to be distant).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Every rebellious being, from jinn or mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for Iblīs; extended to all who incite evil.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fire; extended to the Fire of Hell.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for fire; metaphorical for punishment.\n- Root: ماء (māʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Water.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The liquid of life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for water; extended to purity.\n- Root: ظلم (ẓalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be unjust; darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): To place something in other than its proper place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for darkness; metaphorical for wrongdoing.\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light; extended to guidance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for light; metaphorical for faith.\n- Root: قبر (qabara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To bury; the grave.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave; the place of the dead.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for burial; extended to the afterlife.\n- Root: حشر (ḥashara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To gather.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The gathering of creation on the Day of Resurrection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for gathering; extended to the Day of Judgment.\n- Root: صراط (ṣirāṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The path.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The clear way; the Straight Path.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for road; metaphorical for true religion.\n- Root: جنة (janna)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Garden; Paradise.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Garden of Delight.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for garden; extended to Paradise.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The pleasures of Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for delight; extended to eternal reward.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Painful retribution.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for torment; extended to Hell.\n- Root: حساب (ḥisāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Reckoning, account.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The accounting of deeds on the Day of Judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for counting; extended to divine judgment.\n- Root: ميزان (mīzān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Balance, scale.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The scale of justice on the Day of Judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for weighing; extended to divine equity.\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: **Bāb al-ʿAṭf ʿalā al-Maʿṭūf** (Coordination of Nouns and Clauses) — The text employs extensive coordination (ʿaṭf) to enumerate the eight categories of filth and the corresponding acts of cleansing, each governed by the same syntactic and legal predicate.\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "11": [{"arabic": "فلم تبق له دنياه ولم يسلم له دينه {خسر الدنيا والآخرة ذلك هو الخسران المبين} فيا لها من مصيبة ما أفظعها ورزية ما أجلها ألا فراقبوا الله إخواني ولا يغرنكم الشيطان وأولياؤه من الآنسين بالحجج الداحضة عند الله فإنهم يتكالبون على الدنيا ثم يطلبون لأنفسهم المعاذير والحجج ويزعمون أن أصحاب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كانت لهم أموال فيتزين المغرورون بذكر الصحابة ليعذرهم الناس على جمع المال ولقد دهاهم الشيطان وما يشعرون ويحك أيها المفتون إن احتجاجك بمال عبد الرحمن بن عوف مكيدة من الش", "english": "And after this, I have seen the ruined one who prefers this world — his joy is mingled with vexation, and from it burst forth varieties of anxieties and kinds of disobedience, and to ruin and destruction is his final end. The ruined one rejoices in his hope, yet neither does his world remain for him, nor does his religion remain intact for him. {«خَسِرَ الدُّنْيَا وَالْآخِرَةَ ۚ ذَٰلِكَ هُوَ الْخُسْرَانُ الْمُبِينُ»} (\"He has lost this world and the Hereafter. That is the manifest loss.\") [Quran 22:11]", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World (Dhamm al-Dunya)", "section_title_ar": "Section 783", "section_title_en": "The Condemnation of the Worldly Scholars — The Admonition of al-Ḥārith al-Muḥāsibī", "section_index": 783, "anchors": "- Root: خرج (kharaja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To extract, draw forth, or expel something from its place of containment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for the emergence of something from its potential state into actuality, or from the inward to the outward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Here used metaphorically (Majaz) for \"uttering\" — extracting wisdom from the mouth while the heart remains sealed against it.\n- Root: غل (ghalla)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Concealed rancor, malice, or treachery harbored within the breast.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A hidden corruption of the heart's disposition toward another, often linked to betrayal of trust.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is \"thirst\" or \"hidden enmity\"; here it denotes the inward state of hypocrisy where outward speech contradicts inward conviction.\n- Root: خسر (khasira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To lose, to suffer diminution of capital or principal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخسر والخسران: انتقاص رأس المال\" — loss of one's true capital (faith and the Hereafter), which is the \"manifest loss\" (الخسران المبين).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for commercial loss; metaphorical (Majaz) for the one who disobeys God — \"ومن لم يطع الله فهو خاسر\" (Whoever does not obey God is a loser).\n- Root: دحض (dahaḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To slip, slide, or become void; a false argument that has no firm footing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): An argument that is invalidated and rendered null, having no grounding in truth.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a foot slipping on slippery ground; metaphorical for specious reasoning that collapses under scrutiny.\n- Root: دها (dahā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To befall with calamity, to deceive cunningly, to afflict with a great matter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A profound and overwhelming affliction or cunning deception that strikes unaware.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a sudden calamity; here metaphorical for Satan's subtle deception that overcomes the heedless.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, allowing for rhetorical emphasis and dramatic effect in periodic sentences, as seen in the extended condemnations and rhetorical questions of this admonition.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of the World (Dhamm al-Dunya)'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "وفوز المقربين وذلك هو الخسران المبين ولقد كان الحسن البصري رحمه الله أشبه الناس كلاما بكلام الأنبياء عليهم الصلاة والسلام وأقربهم هديا من الصحابة رضي الله عنهم اتفقت الكلمة في حقه على ذلك وكان أكثر كلامه في خواطر القلوب وفساد الأعمال ووساس النفوس والصفات الخفية الغامضة من شهوات النفس وقد قيل له يا أبا سعيد إنك تتكلم بكلام لا يسمع من غيرك فمن أين أخذته قال من حذيفة بن اليمان وقيل لحذيفة نراك تتكلم بكلام لا يسمع من غيرك من الصحابة فمن أين", "english": "And it was said to Ḥudhayfah: \"We see you speaking words not heard from any other of the Companions—so from where did you take them?\" He said: \"The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) singled me out for them. The people used to ask him about the good, while I asked him about the evil, fearing that I might fall into it. And I knew that the good would not outstrip me in knowledge of it.\" And he said on another occasion: \"And I knew that whoever does not know evil does not know good.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 62", "section_title_en": "The Science of the Heart's Subtleties and the Corruption of Deeds — Section 62", "section_index": 62, "anchors": "- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of proximity, nearness in place, time, and rank; the verbal noun *qurbān* denotes drawing near through devotional acts.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القرب والبعد يتقابلان\" — proximity and distance are opposites; used in place, time, relation, favor, care, and power. Theologically, *al-taqarrub ilā Allāh* is the soul's approach to God through knowledge and worship, not spatial movement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): physical nearness. Metaphorical (*majāz*): \"فلان يقرب أمرا لا يتسهل له\" — one who undertakes a difficult matter; and \"حياك الله وقرب دارك\" — a greeting of honor. Here, *al-taqarrub* from creation vs. from God marks the contrast between seeking favor of men and seeking nearness to God.\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation of a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named for its constant fluctuation (*taqallub*) between states; it is the seat of intention and the organ by which one turns toward or away from God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and discerns their realities. In this text, *khaṭarāt al-qulūb* (thoughts that pass through hearts) are the subtle movements of the inner faculty that determine the soundness or corruption of deeds.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, breath; that by which a thing is valued.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul; also the essence (*al-dhāt*), as in \"ويحذركم الله نفسه\" where *nafsahu* means His very Self. In moral psychology, *al-nafs* is the lower self with its appetites (*shahawāt*), the source of *waswās* (whispering temptations).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self or person. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — \"he poured out his self,\" i.e., his blood; and \"أصابته نفس: عين\" — \"a self struck him,\" i.e., the evil eye. Here, *wasāwis al-nufūs* refers to the insinuating promptings that arise from the soul's lower impulses.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-m-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deed, action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد\" — work is every act done by a living being with intention; it is more specific than *fiʿl* (mere act). Deeds are either righteous (*ṣāliḥāt*) or evil (*sayyiʾāt*), and their value depends on the heart's state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical labor. Metaphorical: \"فلان ابن عمل إذا كان قويا عليه\" — one who is master of his work. In this passage, *āfāt al-aʿmāl* (corruptions of deeds) are the hidden defects that nullify works, which the scholars of the Hereafter investigate.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When *innamā* introduces a clause, it restricts the predicate to the subject alone, excluding all else. In \"وإنما الشأن في معرفة ما يفسدها ويشوشها\" (\"the only concern is knowing what corrupts and disturbs them\"), the restriction emphasizes that the sole essential matter is knowledge of the inward corrupters of deeds, not outward elaboration.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "تعالى {يدعو لمن ضره أقرب من نفعه لبئس المولى ولبئس العشير} وقال الشاعر الناس شتى إذا ما أنت ذقتهم ... لا يستوون كما لا يستوي الشجر هذا له ثمر حلو مذاقته ... وذاك ليس له طعم ولا ثمر فإذا لم يجد رفيقا يؤاخيه ويستفيد به أحد هذه المقاصد فالوحدة أولى به قال أبو ذر رضي الله عنه الوحدة خير من الجليس السوء والجليس الصالح خير من الوحدة ويروى مرفوعا وأما الديانة وعدم الفسق فقد قال الله تعالى {واتبع سبيل من أناب إلي} ولأن مشاهدة الفسق والفساق تهون أمر المعصية على القلب وتب", "english": "So if one does not find a companion with whom to contract brotherhood and from whom to derive one of these purposes, then solitude is more fitting for him. Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: \"Solitude is better than an evil companion, and a righteous companion is better than solitude.\" And it is narrated as raised to the Prophet (marfūʿan).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 416", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquette of Brotherhood: Section 416 — The Conditions of Brotherhood and Its Rights", "section_index": 416, "anchors": "- Root: دنا (d-n-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity in place, time, or rank; the near and the nearer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — proximity by essence or by judgment; used of spatial, temporal, and station-based nearness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical nearness; metaphorical (Majaz) for nearness in rank, honor, or moral standing.\n- Root: صحب (ṣ-ḥ-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The companion (al-ṣāḥib) is the one who accompanies another — whether a person, animal, place, or time; companionship by body is the origin and most frequent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصاحب: الملازم إنسانا كان أو حيوانا، أو مكانا، أو زمانا\" — the companion is the constant attendant, whether by body or by care and concern.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical accompaniment; metaphorical (Majaz) for ownership or mastery — \"هو صاحب مال وعلم\" (he is a possessor of wealth and knowledge) — and for divine guardianship: \"وصحبك الله تعالى\" (may Allah accompany you).\n- Root: ثمر (th-m-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fruit of trees; the produce that is eaten.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الثمر اسم لكل ما يتطعم من أحمال الشجر\" — fruit is the name for all that is savored from the burdens of trees; used metaphorically for benefit and outcome.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for botanical fruit; metaphorical (Majaz) for the knot at the tip of a whip — \"سوط عظيم الثمرة\" — and by extension, for any beneficial result or yield.\n- Root: قصد (q-ṣ-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To intend, to aim, to head toward; the straight and middle path.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القصد: استقامة الطريق\" — moderation is the straightness of the way; commendable mean between two blameworthy extremes (extravagance and stinginess, recklessness and cowardice).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) for physical direction and aiming; metaphorical (Majaz) for moral moderation — \"قصد في الأمر إذا لم يجاوز فيه الحد\" — and for the right path: \"على قصد السبيل\" (upon the straight way).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — When the text employs restrictive particles or contrastive structures (e.g., \"ليس ما يشترط... مشروطا\"), the predicate is confined to the subject alone, demanding precise scoping of conditions across distinct categories.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "18": [{"arabic": "إلى السماء والأرض والشمس والقمر والنجوم والغيم والمطر وسائر الجمادات والثانية النظر إلى اختيار الحيوانات وهي أعظم العقبتين وأخطرهما وبقطعهما كمال سر التوحيد فلذلك عظم ثواب هذه الكلمة أعني ثواب المشاهدة التي هذه الكلمة ترجمتها فإذا رجع حال التوكل إلى التبري من الحول والقوة والتوكل على الواحد الحق وسيتضح عند ذكرنا تفصيل أعمال التوكل إن شاء الله تعالى", "english": "Whoever crosses this obstacle (ʿaqabah) by the success (tawfiq) of Allah granted to him, his rank has risen and his degree has become great — and he is the one who truly affirms the saying \"There is no power and no strength except through Allah.\" And we have mentioned that in tawhid there are only two obstacles: one is the consideration of the heaven and the earth, the sun and the moon, the stars and the clouds and the rain, and the rest of inanimate things; and the second is the consideration of the choice (ikhtiyar) of animals — and it is the greater of the two obstacles and the more perilous. By cutting through both is the perfection of the secret of tawhid attained. For this reason, the reward of this word is magnified — I mean, the reward of the witnessing of which this word is the translation (tarjumah).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1119", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Reliance (Haqiqat al-Tawakkul) — Degrees of Trust and the Station of Witnessing", "section_index": 1119, "anchors": "- Root: دبر (d-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دبر الشيء: خلاف القبل\" — the rear, the back; by extension, the management of affairs as one turns away from one thing toward another (tadbir: to manage, to deliberate over outcomes).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"دبر الشيء: خلاف القبل، وكني بهما عن العضوين المخصوصين\" — used literally for spatial orientation; metaphorically for the ordering of one's own affairs (tadbir al-amr), which is the very faculty that tawakkul suspends or redirects.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أدبر النهار ودبر دبورا\" (the day passed away). Metaphorical: \"ما يعرف قبيلا من دبير\" — one who cannot distinguish front from back, i.e., total loss of self-direction; relevant to the state of the \"bewildered\" (mabhut) one.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — applies to the phrase \"لا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله\" where the restriction (hasr) confines all power (hawl) and strength (quwwah) exclusively to Allah, negating any independent creaturely agency.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "ومهما دعوا بالثبور صب من فوق رءوسهم الحميم يصهر به ما في بطونهم والجلود ولهم مقامع من حديد تهشم بها جباههم فيتفجر الصديد من أفواههم وننقطع من العطش أكبادهم وتسيل على الخدود أحداقهم ويسقط من الوجنات لحومها ويتمعط من الأطراف شعورها بل جلودها وكلما نضجت جلودهم بدلوا جلودا غيرها قد عريت من اللحم عظامهم فبقيت الأرواح منوطة بالعروق وعلائق العصب وهي تنش في لفح تلك النيران وهم مع ذلك يتمنون الموت فلا يموتون فكيف بك لو نظرت إليهم وقد سودت وجو", "english": "So they are made to dwell in an abode narrow of confines, dark of passages, obscure of destruction-pits. The captive is made immortal therein, and the blaze (saʿīr) is kindled therein. Their drink therein is boiling water (al-ḥamīm), and their resting place is the Inferno. The Zabāniyah subdue them, and the Hāwiyah (the abyss) gathers them. Their hopes therein are destruction, and they have no release therefrom. Their feet have been bound to their forelocks, and their faces blackened by the darkness of their disobediences. They call out from its sides and cry aloud in its regions and extremities: \"O Mālik! The threat has been verified against us! O Mālik! The iron has weighed us down! O Mālik! Our skins have been fully roasted! O Mālik! Bring us forth, for we shall not return!\" But the Zabāniyah say: \"Away! Far be it! There is no time for hope, and no exit for you from the abode of humiliation. {«فاخسئوا فيها ولا تكلمون»} [Be silenced therein, and do not speak to Me!]. And were you to be brought forth from it, you would indeed return to what you were forbidden.\" At that point they despair, and over what they neglected regarding God they grieve. But remorse does not save them, nor does sorrow avail them. Rather, they are cast upon their faces, fettered — the Fire above them, the Fire beneath them, the Fire to their right, the Fire to their left. So they are drowned in the Fire: their food is Fire, their drink is Fire, their garments are Fire, their beds are Fire.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1348", "section_title_en": "Discourse on the Attributes of Hellfire, Its Terrors, and Its Fetters", "section_index": 1348, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the primordial act of approaching a watering-place; from this derives the sense of arriving at any destination, whether desired or dreaded.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — its origin is the intent to reach water; then applied generally, and used of the Fire \"على سبيل الفظاعة\" (by way of horrification), as in {وإن منكم إلا واردها} — the universal \"arrival\" at Hell is a divinely decreed certainty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): ورد الماء — arriving at water to drink. Metaphorical (Majaz): وردت البلد — arriving at a land; ورد عليه أمر لم يطقه — an overwhelming matter descended upon him. Here, \"al-wurūd\" is majaz extended to the eschatological \"arrival\" at the Fire, a passage all must undergo.\n- Root: جلد (j-l-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The skin or hide covering the body; جلد الكتاب — to cover a book with leather; جلدته بالسياط — to flog.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الجلد: قشر البدن\" — the skin is the body's integument; in the Quranic verse {كلما نضجت جلودهم بدلناهم جلودا غيرها} [النساء/56], the renewal of skins is the renewal of the seat of pain, ensuring perpetual sensation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: flaying, covering, stripping. Metaphorical: جلدته على هذا الأمر — compelled him. In this text, the literal sense governs: skins roasted, then replaced, so that torment never ceases.\n- Root: ظلم (z-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Darkness, absence of light; also wrongdoing. The lexicographers note the moral-ontological link: injustice is a kind of darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الظلمة: عدم النور\" — darkness is the privation of light; used metaphorically for ignorance, polytheism, and transgression, as light is used for their opposites.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"الظلم ظلمات يوم القيامة\" — wrongdoing is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection. Metaphorical: أرض مظلومة — land never before dug. Here, \"zulumāt\" (ظلمات) is used literally of Hell's physical darknesses, yet these are also the reified shadows of the inmates' own wrongs.\n- Root: نار (n-w-r / n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fire; the root also yields نار — to kindle, to blaze up. The lexicons record the proverb: ورَيْتُ بك زنادي — \"I struck fire from your flint,\" i.e., I perceived from you good counsel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): Fire is the elemental reality of Hell, but its Quranic usage spans both physical flame and the \"Fire of God kindled\" which mounts over hearts [الهمزة/6-7] — the inward fire of heedlessness made outward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: flame, burning. Metaphorical: نار الحرب — the fire of war. In this passage, the literal fire of Jahannam is described with maximal sensory concreteness, yet it remains the outward manifestation of the soul's own kindled crimes.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening phrase is semantically complete. Applied here in the periodic constructions where long descriptive clauses intervene between the initial vocative (يا أيها الغافل) and its eventual predicate, sustaining rhetorical tension across the entire passage.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "الدنيا نزلت إليه ملائكة غلاظ شداد معهم ثياب من نار وسرابيل من قطران فيحتوشونه فإذا خرجت نفسه لعنه كل ملك بين السماء والأرض وكل ملك في السماء وغلقت أبواب السماء فليس بها باب إلا يكره أن يدخل بروحه منه فإذا صعد بروحه نبذ وقيل أي ربعبدك فلان لم تقبله سماء ولا ارض فيقول الله عز وجل ارجعوه فأروه ما أعددت له من الشر إني وعدته {منها خلقناكم وفيها نعيدكم ومنها نخرجكم تارة أخرى} وأنه ليسمع خفق نعالهم إذا ولوا مدبرين حتى يقال له يا هذا من ربك ومن نبيك وما دينك فيقول لا أدرى فيقال لا دريت ثم يأتيه آت قبيح الوجه منتن الريح قبيح الثياب فيقول أبشر بسخط من الله وبعذاب أليم مقيم فيقول بشرك الله شرا من أنت فيقول أنا عملك الخبيث والله إن كنت لسريعا في معصية الله بطيئا عن طاعة الله فجزاك الله شرا فيقول وأنت فجزاك الله شرا ثم يقيض له أعمى أصم أبكم معه مرزبة من حديد لو اجتمع عليها الثقلان على أن يقلوها لم يستطيعوا لو ضرب بها جبل صار ترابا فيضربه بها ضربة فيصير ترابا ثم تعود فيه الروح فيضربه بها بين عينيه ضربة يسمعها من على الأرضين ليس الثقلين {قال} ثم ينادي مناد أن افرشوا له لوحين من النار وافتحوا له بابا إلى النار فيفرش له لوحان من نار ويفتح له باب إلى النار (2) وقال محمد بن علي ما من ميت يموت إلا مثل له عند الموت أعماله الحسنة وأعماله السيئة قال فيشخص إلى حسناته ويطرق عن سيئاته وقال أبو هريرة قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم إن المؤمن إذا احتضر أتته الملائكة بحريرة فيها مسك وضبائر الريحان فتسل روحه كما تسل الشعرة من العجين ويقال أيتها النفس المطمئنة اخرجي راضية ومرضيا عنك إلى روح الله وكرامته فإذا أخرجت روحه وضعت على ذلك المسك والريحان وطويت عليها الحريرة وبعث بها إلى عليين وإن الكافر إذا احتضر أتته الملائكة بمسح فيه جمرة فتنزع روحه انتزاعا شديدا ويقال أيتها النفس الخبيثة أخرجي ساخطة ومسخوط عليك إلى هوان الله وعذابه فإذا أخرجت روحه وضعت على تلك الجمرة وأن لها نشيشا ويطوى عليها المسح ويذهب بها إلى سجين (1) وعن محمد بن كعب القرظي أنه كان يقرأ قوله تعالى حتى إذا جاء أحدهم الموت قال ربي أرجعون لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت قال أي شيء تريد في أي شيء ترغب أتريد أن ترجع لتجمع المال وتغرس الغراس وتبني البنيان وتشق الأنهار قال لا لعلي أعمل صالحا فيما تركت قال فيقول الجبار {كلا إنها كلمة هو قائلها} أي ليقولنها عند الموت وقال أبو هريرة قال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم المؤمن في قبره في روضة خضراء ويرحب له في قبره سبعون ذراعا ويضىء حتى يكون كالقمر ليلة البدر هل تدرون فيماذا أنزلت فإن له معيشة ضنكا قالوا الله ورسوله أعلم قال عذاب الكافر في قبره يسلط عليه تسعة وتسعون تنينا هل تدرون ما التنين تسعة وتسعون حية لكل حية تسعة رؤوس يخدشونه ويلحسونه وينفخون في جسمه إلى يوم يبعثون (2) ولا ينبغي أن يتعجب من هذا العدد على الخصوص فإن أعداد هذه الحيات والعقارب بعدد الأخلاق المذمومة من الكبر والرياء والحسد والغل والحقد وسائر الصفات فإن لها أصولا معدودة ثم تتشعب منها فروع معدودة ثم تنقسم فروعها إلى أقسام وتلك الصفات بأعيانها هي المهلكات وهي بأعيانها تنقلب عقارب وحيات فالقوي منها يلدغ لدغ التنين والضعيف يلدغ لدغ العقرب وما بينهما يؤذي إيذاء الحية وأرباب القلوب والبصائر يشاهدون بنور البصيرة هذه المهلكات والشعاب فروعها إلا أن مقدار عددها لا يوقف عليه إلا بنور النبوة فأمثال هذه الأخبار لها ظواهر صحيحة وأسرار خفية ولكنها عند أرباب البصائر واضحة فمن لم تنكشف له حقائقها فلا ينبغي أن ينكر ظواهرها بل أقل درجات الإيمان التصديق والتسليم فإن قلت فنحن نشاهد الكافر في قبره مدة ونراقبه ولا نشاهد شيئا من ذلك فما وجه التصديق على خلاف المشاهدة فأعلم أن لك ثلاث مقامات في التصديق بأمثال هذا إحداهما وهو الأظهر والأصح والأسلم أن تصدق بأنها موجودة وهي تلدغ الميت ولكنك لا تشاهد ذلك فإن هذه العين لا تصلح لمشاهدة الأمور الملكوتية وكل ما يتعلق بالآخرة فهو من علم الملكوت أما ترى الصحابة رضي الله عنهم كيف كانوا يؤمنون بنزول جبريل وما كانوا يشاهدونه ويؤمنون بأنه عليه السلام يشاهده فإن كنت لا تؤمن بهذا فتصحيح أصل الإيمان بالملائكة والوحي أهم عليك وإن كنت آمنت به وجوزت أن يشاهد النبي مالا تشاهده الأمة فكيف لا تجوز هذا في الميت وكما أن الملك لا يشبه الآدميين والحيوانات فالحيات والعقارب التي تلدغ في القبر ليست من جنس حيات عالمنا بل هي جنس آخر وتدرك بحاسة أخرى", "english": "And Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Verily, the believer, when he is at the point of death, the angels come to him with a silken cloth containing musk and bundles of sweet basil. His soul is drawn out gently, just as a hair is drawn out from dough. And it is said: 'O tranquil soul, come forth content and well-pleasing, to the spirit of God and His honor.' When his soul is brought out, it is placed upon that musk and basil, and the silken cloth is folded over it, and it is borne up to ʿIlliyyūn (the Highest Heights). And verily, the disbeliever, when he is at the point of death, the angels come to him with a coarse cloth containing a burning ember. His soul is torn out with violent tearing. And it is said: 'O wicked soul, come forth in wrath and met with wrath, to the humiliation of God and His torment.' When his soul is brought out, it is placed upon that ember, and it indeed has a sizzling sound, and the coarse cloth is folded over it, and it is borne away to Sijjīn (the Lowest Prison).\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1323", "section_title_en": "The Torments of the Grave and the Visions of the Soul at Death — Section 1323", "section_index": 1323, "anchors": "- Root: قبر (q-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The grave as the abode of the dead; the act of interment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave is the place of repose for the dead, and the term extends metaphorically to the state of the barzakh (intermediate realm). The Quranic phrase {حتى زرتم المقابر} [التكاثر/ 2] is a metonymy for death itself.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — the physical grave. Metaphorical (Majaz) — the spiritual state of the soul after death, as in \"رفع قبراه\" (raising one's grave-mound), i.e., arrogance, indicating that the grave's true reality transcends mere earth.\n- Root: ملك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dominion and authority; the angels as emissaries of divine command.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — the one who disposes by command and prohibition. Angels are termed \"رسل\" (messengers) of God, as in {الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا} [الحج/ 75], and their acts are entirely subordinate to divine will.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — angels as corporeal agents of punishment or mercy. Metaphorical — their true nature is immaterial (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah), imperceptible to the physical senses of the living.\n- Root: روح (r-w-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Breath, spirit, the animating principle.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (rūḥ) is a subtle divine reality (laṭīfah rabbāniyyah) that departs the body at death; its extraction is described in degrees of ease or violence according to faith or disbelief.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — the breath of life. Metaphorical — the spiritual essence that perceives the malakūt (invisible dominion) after death, a realm beyond the reach of the corporeal eye.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of the hadith, e.g., \"خرجنا مع رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في جنازة رجل من الأنصار,\" demonstrates how prepositional phrases (مع، في، من) interlock to build a narrative sequence, each clause grammatically complete yet subordinate to the overarching report, mirroring the unbroken chain of transmission (isnād).", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "23": [{"arabic": "قال الله يحلون فيها من أساور من ذهب ولؤلؤا ولباسهم فيها حرير والآيات في ذلك كثيرة وإنما تفصيله في الأخبار فقد روى أبو هريرة أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قال من يدخل الجنة ينعم لا يبأس لا تبلى ثيابه ولا يفنى شبابه في الجنة ما لا عين رأت ولا أذن سمعت ولا خطر عن قلب بشر (1) وقال رجل يا رسول الله أخبرنا عن ثياب أهل الجنة أخلق تخلق أم نسج تنسج فسكت رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وضحك بعض القوم فقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مم تضحكون من جاه", "english": "Ponder the form of Paradise, and reflect upon the bliss of its inhabitants, and upon the remorse of him who is deprived of it for his contentment with this world as a substitute for it. For Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Verily, the wall of Paradise is a brick of silver and a brick of gold; its soil is saffron, and its clay is musk.\" (1) And he was asked, may God bless him and grant him peace, about the earth of Paradise, and he said: \"A white flour-like pure musk.\" (2) And Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Whosoever would delight that God, Mighty and Majestic, should give him wine to drink in the Hereafter, let him abandon it in this world; and whosoever would delight that God should clothe him in silk in the Hereafter, let him abandon it in this world.\" (3) And he said: \"The rivers of Paradise gush forth from beneath hills, or beneath mountains, of musk.\" (4) And were the least of the people of Paradise in adornment to be weighed against the adornment of all the people of this world together, that with which God, Mighty and Majestic, adorns him in the Hereafter would still be better than all the adornment of this world. (5) And Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Verily, in Paradise there is a tree beneath whose shade a rider travels for a hundred years without crossing it. Recite, if you wish: {And a shade extended} (6) {«وَظِلٍّ مَمْدُودٍ»}.\" And Abu Umāmah said: The companions of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to say: \"God, Mighty and Majestic, benefits us through the Bedouins and their questions.\" A Bedouin came forward and said: \"O Messenger of God, God has mentioned in the Qur'an a harmful tree, and I did not know that in Paradise there is a tree that harms its companion.\" The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"{What is it?} {«مَا هِيَ»}\" He said: \"The lote-tree (al-sidr), for it has thorns.\" He said: \"God, Exalted is He, has said: {In a lote-tree that is thornless} {«فِي سِدْرٍ مَخْضُودٍ»} — God clips its thorns and places in the stead of every thorn a fruit; then the fruit splits open from it into seventy-two colors of food, no one color of which resembles another.\" (7) And Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: We alighted at al-Ṣifāḥ, and there was a man sleeping beneath a tree, the sun having nearly reached him. I said to the youth: \"Go with this leather sheet and shade him.\" So he went and shaded him. When he awoke, behold, it was Salmān. I came to him and greeted him, and he said: \"O Jarīr, humble yourself before God, for whosoever humbles himself before God in this world, God raises him on the Day of Resurrection. Do you know what the darknesses are on the Day of Resurrection?\" I said: \"I do not know.\" He said: \"The wronging of people, one another.\" Then he took a small twig that I could scarcely see due to its smallness, and said: \"O Jarīr, were you to seek the like of this in Paradise, you would not find it.\" I said: \"O Abu ʿAbd Allāh, where then are the date-palms and the trees?\" He said: \"Their roots are of pearl and gold, and their tops are the fruit.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1354", "section_title_en": "The Description of the Wall of Paradise, Its Lands, Trees, and Rivers; and the Description of the Garments, Furnishings, Couches, Canopies, and Tents of Its Inhabitants", "section_index": 1354, "anchors": "- Root: حوط (ḥ-w-ṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The enclosing wall or boundary that surrounds and protects a garden; its core sense is preservation and encompassment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the eschatological context, the wall (ḥā'iṭ) is a corporeal boundary delineating the Garden, yet its materials (silver and gold bricks) signify a reality transcending earthly substance (jawhar), pointing to the immaterial bounty (faḍl) of the divine grant.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for the physical wall; metaphorical (majāz) extension when the \"wall\" denotes the barrier of honor separating the blessed from deprivation.\n- Root: خلد (kh-l-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perpetuity and endless duration; the state of abiding without termination.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Eternal subsistence (khulūd) is an attribute of the abode of recompense, contrasting with the temporal accidents (aʿrāḍ) of worldly existence; it is a fixed, necessary concomitant of the divine promise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for unceasing time; metaphorical for the soul's permanent state of felicity, free from the vicissitudes of generation and corruption.\n- Root: ثمر (th-m-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The fruit borne by trees; the yield of a plant at its culmination.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Garden, fruit (thamarah) is a designated reality (musammā) whose essence is transformed by divine power—each thorn replaced by fruit—demonstrating the absolute capacity of the Creator over nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for edible produce; metaphorical for the \"fruit\" of deeds, whether reward or punishment, as an accrued outcome.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — Governing how predicates like \"its soil is saffron\" and \"its clay is musk\" are syntactically focused to affirm the exclusive, unprecedented nature of Paradise's attributes, distinguishing them from earthly analogues.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "25": [{"arabic": "حلت بينه وبينه وقد قال الله تعالى سواء العاكف فيه والباد من جعل لك هذا فنظر في وجهه وكان بعرفه لأنه من مواليهم فقال أعبد الله ابن مرزوق قال نعم فأخذ فجىء به إلى بغداد فكره أن يعاقبه عقوبة يشنع بها عليه في العامة فجعله في اصطبل الدواب ليسوس الدواب وضموا إليه فرسا عضوضا سيىء الخلق ليعقره الفرس فلين الله تعالى له الفرس قال ثم صيروه إلى بيت وأغلق عليه وأخذ المهدي المفتاح عنده فإذا هو قد خرج بعد ثلاث إلى البستان يأكل البقل فأوذن به المهدي فقال له من أخرجك فقال", "english": "As it is related that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam delivered the sermon before the prayer of ʿĪd, and a man said to him: \"The sermon is only after the prayer.\" Marwān said to him: \"Leave that, O so-and-so.\" Abū Saʿīd said: \"As for this one, he has discharged what was upon him.\" The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said to us: {«Whoever among you sees a munkar, let him forbid it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart — and that is the weakest of faith»} (1). For they understood from these universal statements the inclusion of the sultans under them — so how could they need their permission?", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 526", "section_title_en": "The Fourth Condition: Authorization from the Imam and Governor — Its Invalidity, and the Five Ranks of Ḥisbah", "section_index": 526, "anchors": "- Root: أ ذ ن (ʾ-dh-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"permission\" or \"license\" — to grant leave for an act; also \"ear\" (udhun), the instrument of hearing command.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ʾidhn denotes divine or authoritative sanction that legitimizes an action; its absence renders the act transgressive (muʿtadā).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense is \"to permit\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to the Imam's delegation of authority, which is a contingent, not essential, condition.\n- Root: ن ك ر (n-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nakirah denotes the unrecognized or the objectionable; munkar is that which the sound intellect and divine law repudiate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Inkār is the heart's rejection of what it does not conceive as right; it opposes maʿrifah (recognition). The tongue's inkār may be true or feigned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Nakira is stronger than ankara — the former is the heart's denial, the latter the eye's; here, munkar is the objective evil that the enjoiner must repel.\n- Root: ع ر ف (ʿ-r-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: ʿArafa means to know by trace or sign; maʿrūf is that which is well-known and approved by revelation and custom.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maʿrifah is knowledge attained through reflection on effects — more specific than ʿilm; hence, al-amr bi-l-maʿrūf is commanding what is recognized as good by divine attestation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense is \"to know\"; here, maʿrūf is the established good, and its enjoining is a communal obligation independent of temporal authorization.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Specification) — The conditional clause \"sharaṭa hādhā al-sharṭ\" is a restrictive predication that the author refutes by universalizing the command (ʿalā al-ʿumūm), a syntactic move that nullifies the exception.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وأما هؤلاء فإنهم يصدون عن سبيل الله ويجرون الخلق إلى الغرور بالله بلفظ الرجاء فيزيدهم كلامهم جراءة على المعاصي ورغبة في الدنيا لا سيما إذا كان الواعظ متزينا بالثياب والخيل والمراكب فإنه تشهد هيئته من فرقه إلى قدمه بشدة حرصه على الدنيا فما يفسده هذا المغرور أكثر مما يصلحه بل لا يصلح أصلا ويضل خلقا كثيرا ولا يخفى وجه كونه مغرورا وفرقة أخرى منهم قنعوا بحفظ كلام الزهاد وأحاديثهم في ذم الدنيا فهم يحفظون الكلمات على وجهها ويؤدونها من غير", "english": "But as for these [deluded ones], they turn others away from the path of God and drag creation into self-deception concerning God (al-ghurūr billāh) by means of the word \"hope\" (al-rajāʾ). Thus their speech increases them in audacity toward sins and desire for this world — especially when the preacher is adorned with fine garments, horses, and mounts, for his very appearance bears witness from his hair to his feet to his intense greed for this world. So what this deluded one corrupts is more than what he reforms — nay, he reforms nothing at all, and he leads many people astray. Nor is the aspect of his being deluded hidden.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 899", "section_title_en": "The Categories of the Deluded Among Preachers and Hadith Transmitters", "section_index": 899, "anchors": "- Root: أ-ص-ل (ʾ-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of foundation, root, and origin; to set right what is corrupt.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): اصلاح (iṣlāḥ) as the act of bringing something from a state of corruption (fasād) to soundness (ṣalāḥ), whether in belief, action, or speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes rectifying a physical object; metaphorical (majāz) usage applies to moral and spiritual correction of others.\n- Root: غ-ر-ر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: غرور (ghurūr) — deception, delusion, vanity; from غرّ (gharra), to be heedless or deceived by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الغرور (al-ghurūr) is the delusion that leads one to rely on false hopes, particularly regarding God's mercy while persisting in sin; the state of one who is deceived by Satan or by his own vain fancies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a thing that deceives by its outward show; Metaphorical: the soul's self-deception through false reliance on divine forgiveness without repentance.\n- Root: س-م-ع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السمع (al-samʿ) — hearing; the auditory faculty and its act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing as the means of acquiring transmitted knowledge (samāʿ); in hadith terminology, the formal act of hearing a tradition from a shaykh, which is the first stage of transmission.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical act of hearing; Metaphorical: attentive listening with the heart, as in \"ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" {«وَأَلْقَى السَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌ»} [Q. 50:37] — one who listens with full presence of mind.\n- Root: ح-ف-ظ (ḥ-f-ẓ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الحفظ (al-ḥifẓ) — preservation, guarding, memorization; keeping something safe from loss or alteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الحفظ is the faculty of the soul by which what is understood is retained; its opposite is forgetfulness (nisyān). It also denotes vigilant care and protection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: guarding a physical object; Metaphorical: committing knowledge to memory such that it is preserved from distortion.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of a preposition from its object by intervening clauses, as in the periodic structure \"فإذا لم تحفظ لا بالقلب ولا بالكتاب وجرى على سمعك صوت غفل\" where the conditional clause (فإذا) governs a sequence of coordinated conditions, each requiring its own apodosis, demonstrating the classical pattern of layered conditional sentences.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "إلا مكة وتلا قوله تعالى ومن يرد فيه بإلحاد بظلم نذقه من عذاب أليم أي أنه على مجرد الإرادة ويقال إن السيئات تضاعف بها كما تضاعف الحسنات وكان ابن عباس رضي الله عنه يقول الاحتكار بمكة من الإلحاد في الحرم وقيل الكذب أيضا وقال ابن عباس لأن أذنب سبعين ذنبا بركية أحب إلي من أن أذنب ذنبا واحدا بمكة وركية منزل بين مكة والطائف والخوف ذلك انتهى بعض المقيمين إلى أن لم يقض حاجته في الحرم بل كان يخرج إلى الحل عند قضاء الحاجة وبعضهم أقام شهرا وما وضع جنبه على الأرض وللمنع من الإقا", "english": "It is related from Wahib ibn al-Ward al-Makki that he said: \"I was one night in the Hijr praying, and I heard speech between the Ka'bah and the curtains saying: 'To Allah I complain, then to you O Gabriel, of what I suffer from those circumambulating around me — of their engrossment in idle talk, their frivolity, and their play. If they do not desist from that, I will surely shake myself with such a shaking that every stone of me shall return to the mountain from which it was cut.'\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 195", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Residing in Mecca (May Allah Protect It) and the Disapproval of Doing So", "section_index": 195, "anchors": "- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of apprehension and dread of a feared outcome\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — the expectation of something disliked based on a probable or known indication\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): fear of a tangible threat; Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه\" — to diminish gradually, as in \"تخوف السير منها\" (the journey wore it down little by little)\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the permissible separation between a preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis\n\n- Root: بيت (b-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The place of overnight dwelling; the Ka'bah as the sacred house\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل البيت: مأوى الإنسان بالليل\" — the origin is man's shelter by night; then applied to any dwelling, with \"البيوت\" more specific to residences\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical structure of the Ka'bah; Metaphorical: the heart's attachment and longing toward it\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Contextual Specification — the term \"البيت\" (the House) in sacred context refers specifically to the Ka'bah by customary designation", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "26": [{"arabic": "ومن كان آخر كلامه لا إله إلا الله لم تسمه النار (8) ومن لقي الله لا يشرك به شيئا حرمت عليه النار (9) ولا يدخلها من في قلبه مثقال ذرة من إيمان (10) وفي خبر آخر لو علم الكافر سعة رحمة الله ما آيس من جنته أحد (11) ولما تلا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قوله تعالى إن زلزلة الساعة شيء عظيم {قال} أتدرون أي يوم هذا هذا يوم يقال لآدم عليه الصلاة والسلام قم فابعث بعث النار من ذريتك فيقول كم فيقال من كل ألف تسعمائة وتسعة وتسعون إلى النار وواحد إلى الجنة قال فأبلس القوم وجعلوا يبكون وتعطل", "english": "\"And whoever's last words are 'There is no god but God,' the Fire will not touch him.\" (8)", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "27": [{"arabic": "قال الله عز وجل {وأذن في الناس بالحج يأتوك رجالا وعلى كل ضامر يأتين من كل فج عميق} وقال قتادة لما أمر الله عز وجل إبراهيم صلى الله عليه وسلم وعلى نبينا وعلى كل عبد مصطفى أن يؤذن في الناس بالحج نادى يا أيها الناس إن الله عز وجل بنى بيتا فحجوه وقال تعالى {ليشهدوا منافع لهم} قيل التجارة في الموسم والأجر في الآخرة ولما سمع بعض السلف هذا قال غفر لهم ورب الكعبة وقيل في تفسير قوله عز وجل {لأقعدن لهم صراطك المستقيم} أي طريق مكة يقعد الشيطان عليها ليمنع الناس منها وقال صلى", "english": "I say: The first [matter] concerning the virtue of the pilgrimage — God Most High said: {«وَأَذِّن فِي النَّاسِ بِالْحَجِّ يَأْتُوكَ رِجَالًا وَعَلَىٰ كُلِّ ضَامِرٍ يَأْتِينَ مِن كُلِّ فَجٍّ عَمِيقٍ»} (And proclaim to the people the pilgrimage; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel, coming from every deep and distant highway). Qatādah said: When God Most High commanded Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him — and upon our Prophet and upon every chosen servant) to proclaim the pilgrimage to the people, he called out: \"O people! God Most High has built a House, so perform the pilgrimage to it.\" And He Most High said: {«لِيَشْهَدُوا مَنَافِعَ لَهُمْ»} (That they may witness benefits for them) — it is said [these benefits are] commerce during the season and reward in the Hereafter.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 192", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of the Pilgrimage (Faḍīlat al-Ḥajj)", "section_index": 192, "anchors": "- Root: ح ج ج (ḥ-j-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to repair to a place repeatedly, to seek a thing, to overcome in argument.\" Al-Ayn: \"الحج: القصد والتردد إلى الشيء.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحج: القصد إلى البيت الحرام للنسك، وأصله من قصدت الشيء وأقبلت عليه.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قصدت فلانا وحججت إليه.\" Metaphorical: \"حاججته فحججته\" — to overcome in disputation, from the idea of repeatedly pressing one's point.\n- Root: غ ف ر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الغفر: الستر والتغطية.\" Al-Ayn: \"غفرت الشيء: سترته.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغفران والمغفرة من الله هو أن يصون العبد من أن يمسه العذاب.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"اغفر ثوبك في الوعاء\" (cover/protect your garment). Metaphorical: Divine forgiveness as protective covering of sins.\n- Root: و ف د (w-f-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوفد: القوم يأتون السلطان أو الرئيس زائرين أو مستعطفين.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوفد: القوم المجتمعون القاصدون إلى رئيس.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وفد فلان على الأمير\" — he came as a delegate. Metaphorical: The pilgrims as \"delegation of God\" (وفد الله) — those who come to Him seeking honor and bounty.\n- Root: ر ح م (r-ḥ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الرحمة: الرقة والتعطف.\" Al-Ayn: \"الرحمة: رقة القلب.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الرحمة: رقة تقتضي الإحسان إلى المرحوم.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رحمت فلانا\" — I felt compassion. Metaphorical: Divine mercy as the descent of blessing and pardon upon creation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the placement of prepositional phrases and their objects, particularly relevant in the verse {«يأتين من كل فج عميق»} where the prepositional phrase \"من كل فج\" is interposed between the verb and its subject, demonstrating the rhetorical emphasis on the vastness of the routes of pilgrimage.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "31": [{"arabic": "تهوي بنا الريح في مكان سحيق أحب إلينا من أن نتكلم بها فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أو قد وجدتموه قالوا نعم قال ذلك صريح الإيمان (1) حديث ابن عباس الحمد لله الذي رد كيد الشيطان إلى الوسوسة أخرجه أبو داود والنسائي في اليوم والليلة بلفظ كيده وقال أبو حازم ما كان من نفسك وكرهته نفسك لنفسك فلا يضرك ما هو من عدوك وما كان من نفسك فرضيته نفسك لنفسك فعاتبها عليه فإذن وسوسة الشيطان ومنازعة النفس لا تضرك مهما رددت مرادهما بالإباء والكراهة", "english": "Evidence for this from the reports (akhbār) is what is related that the Companions of the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) complained to him, saying: \"Things occur to our hearts such that we would rather fall from the sky and have birds snatch us away, or have the wind hurl us into a far-off place, than speak them.\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Have you indeed found that?\" They said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"That is clear faith (ṣarīḥ al-īmān).\" — The hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās: \"Praise be to God who has turned the Devil's cunning back to mere whispering (waswasah).\" Narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i in \"Al-Yawm wa al-Laylah\" with the wording \"his cunning.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 829", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Resistance to Ostentation and the Devil's Wiles — Section 829", "section_index": 829, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (Shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of burning, scorching, or spoiling by fire; the Devil as that which burns and destroys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الشيطان as the one who leads astray and incites; his \"نزغات\" (incitements) are his piercing stings of temptation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شاط اللحم\" (meat scorched/ruined). Metaphorical: \"أشاط دمه\" (shed his blood vainly) — the Devil's aim is to ruin the soul's deeds.\n- Root: كره (K-R-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dislike, aversion, abhorrence; the opposite of love (ḥubb) and desire (mīl).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The moral faculty of repulsion against sin; a trait of faith (īmān) distinguishing the believer's higher self from the lower nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"كرهت الشيء\" (I disliked it). Metaphorical: The heart's rejection of the soul's base inclinations as an act of worship.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; the seat of desires and passions.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس as the lower commanding soul (النفس الأمارة) that inclines to lusts, distinct from the intellect (عقل) and faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس سائلة\" (flowing blood/life). Metaphorical: The inner self that must be opposed when it inclines to ostentation.\n- Root: جهد (J-H-D) / مجادلة (Jadal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Striving, exertion, argumentation; from جدل (twisting ropes tightly).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): المجادلة as disputation that can distract from divine remembrance (ذكر الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"جادله\" (he argued with him). Metaphorical: The soul's engagement with the Devil's whispers as a subtle trap, diverting from sincere worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to delimit the four ranks of resistance, each defined by what it exclusively does or omits.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "32": [{"arabic": "تفسير قوله تعالى ذلك ومن يعظم شعائر الله إنه تحسينه وتسمينه وسوق الهدي من الميقات أفضل إن كان لا يجهده ولا يكده وليترك المكاس في شرائه فقد كانوا يغالون في ثلاث ويكرهون المكاس فيهن الهدي والأضحية والرقبة فإن أفضل ذلك أغلاه ثمنا وأنفسه عند أهله وروى ابن عمر أن عمر رضي الله عنهما أهدى بختية فطلبت منه بثلثمائة دينار فسأل رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أن يبيعها ويشتري بثمنها بدنا فنهاه عن ذلك وقال بل أهدها (1) وذلك لأن القليل الج", "english": "In sum: in every living liver (kabad ḥarrāʾ) there is a reward. So let him observe the right of the mount and the right of the camel-driver together—and in his dismounting for a while is the resting of the beast and the gladdening of the driver's heart.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 215", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Secrets of Pilgrimage — Section 215: On the Etiquette of Pilgrimage (Ādāb al-Ḥajj)", "section_index": 215, "anchors": "- Root: ش ع ث (sha-ʿ-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dishevelment, dust-covered hair, unkempt appearance; the state of one who has abandoned grooming and adornment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The outward state of humility and abandonment of worldly ornament—the physical correlative of spiritual self-abasement before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: unkempt hair and dust. Metaphorical: the visible sign of the pilgrim's total absorption in the sacred journey, forsaking all vanity.\n- Root: ن ف ث (n-f-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To blow or spit; in the ḥadīth, the disheveled and dust-covered pilgrim who blows dust from himself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The pilgrim who has completed the rites and blows away the dust of the journey—a state of completion and purification.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expelling breath. Metaphorical: the final act of shedding the pilgrim state's physical residue.\n- Root: ث ج (th-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The pouring forth of blood; the flowing of the sacrificial animal's blood at slaughter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ritual shedding of blood (nahr/dhabḥ) as an act of drawing near (qurbah) to God—the outward pouring of blood signifying the inward pouring forth of the soul in submission.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: copious flowing. Metaphorical: the complete and generous offering of the sacrificial animal.\n- Root: ع ج (ʿ-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To raise the voice; the loud utterance of the talbiyah.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The vocal proclamation of God's oneness and presence at the pilgrimage—the audible counterpart to the silent intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: loud crying out. Metaphorical: the pilgrim's public declaration of submission, contrasting with the silent bloodshedding.\n- Root: ق ض ي (q-ḍ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fulfill, complete, or discharge an obligation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The completion of the rites of pilgrimage—specifically, the removal of the state of iḥrām's prohibitions through shaving, trimming, and cleansing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to settle a debt. Metaphorical: the discharge of the ritual obligations incumbent upon the pilgrim.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's statement \"إنما الحاج الشعث النفث\" restricts the true essence of the pilgrim to the disheveled and dust-covered state, negating the essence for others.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "37": [{"arabic": "والقرابين التي هي مجاهدة للنفس بتنقيص المال قال الله تعالى لن ينال الله لحومها ولا دماؤها ولكن يناله التقوى منكم أي الصفة التي استولت على القلب حتى حملته على امتثال الأوامر هي المطلوبة فكيف الأمر في الصلاة ولا أرب في أفعالها فهذا ما يدل من حيث المعنى على اشتراط حضور القلب فإن قلت إن حكمت ببطلان الصلاة وجعلت حضور القلب شرطا في صحتها خالفت إجماع الفقهاء فإنهم لم يشترطوا إلا حضور القلب عند التكبير فاعلم أنه قد تقدم في كتاب العلم أن الفقهاء لا يتصرفون في الباطن ولا يشقون عن القلوب ولا في طريق الآخرة بل يبنون أحكام", "english": "I do not see that all this grandeur belongs to the prayer by virtue of its outward actions alone — unless there be added to it the purpose of intimate converse (munājāh). For that takes precedence over fasting, alms-giving, pilgrimage, and other acts. Nay, even over the sacrificial animals and offerings (qarābīn), which are a striving of the soul through the diminution of wealth. Allah—exalted be He—said: {«لَن يَنَالَ اللَّهَ لُحُومُهَا وَلَا دِمَاؤُهَا وَلَكِن يَنَالُهُ التَّقْوَى مِنكُمْ»} (\"Neither their flesh nor their blood reaches Allah, but the piety from you reaches Him\") — that is, the attribute that has taken possession of the heart, such that it compels it to obey the commands, is what is sought. So how then with the prayer, in whose actions there is no doubt? This is what indicates, from the standpoint of meaning, the condition of heart-presence (ḥuḍūr al-qalb).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 132", "section_title_en": "On the Remembrance, Recitation, Bowing, and Prostration — The Inner Reality of Prayer and the Condition of Heart-Presence", "section_index": 132, "anchors": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الحفظ للشيء، والذكر باللسان والقلب. والذكر: الصوت الذي يذكر به، والذكر: الشرف والصيت.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور معنى الشيء في النفس، إما بنطق اللسان أو بخطور القلب. والذكر ضربان: أحدهما ذكر باللسان، والآخر ذكر بالقلب. والذكر بالقلب أبلغ من الذكر باللسان، لأن الذكر باللسان قد يكون مع غفلة القلب، والذكر بالقلب لا يكون إلا مع التيقظ.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"ذكرت الشيء: نطقت به أو استحضرته في قلبك.\" Majaz: \"ذكرت فلانا بخير: أثنيت عليه، وذكرت له نعمة: عددتها عليه. والذكر: الثناء الجميل، والذكر: الصلاة لله.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure \"فأما أن يكون المقصود منه كونه خطابا ومحاورة أو المقصود منه الحروف والأصوات\" employs the disjunctive particle (أما) to establish a bipartite division, followed by the accusative of specification (التمييز) in \"امتحانا للسان\" to clarify the nature of the action.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "38": [{"arabic": "وسلم فقال نعم إن الله لا يحب المستكبرين فنزل وقعد معهم على الأرض وأكل ثم سلم عليهم وركب وقال قد أجبتكم فأجيبوني قالوا نعم فوعدهم وقتا معلوما فحضروا فقدم إليهم فاخر الطعام وجلس يأكل معهم وأما قول القائل إن من وضعت يدي في قصعته فقد ذلت له رقبتي فقد قال بعضهم هذا خلاف السنة وليس كذلك فإنه ذل إذا كان الداعي لا يفرح بالإجابة ولا يتقلد منة وكان يرى ذلك يدا له على المدعو ورسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يحضر لعلمه أن الداع", "english": "And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Do not eat except the food of a God-fearing man, and let none eat your food except the God-fearing.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 290", "section_title_en": "The Etiquettes of Hospitality: Invitation, Response, Attendance, and the Serving of Food — Section 290 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 290, "anchors": "- Root: ضيف (ḍ-y-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to incline, to lean toward.\" The guest (al-ḍayf) is one who inclines toward you, alighting as a visitor; the root carries the sense of leaning or bending.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The guest is \"one who inclines to you, alighting with you\"; hospitality (al-ḍiyāfah) became customary in the sense of receiving such a one. The word is originally a verbal noun, hence one and many are equal in most usage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ḍāfa ilayhi: he inclined toward him\"; \"ḍāfat al-shams: the sun inclined toward setting.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"aḍāfa ilayhi amran: he attributed a matter to him, leaning it upon him\"; \"al-muḍāf: the one constrained, hemmed in, in distress.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Prophet's statement \"innamā hādhihi al-akhlāq bi-yad Allāh\" (these morals are only in the hand of Allah), where innamā restricts the attribution of moral character exclusively to divine bestowal.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "40": [{"arabic": "أهل الأرض التي يذكر فيها اسم الله تعالى كما تتراءى النجوم وقال سفيان بن عيينة رحمه الله إذا اجتمع قوم يذكرون الله تعالى اعتزل الشيطان والدنيا فيقول الشيطان للدنيا ألا ترين ما يصنعون فتقول الدنيا دعهم فإنهم إذا تفرقوا أخذت بأعناقهم إليك وعن أبي هريرة رضي الله عنه أنه إذا دخل السوق وقال أراكم ههنا وميراث رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يقسم في المسجد فذهب الناس إلى المسجد وتركوا السوق فلم يروا ميراثا فقالوا يا أبا هريرة ما رأ", "english": "And David, peace be upon him, said: \"O my God! When You see me passing by the assemblies of those who remember You toward the assemblies of the heedless, then break my leg before them (preventing me from reaching them), for that is a blessing (niʿmah) which You bestow upon me.\" {«إلهي إذا رأيتني أجاوز مجالس الذاكرين إلى مجالس الغافلين فاكسر رجلي دونهم فإنها نعمة تنعم بها علي»}", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 236", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Assemblies of Remembrance (Majālis al-Dhikr)", "section_index": 236, "anchors": "- Root: جلس (J-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning denotes sitting, but etymologically derives from seeking elevated, firm ground (jals) upon which to seat oneself; also signifies what is rugged and elevated from the lowland.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root signifies the act of directing one's sitting place toward elevated ground; then generalized to any act of sitting (quʿūd). A majlis is any place where one sits. The term carries a sense of deliberate assembly and settled presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical sitting and companionship. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"jalis nafsihi\" — one who is a companion to himself, i.e., a recluse; also \"jalasat al-rakhamah\" (the vulture crouched). Here, the \"majlis\" is the locus of a spiritual gathering, not mere physical posture.\n- Root: ذكر (Dh-K-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The tongue's utterance and the heart's presence; remembrance, invocation, and mention. It is the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān) and also signifies honor and renown.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Remembrance (dhikr) is the presence of a thing in the mind, whether by utterance or by heart. It is graded: remembrance of God by the tongue, by the heart, and by both. The highest is when the heart is wholly present with God, which is the reality of dhikr.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to mention, to recollect. Metaphorical: dhikr as the act of glorification and praise that elevates the servant. In the prophetic traditions below, \"dhikr\" is the collective act of praising God, reciting the Qur'an, and seeking His face.\n- Root: حفف (Ḥ-F-F)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To surround, encompass, or encircle on all sides. A thing that encircles another is said to \"ḥaffa\" it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not explicitly listed, but the root implies comprehensive encircling, used here to denote the angels completely surrounding the assembly as a mark of honor and protection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical encircling. Metaphorical: the encompassing of divine mercy and angelic presence, signifying total spiritual envelopment.\n- Root: غشي (Gh-Sh-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, overwhelm, or envelop. It implies something that comes over a thing and covers it entirely, like night covering the day.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not explicitly listed, but the root implies an overwhelming covering. Here, \"ghashiyat-hum al-rahmah\" means mercy overwhelms and covers them completely.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical covering. Metaphorical: the descent of mercy that envelops the hearts, leaving no part untouched.\n- Root: سوع (S-W-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Evil, corruption, or that which is displeasing and harmful. Its opposite is good (ḥusn).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Evil (sūʾ) is that which causes grief or harm, whether in this world or the next. It is the opposite of good (ḥusn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: that which is bad. Metaphorical: \"majālis al-sūʾ\" — assemblies of evil, i.e., gatherings that lead to sin, heedlessness, and moral corruption.\n- Root: ستح (S-Y-Ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To travel, roam, or journey freely upon the earth. A \"sāʾiḥ\" is one who travels for worship or purpose.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): Not explicitly listed, but the root implies free movement. Here, angels \"roaming\" the earth signifies their mission to seek out assemblies of dhikr.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical travel. Metaphorical: the angels' ceaseless, purposeful movement across the earth in search of those who remember God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The phrase \"إلا حفت بهم الملائكة\" (except that the angels surround them) uses the restrictive particle \"illā\" to negate any outcome other than the angels' encompassing and mercy's descent, affirming the exclusive, guaranteed reward for such assemblies. Also, the conditional constructions (mā... illā) establish a universal, binding correlation between the act of sitting in remembrance and the divine response.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "وقال في قوله تعالى {ولولا دفع الله الناس بعضهم ببعض} قال بالرغبة والرهبة والحياء والمداراة وقالت عائشة رضي الله عنها استأذن رجل على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قال ائذنوا له فبئس رجل العشيرة هو فلما دخل ألان له القول حتى أن له عنده منزلة فلما خرج قلت له لما دخل قلت الذي قلت ثم ألنت له القول فقال يا عائشة إن شر الناس منزلة عند الله يوم القيامة من تركه الناس اتقاء فحشه (4) وفي الخبر ما وقى الرجل به عرضه فهو له صدقة (5) وفي الأثر خالطوا", "english": "Abū al-Dardāʾ said: We smile in the faces of people while our hearts curse them. This is the meaning of al-mudārāh (gentle forbearance), which is toward one whose evil is feared. God the Exalted said: {«ادفع بالتي هي أحسن السيئة»} — \"Repel the evil with that which is better.\" (Q. 23:96)", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 435", "section_title_en": "Section 435", "section_index": 435, "anchors": "- Root: رد (r-d-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To return, repel, defend, avert; al-radd ʿan al-ʿirḍ is to repel defamation from one's honor\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Defense against false accusation; the theological nuance of divine recompense (al-mukāfaʾah) for defending a brother's honor\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal repulsion of physical harm extended metaphorically to verbal defense of honor (ḥaqīqah vs. majāz)\n- Root: عطس (ʿ-t-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sneezing; al-tashmīt is the invocation of mercy upon the sneezer\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sneeze is attributed to God (min Allāh) as a sign of divine favor, while yawning is attributed to Satan\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The response \"yarḥamukallāh\" is a supplication, not an assertion — distinguishing literal invocation from mere congratulation\n- Root: عاد (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To visit repeatedly; al-ʿiyādah is the visitation of the sick\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Returning to someone in his affliction; the reward is measured by the intensity of compassion shown\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The \"return\" (ʿawd) is literal in visitation, but the reward is spiritual — a metaphorical extension of recompense\n- Root: شيع (sh-y-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To accompany, follow; al-tashyīʿ is the funeral procession\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Following the bier with the intention of honoring the deceased and taking admonition (al-iʿtibār)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The qīrāṭ is a measure of reward — metaphorical weight, not literal coin\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conditional Protasis (Bāb al-sharṭ) — The construction \"man ... fa-...\" (whoever does X, then Y) establishes a causal nexus requiring the apodosis to be a new sentence, often carrying the force of divine decree.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "41": [{"arabic": "ويدل ذلك على الوجوب أيضا وقال تعالى الذين إن مكناهم في الأرض أقاموا الصلاة وآتوا الزكاة وأمروا بالمعروف ونهوا عن المنكر فقرن ذلك بالصلاة والزكاة في نعت الصالحين والمؤمنين وقال تعالى وتعاونوا على البر والتقوى ولا تعاونوا على الإثم والعدوان وهو أمر جزم ومعنى التعاون الحث عليه وتسهيل طرق الخير وسد سبل الشر والعدوان بحسب الإمكان وقال تعالى لولا ينهاهم الربانيون والأحبار عن قولهم الإثم وأكلهم السحت لبئس ما كانوا يصنعون فبين أنهم أثموا بترك النهي وقال تعالى فلولا كان من القرون من قبلكم أولوا بقية ينهون عن الفساد في الأرض الآية", "english": "- Root: نكر (nakira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To reject, disavow, not recognize; munkar is that which is rejected and disapproved.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-munkar is what the soul rejects and the sacred law condemns; its repudiation (inkār) is the heart's refusal and the tongue's and hand's opposition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal denial versus metaphorical — the degrees of inkār: by hand, by tongue, by heart — the weakest of which is faith itself.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 521", "section_title_en": "Section 521", "section_index": 521, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "46": [{"arabic": "السموات والأرض الآية وسمي ضده عمى فقال تعالى فإنها لا تعمى الأبصار ولكن تعمي القلوب التي في الصدور وقال تعالى ومن كان في هذه أعمى فهو في الآخرة أعمى وأضل سبيلا وهذه الأمور التي كشفت للأنبياء بعضها كان بالبصر وبعضها كان بالبصيرة وسمي الكل رؤية وبالجملة من لم تكن بصيرته الباطنة ثاقبة لم يعلق به من الدين إلا قشوره وأمثلته دون لبابه وحقائقه فهذه أقسام ما ينطلق اسم العقل عليها", "english": "Thus, the defect of insight (khalal al-baṣīrah) runs its course — and it is more grievous and greater. For the soul is like the horseman, and the body is like the horse — and the blindness of the horseman is more harmful than the blindness of the horse. And because of the resemblance of the inner insight to the outer sight, God Most High said: {«مَا كَذَبَ الْفُؤَادُ مَا رَأَىٰ»} — \"The heart did not lie concerning what it saw\" (Quran 53:11). And He said, Most High: {«وَكَذَٰلِكَ نُرِي إِبْرَاهِيمَ مَلَكُوتَ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ»} — \"And thus We showed Abraham the kingdom of the heavens and the earth\" (Quran 6:75). And He named its opposite \"blindness,\" saying, Most High: {«فَإِنَّهَا لَا تَعْمَى الْأَبْصَارُ وَلَٰكِن تَعْمَى الْقُلُوبُ الَّتِي فِي الصُّدُورِ»} — \"For indeed, it is not the eyes that grow blind, but it is the hearts within the breasts that grow blind\" (Quran 22:46). And He said, Most High: {«وَمَن كَانَ فِي هَٰذِهِ أَعْمَىٰ فَهُوَ فِي الْآخِرَةِ أَعْمَىٰ وَأَضَلُّ سَبِيلًا»} — \"And whoever is blind in this [life] will be blind in the Hereafter, and further astray from the way\" (Quran 17:72). And these matters that were unveiled to the prophets — some of them were by outer sight (al-baṣar) and some by inner insight (al-baṣīrah), and the whole was called \"vision\" (ruʾyah).", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "50": [{"arabic": "كأنك حفي عنها أي يسألونك عنها كأنك حفي بها وقوله عز وجل لهم مغفرة ورزق كريم كما أخرجك ربك من بيتك بالحق فهذا الكلام غير متصل وإنما هو عائد إلى", "english": "And the Exalted said: {«حَتَّىٰ تَوَارَتْ بِالْحِجَابِ»} (\"Until it concealed itself behind the veil\") — meaning: the sun, though no prior mention of it occurred. And His saying the Exalted: {«وَالَّذِينَ اتَّخَذُوا مِن دُونِهِ أَوْلِيَاءَ مَا نَعْبُدُهُمْ إِلَّا لِيُقَرِّبُونَا إِلَى اللَّهِ زُلْفَىٰ»} (\"And those who take protectors besides Him: 'We do not worship them except to bring us near to Allah in proximity'\") — meaning: they say, \"We do not worship them.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "fiqh", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the inner and outer dimensions of worship, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Worship"}], "52": [{"arabic": "عائشة وقرأ ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما وما أرسلنا من قبلك من رسول ولا نبي ولا محدث يعني الصديقين والمحدث هو الملهم والملهم هو الذي انكشف له في باطن قلبه من جهة الداخل لا من جهة المحسوسات الخارجة والقرآن مصرح بأن التقوى مفتاح الهداية والكشف وذلك علم من غير تعلم وقال الله تعالى وما خلق الله في السموات والأرض لآيات لقوم يتقون خصصها بهم وقال تعالى {هذا بيان للناس وهدى وموعظة للمتقين} وكان أبو يزيد وغيره يقول ليس العالم الذي يحفظ من كتاب فإذا نسي ما حفظه صار جاهلا إنما العالم الذي يأخذ علمه من ربه أي وقت شاء بلا حفظ ولا درس وهذا هو العلم الرباني", "english": "And the Qurʾan declares explicitly that God-consciousness (taqwā) is the key to guidance and unveiling — and that is knowledge without learning. And God (Exalted is He) said: {«وَمَا خَلَقَ اللَّهُ فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ لَآيَاتٍ لِقَوْمٍ يَتَّقُونَ»} (\"And what God has created in the heavens and the earth are signs for a people who fear God\") — singling them out with it. And He (Exalted is He) said: {«هَذَا بَيَانٌ لِلنَّاسِ وَهُدًى وَمَوْعِظَةٌ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ»} (\"This is a declaration for mankind and a guidance and an admonition for the God-fearing\") (Q. 3:138). And Abū Yazīd and others used to say: \"The scholar is not the one who memorizes from a book, such that when he forgets what he has memorized he becomes ignorant. Rather, the scholar is the one who takes his knowledge from his Lord at any time he wishes, without memorization and without study.\" And this is the lordly knowledge (al-ʿilm al-rabbānī), and to it points His saying (Exalted is He): {«وَعَلَّمْنَاهُ مِنْ لَدُنَّا عِلْمًا»} (\"And We taught him knowledge from Our very presence\") (Q. 18:65) — even though all knowledge is from His presence, yet some of it comes through the intermediaries of created instruction, and that is not called \"knowledge from His presence\" (ladunnī). Rather, the ladunnī is that which opens up in the secret of the heart without any familiar cause from without. These, then, are the transmitted evidences — and were one to gather all that has come concerning this of verses, reports, and traditions, it would exceed all enumeration.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 594", "section_title_en": "The Unveiling of Knowledge Through Divine Inspiration and the Evidences of the Unseen", "section_index": 594, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning encompasses both perception of a thing's essence and judgment upon it—knowledge as direct apprehension or discursive ruling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence (transitive to one object) and judgment upon existence or non-existence (transitive to two objects).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage denotes certainty and awareness (\"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\"); metaphorical extension applies to marks, signs, and beacons of guidance (معالم الطريق) — here, the inspired knowledge is a \"mark\" of the path.\n- Root: كشف (kashafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To remove a covering, unveil, disclose what was hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Unveiling (kashf) in theological terms is the removal of the veil (ghishāwah) from the heart such that the realities of things become directly witnessed, not merely inferred.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to lift a covering from a thing; metaphorical: the disclosure of inward states and hidden matters — the heart's eye perceives what the outward senses cannot.\n- Root: فرس (farasa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To prey upon, devour; farāsah — the lion's pouncing upon its prey.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Discernment (farāsah) is a spiritual perception by which the believer penetrates into the inner states of others, a light cast by God into the heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the beast of prey seizing its quarry; metaphorical: the believer's insight seizing the truth of a matter or the hidden state of a person — \"اتقوا فراسة المؤمن فإنه ينظر بنور الله.\"\n- Root: حدث (ḥadatha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be new, originate after non-existence; muḥaddath — one who is spoken to, inspired with true speech from the unseen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination after non-existence; the muḥaddath is one to whom divine inspiration comes as a new, immediate occurrence without acquired means.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: newness of a thing; metaphorical: \"كان عمر رضي الله عنه محدثا أي صادق الحدس، كأنما حدث بما ظن\" — one whose intuition is so true it is as if he were directly spoken to.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification (الحصر والتقييد) — the particle \"إنما\" and conditional clauses (إن/إذا) restrict predicates to their subjects, as in \"إنما العالم الذي يأخذ علمه من ربه\" — the true scholar is exclusively the one who takes knowledge from his Lord.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "58": [{"arabic": "أمضى السبيل فقلت وما إمضاء السبيل فقيل لي كنت تقول للشيء إنه في سبيل الله ثم ترجع فيه فلو كنت أمضيت السبيل لأمضينا لك وحكي عن بعض العباد بمكة أنه كان نائما إلى جنب رجل معه هميانه فانتبه الرجل ففقد هميانه فاتهمه به فقال له كم كان في هميانك فذكر له فحمله من البيت ووزنه من عنده ثم بعد ذلك أعلمه أصحابه أنهم كانوا أخذوا الهميان مزحا معه فجاء هو وأصحابه معه وردوا الذهب فأبى وقال خذه حلالا طيبا فما كنت لأعود في مال أخرجته في سبيل الله عز وجل فلم يقبل فألحوا عليه فدعا ابنه وجعل يصره صر", "english": "And it is narrated that al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym's horse was stolen — its value being twenty thousand — while he was standing in prayer. He did not cut short his prayer, nor was he disturbed to seek it. Then a group came to offer him condolences. He said: \"As for me, I had indeed seen him while he was untying it.\" It was said: \"And what prevented you from rebuking him?\" He said: \"I was engaged in something more beloved to me than that\" — meaning the prayer. So they began supplicating against him [the thief]. He said: \"Do not do so. Say good instead, for I have made it a charity (ṣadaqah) for him.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1134", "section_title_en": "On the Etiquettes of Reliance (Adab al-Tawakkul) — Section 1134: Concerning One Whose Wealth is Stolen", "section_index": 1134, "anchors": "- Root: ظلم (Ẓ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to place something in other than its proper place\"; darkness (ẓulmah) as its physical correlate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ẓulm is \"transgression against right\" and is expressed as darkness (ẓulumāt) versus light (nūr) — ignorance, shirk, and fisq.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He wrongs and is wronged.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"A land ẓalīm\" — one never dug; the wrongdoer's darkness is like the darkness of the grave.\n- Root: سبل (S-B-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A clear, easy path (sabīl); its plural subul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"The way in which there is ease\"; when used absolutely, it designates the path of truth (ṭarīq al-ḥaqq).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"Take this path, for it is the most even of paths.\" Metaphorical: \"He let down (asbala) the rain\" — as if lowering a curtain; \"he let flow tears.\"\n- Root: سرق (S-R-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Taking what is not one's own covertly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Theft is the taking of what one has no right to take, in secret\"; legally specified by place and amount.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A thief between thefts.\" Metaphorical: \"He stole the hearing\" (listened secretly); \"a stolen voice\" (hoarse).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Specification with innamā) — Used to confine a predicate to a subject, as in the dream narrative: \"It is only for him who carried the path through (amḍā al-sabīl).\"", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "61": [{"arabic": "في النهار لا يقبله في الليل وأن لله حقا في الليل لا يقبله في النهار وأنه لا يقبل النافلة حتى تؤدى الفريضة وإنما ثقلت موازين من ثقلت موازينهم يوم القيامة باتباعهم الحق في الدنيا وثقله عليهم وحق لميزان لا يوضع فيه إلا الحق أن يثقل وإنما خفت موازين من خفت موزاينهم يوم القيامة باتباع الباطل وخفته عليهم وحق لميزان لا يوضع فيه إلاالباطل أن يخف وإن الله ذكر أهل الجنة بأحسن أعمالهم وتجاوز عن سيئاتهم فيقول القائل أنا دون هؤلا", "english": "And Salmān al-Fārisī (may Allah Most High be pleased with him) entered upon him to visit him and said: \"O Abu Bakr, give us your counsel.\" He said: \"Verily Allah will open the world upon you — so take not from it except your sufficiency. And know that whoever prays the dawn prayer is under the protection of Allah (dhimmah); so do not violate Allah's protection, lest He cast you into the Fire on your face.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1302", "section_title_en": "The Death of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah Most High be pleased with him)", "section_index": 1302, "anchors": "- Root: حشرج (ḥashraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The rattling, gurgling sound of the death-throe in the throat; the final breath struggling in the chest.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The terminal state of the soul's separation from the body, the moment of overwhelming divine decree.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical rattle of the dying; Metaphorical — the constriction of the breast as the soul is drawn forth.]\n- Root: سكر (sakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The veiling of the intellect; intoxication; the stupor that overcomes one at the onset of death.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The stupor of death (sakrat al-mawt) as the removal of all veils, wherein the truth of the Hereafter becomes undeniably present.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — intoxication from drink; Metaphorical — the overwhelming bewilderment of the soul at death's onset.]\n- Root: ذمم (dhimam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The covenant of protection; the bond of safe-conduct; that which one is obligated to honor and safeguard.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The divine guarantee (dhimmat Allah) extended to the believer who prays the dawn prayer — a sacred pact not to be violated.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — a pledge of security between men; Metaphorical — the inviolable covenant between the servant and his Lord.]\n- Root: وزن (wazana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: To weigh; the balance; the measure of equivalence between two things.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The weighing of deeds on the Day of Resurrection as the manifestation of divine justice — the scales (mawāzīn) receiving only truth or falsehood.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical act of weighing; Metaphorical — the spiritual weighing of deeds by their conformity to truth (ḥaqq) or falsehood (bāṭil).]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — [The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in Abu Bakr's dictum: \"It is only that the scales of those whose scales are heavy on the Day of Resurrection are weighed down by their following the truth in this world.\"]", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "65": [{"arabic": "دعائه يا ممسك السماء أن تقع على الأرض إلا بإذنك أمسك الدنيا عني وقال محمد بن المنكدر أرأيت لو أن رجلا صام الدهر لا يفطر وقام الليل لا ينام وتصدق بماله وجاهد في سبيل الله واجتنب محارم الله غير أنه يؤتى به يوم القيامة فيقال إن هذا عظم في عينه ما صغره الله وصغر في عينه ما عظمه الله كيف ترى يكون حاله فمن منا ليس هكذا الدنيا عظيمة عنده مع ما اقترفنا من الذنوب والخطايا وقال أبو حازم اشتدت مؤنة الدنيا والآخرة فأما مؤنة الآخرة فإنك لا تجد عليها أعوانا", "english": "He also said: \"When Allah desires good for a servant, He gives him a portion of the world, then withholds it. When it is exhausted, He returns it to him. And when a servant is contemptible to Him, He spreads the world out for him generously.\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 735", "section_title_en": "Section 735", "section_index": 735, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "70": [{"arabic": "وبيان ذلك في كتاب البيع والسلم والإجارة والحوالة والضمان والقراض والشركة والمساقاة والشفعة والصلح والخلع والكتابة والصداق وسائر المعاوضات الخامس ما يؤخذ عن رضا من غير عوض وهو حلال إذا روعي فيه شرط المعقود عليه وشرط العاقدين وشرط العقد ولم يؤد إلى ضرر بوارث أو غيره وذلك مذكور في كتاب الهبات والوصايا والصدقات السادس ما يحصل بغير اختيار كالميراث وهو حلال إذا كان الموروث قد اكتسب المال من بعض الجهات الخمس على", "english": "The fourth [category]: What is taken by mutual consent through an exchange (muʿāwaḍah). That is lawful when the condition of the two exchanged things is observed, and the condition of the two contracting parties, and the condition of the two utterances — namely, the offer (ījāb) and the acceptance (qabūl) — along with what the Sacred Law has commanded of avoiding corrupting conditions.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 339", "section_title_en": "The Book on the Divisions of Alms, Endowments, and Maintenance — The Five Categories of Acquisition", "section_index": 339, "anchors": "- Root: فرق (farraqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, division, distinguishing between things; al-furqān is that which distinguishes truth from falsehood.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-farq is the separation of one thing from another, and al-furqān is the criterion by which truth is distinguished from falsehood — hence the Qur'an is called al-Furqān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dividing and separating. Metaphorical: \"وقفته على مفارق الحديث\" — I made him stand upon the clear aspects of the matter.\n- Root: صدق (ṣadaqa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Truthfulness in speech and action; al-ṣadaqah is that which is given seeking reward from Allah, verifying one's faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ṣidq is primarily in speech, whether past or future, and by extension in action — a man is ṣādiq al-ḥamlah (truthful in attack) when his action matches his intent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: truthfulness in speech. Metaphorical: \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — a man whose attack is sincere and effective.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Standing still, halting; al-waqf is the endowment of property for charitable purposes, withholding it from circulation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Waqaftu al-qawm — I made them stand. From this is derived the endowment of a house (waqf al-dār): restraining it from being sold or inherited.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing. Metaphorical: \"وقفت الأرض على ولدي\" — I endowed the land upon my children, restraining it from transfer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The governing rule permits separation between the operative word and its object where clarity is maintained, as in the periodic structure of enumerating conditions before their governing verb.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "73": [{"arabic": "وأن كل من في السموات والأرض لن يخلقوا ذبابا ولو اجتمعوا له ولا فراشا ونشكره إذ رفع السماء لعباده سقفا مبنيا ومهد الأرض بساطا لهم وفراشا وكور الليل على النهار فجعل الليل لباسا والنهار معاشا لينتشروا في إبتغاء فضله وينتعشوا به عن ضراعة الحاجات انتعاشا ونصلي على رسوله الذي يصدر المؤمنون عن حوضه رواء بعد ورودهم عليه عطاشا وعلى آله وأصحابه الذين لم يدعوا في نصرة دينه تشمرا وانكماشا وسلم تسليما كثيرا أما بعد فإن رب الأرباب ومسبب الأسباب جعل الآخرة دار ال", "english": "In the name of God, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 311", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Earning and Livelihood — Introduction and Chapter Divisions", "section_index": 311, "anchors": "- Root: زوج (z-w-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pairing, coupling; each of the two members of a pair — whether male/female in animals, or two matched things like sandals; \"زوجة\" is a weak (radī'ah) dialectal form.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Every one of two paired things — in animals, the male and female; in other things, any two that are paired, similar or opposite. From this: the wife is called \"zawj\" for the husband, and he \"zawj\" for her.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): \"He is her zawj and she is his zawj or zawjah; the two are zawjān.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"The two discourses paired (tazāwajā) and were coupled (izdawajā).\"\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A small creature; \"Umm risālah\": the carrion kite. The root conveys sending forth with gentleness and ease.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The origin of \"risl\" is emission with leisurely ease — a she-camel that is \"raslah\" is easy-paced. From this, the messenger (rasūl) is one sent forth; and from the sense of gentleness comes \"on your leisure\" (alā rislik).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sending a message, dispatching messengers, releasing a stallion among camels, loosing a dog or falcon upon game. Metaphorical: \"God loosed (arsala) chastisement upon them\"; \"the arrows are the messengers (rusul) of deathly fates.\"\n- Root: داب (d-'-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To persist and toil; \"al-dābbatān\" (the two toiling ones) is said of night and day in their ceaseless alternation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Da'b\" is the continuance of journeying — \"and He subjected the sun and moon to you, both persisting (dā'ibayn)\" {«وَسَخَّرَ لَكُمُ الشَّمْسَ وَالْقَمَرَ دَائِبَيْنِ»}; and \"da'b\" is the habitual custom one persists upon — \"like the custom (ka-da'bi) of the house of Pharaoh.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to strive diligently in work, to toil in travel. Metaphorical: \"This is your da'b\" — i.e., your constant concern and occupation; \"night and day toil (yad'abān) in their mutual succession.\"\n- Root: الي ('-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-alak\" and \"al-ālūk\": the chewing and working of the bit in a horse's mouth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Khalīl said: \"al-ma'lakah\" (the message/angelic mission) is the conveying of a message, because it is \"worked in the mouth\" (tu'lak fī al-fam) — from their saying of a horse: \"it works (ya'lik) the bit,\" i.e., chews it. The angels are called \"malā'ikah\" because they convey messages.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating any extension beyond it. Applied here to the wife's mourning obligation: \"she must not mourn (lā taḥidd) over any dead person more than three days — except over a husband, four months and ten days,\" where the exception clause (istithnā') delimits the general prohibition.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "77": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذل", "english": "And in the ḥadīth of Abān, from Anas ibn Mālik — may Allah be pleased with him: \"Blessed (ṭūbā) is a servant who spends from wealth he earned without disobedience.\" And if what he puts forth is not from the finest of his wealth, then that is from ill-manners (sūʾ al-adab), since he may have kept the finest for himself, or for his servant, or for his family — and so he has preferred over Allah — Exalted is He — someone other than Him. Were he to do this with his guest and offer him the worst food in his house, that would kindle rage in his breast. This is if his regard is toward Allah — Exalted is He. And if his regard is toward himself and his reward in the Hereafter, then he is not intelligent who prefers others over himself — for he has nothing of his wealth save what he gave in charity (so it remains for him) or what he ate (so it is consumed). And what he eats is merely the satisfaction of a need in the present moment. It is not from intelligence to confine one's regard to the immediate (al-ʿājilah) and to abandon laying up provision (al-iddikhār). Allah — Exalted is He — has said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه»} (\"O you who believe, spend from the good things you have earned and from what We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend — while you would not take it yourselves save with closed eyes\") — that is, you would not take it save with aversion and shame, and that is the meaning of closing the eyes (al-ighmāḍ). So do not prefer over your Lord that which you yourselves would disdain.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "78": [{"arabic": "حرام قطعا لا يلزمه ترك الشراء والأكل فإن ذلك حرج وما في الدين من حرج ويعلم هذا بأنه لما سرق في زمان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مجن (1)", "english": "If you say: \"But every number is restricted (maḥṣūr) in God's knowledge — so what is the limit of the restricted? And if a man wished to enumerate the people of a town, he could do so as well, if he were able\" — then know that defining the likes of these matters is not possible; rather, they are only regulated by approximation (taqrīb). So we say: every number which, if all its individuals were gathered upon a single plain, would be difficult for the observer to count by mere glance — like one thousand or two thousand — is unrestricted (ghayr maḥṣūr). And whatever is easy — like ten or twenty — is restricted (maḥṣūr). And between the two extremes there are intermediate cases (awsāṭ) that resemble one another, attaching to one of the two extremes by conjecture (al-ẓann). And whatever falls into doubt, one should seek a ruling from the heart concerning it, for sin is the pricking of the hearts (ḥazāz al-qulūb). And in a station like this, the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said to Wābiṣah: \"Seek a ruling from your heart, even if they give you a ruling — and they give you a ruling — and they give you a ruling.\" {«استفت قلبك وإن أفتوك وأفتوك وأفتوك»}", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 349", "section_title_en": "The Second Division: The Forbidden Mixed with the Unrestricted Permissible", "section_index": 349, "anchors": "- Root: حصر (ḥaṣara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To confine, restrict, restrain, or hem in; al-ḥaṣūr is one who abstains; the root carries the sense of encircling and binding within limits.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحصر: التضييق\" — restriction is a form of constriction or narrowing; in the Qur'anic context {«واحصروهم»} it means to tighten around someone, and {«حصيرا»} denotes that which confines or restrains.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"حصرتهم حصرا: حبستهم\" — to detain or imprison. The term \"maḥṣūr\" (restricted/limited) is applied to what is countable and bounded, while \"ghayr maḥṣūr\" (unrestricted) applies to what exceeds the capacity of enumeration.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — the syntactic structure of \"إنما\" confines the predicate to the subject, and here the legal reasoning confines the ruling to the conjunction of two effective causes (ʿillah): preponderance (ghalabah) and necessity (ḥājah).\n\n- Root: بلد (balad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"البلَدُ: كل موضع مستحيز من الأرض عامر أو غير عامر\" — any circumscribed tract of land, inhabited or uninhabited; the plural is bilād.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البلد: المكان المحيط المحدود المتأثر باجتماع قطانه وإقامتهم فيه\" — a place that is encompassed, bounded, and affected by the gathering and residence of its inhabitants.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a town or land; Metaphorical (majāz): \"هي بلدة بيني وبينك\" — a severance or separation, indicating distance and disconnection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: The syntax of exception and exclusion (al-istithnāʾ) — the phrase \"إلا إذا وقع بين جماعة محصورين\" restricts the general ruling through a conditional exception, marking the sole case where the prohibition applies.\n\n- Root: رسل (rasala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To send forth gently and deliberately; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched; the root conveys measured, orderly emission.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the origin is a calm, measured sending forth; from it derives the sense of proceeding with composure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" — God sent messengers to the nations; Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" — God loosed chastisement upon them, and \"القيح سوء الذكر رسيله\" — foul repute is the companion of sin.\n- Sibawayh Rule: The construction of legal hypotheticals (al-sharṭ wa-al-jawāb) — \"فإن قلت... فاعلم أن\" structures an objection-response dialectic, a hallmark of juristic reasoning (uṣūl al-fiqh).\n\n- Root: فتي (fatā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A young man in his prime; by extension, a servant or slave; the root carries the sense of fresh vigor and capacity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفتى الطري من الشباب\" — the fresh youth; used metaphorically for the servant, as in {«تراود فتاها عن نفسه»}.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هذا فتي بين الفتوة وهي الحرية والكرم\" — one who is noble and generous; Metaphorical: \"لا أفعل ذلك ماكر الفتيان\" — an oath formula meaning \"as long as the two new moons (the days and nights) alternate,\" i.e., forever.\n- Sibawayh Rule: The derivation of verbal nouns from roots (al-maṣdar) — the term \"فتوى\" (legal opinion) derives from this root, signifying the authoritative pronouncement of one qualified to clarify what is obscure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Hadith"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 550, 3625], [2, 3625, 5020], [3, 5020, 6615], [4, 6615, 7517], [5, 7517, 8162], [6, 8162, 9073], [7, 9073, 10596], [8, 10596, 11279], [9, 11279, 12400]], "22:1": [[1, 550, 3625], [2, 3625, 5020], [3, 5020, 6615], [4, 6615, 7517], [5, 7517, 8162], [6, 8162, 9073], [7, 9073, 10596], [8, 10596, 11279], [9, 11279, 12400]], "2": [[1, 310, 1481], [2, 1481, 3401], [3, 3401, 4338], [4, 4338, 4982], [5, 4982, 6223], [6, 6223, 8927], [7, 8927, 10262], [8, 10262, 11643], [9, 11643, 12287], [10, 12287, 13411], [11, 13411, 14137], [12, 14137, 15706], [13, 15706, 17111], [14, 17111, 18832], [15, 18832, 20419], [16, 20419, 21847], [17, 21847, 22292], [18, 22292, 24114], [19, 24114, 25847], [20, 25847, 27264], [21, 27264, 28201], [22, 28201, 29510]], "22:2": [[1, 310, 1481], [2, 1481, 3401], [3, 3401, 4338], [4, 4338, 4982], [5, 4982, 6223], [6, 6223, 8927], [7, 8927, 10262], [8, 10262, 11643], [9, 11643, 12287], [10, 12287, 13411], [11, 13411, 14137], [12, 14137, 15706], [13, 15706, 17111], [14, 17111, 18832], [15, 18832, 20419], [16, 20419, 21847], [17, 21847, 22292], [18, 22292, 24114], [